.TH GEARBENCH\-CTL 1 "2026-11-11" "gearbench\-ctl 1.3.1" "User Commands"
.\" Command, option, key and file names must not break at a hyphen, and a
.\" justified line spreads them apart. groff's man macros set the adjustment
.\" again from the AD string at each tagged paragraph, so .ad l alone lasts
.\" only to the first .TP.
.nh
.ds AD l
.ad l
.SH NAME
gearbench\-ctl \- lighting, G keys, DPI and macros for Logitech G keyboards and mice, and headset settings
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B gearbench\-ctl
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.I command
.RI [ arguments ]
.br
.B gearbench\-ctl \-\-version
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B gearbench\-ctl
is Gearbench's command line: lighting, G keys, DPI and macros for Logitech G
keyboards and mice, and headset settings, several keyboards and several mice at
once. It binds the G keys of a keyboard that has them, per M
profile and on a G-Shift layer, the G1 to G3 of a G933, G935, G633 or G635
headset, and the extra buttons of each mouse but the G305.
It sets the lighting and the mice's DPI and onboard profile.
.PP
On headsets it sets the Astro A50 Gen 4's microphone, mix, default balance,
alert volume, auto shut-off and equalizer; the
Astro A50 X's settings, equalizers and stream mix; and the sidetone, lights
and onboard equalizer of the G432 and the other headsets of its family that
have them, and the G933's auto shut-off. It reads the settings and equalizers of the A50 Gen 5,
PRO X 2, G522 and G325, and refuses their writes until each is tested on that
hardware. It runs:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
Gearbench Spatial, a 7.1 output rendered for headphones on a headset;
.IP \(bu 2
Gearbench Game, Chat and Media, three outputs for games, voice chat and music
on that headset;
.IP \(bu 2
a noise-suppressed microphone input for each headset whose noise suppression
is not in the headset;
.IP \(bu 2
an equalizer output for each of the G432's family with no equalizer of its
own.
.RE
.PP
Each device but the A50 Gen 4 is asked what it can do. A command for
something it lacks is refused, and the window leaves its section out.
.PP
It needs no root: udev
.B uaccess
grants the devices, the
.B gearbench
group grants the G502 X PLUS's event nodes, and the daemon,
.B gearbench\-ctl
and the window all run as you. It needs no online account and sends nothing
over the network.
.PP
A long-running daemon owns the devices; a second process writing lighting
would fight it. It holds the keyboard's software lighting claim (HID++
feature 0x8071, or 0x8070 on the G213 Prodigy) and reads the diverted G, M
and MR keys. It types bindings and macros through a uinput virtual keyboard
and mouse, and sets a device up again when it is plugged back in or switched
on. Every subcommand is a request to that daemon over a unix socket, except
these, which need no daemon:
.BR probe ,
.BR report ,
.BR keys ,
.BR config ,
.BR gui ,
.BR daemon ,
.BR headset\-spatial ,
.BR headset\-outputs ,
.BR headset\-volume ,
.BR g432\-eq ,
.B headset\-noise
and
.BR "headset\-import \-\-library" ;
.B headset\-library
unless it reads or writes a slot; and
.B import\-ghub
and
.B import\-pack
when they only list or run with
.BR \-\-dry\-run .
Even so,
.BR probe ,
.B report
and
.B config
ask a running daemon for its state, and
.B g432\-eq
and
.B headset\-noise
without
.B \-\-headset
ask it which headset of the G432's family it drives.
.PP
The window's controls are requests to the daemon too, except these, which the
window runs itself:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
spatial sound, and the outputs Gearbench Game, Chat and Media;
.IP \(bu 2
the volumes;
.IP \(bu 2
the noise suppression this computer runs for a headset, and its microphone test;
.IP \(bu 2
the equalizer of a model without one of its own;
.IP \(bu 2
the saves, renames and deletes of the presets kept on this computer;
.IP \(bu 2
starting the daemon and turning the focus extension on.
.RE
.PP
.B DEVICES
names the models tested on the hardware and says what each other keyboard
and mouse model gets, what it was built from and what is not yet tested on
it; the headsets are under
.B Headset
in
.BR COMMANDS .
On this page, a mouse not yet tested means any mouse
.B DEVICES
describes but the G502 HERO: Gearbench does not write their onboard profile
yet.
.PP
Gearbench never writes a keyboard's onboard profile.
Keyboard lighting is volatile only, except the brightness: the wired G515 TKL
and the G915 X LIGHTSPEED keep it through a power-off, the G515 LIGHTSPEED TKL
is taken to, and the PRO X TKL LIGHTSPEED, the G915 X, G915 X LIGHTSPEED TKL,
G316 X, G316 X 98, G512 X 75 and G512 X 98 may (see
.BR brightness ).
The five commands under
.B Mouse onboard memory
below write the mouse's onboard profile 1, after a backup, except where that
section says one writes nothing there, as when
.B mouse\-rate
or
.B mouse\-dpi\-stages
saves a game profile's own rate or stages. An A50 Gen 4 change goes to its base
station's memory, and only
.B headset\-save
writes its flash.
.B g432\-sidetone
writes the sidetone level of the headset of the G432's family the daemon
drives, untested models included. The G432's adapter kept that level through
an unplug when tested on 2026-10-17, and the others may keep it too.
.BR g432\-auto\-off ,
.B g432\-lights \-\-startup
and
.B \-\-store
write what the headset keeps, and are refused on a model not yet tested on
its hardware: every one of the G432's family but the G432 and the G933. The
Astro A50 X keeps each setting written to it through a power cycle. Nothing
is written to an A50 Gen 5, PRO X 2, G522 or G325 until each write is tested
on that hardware.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-json
Print JSON instead of the formatted output: the daemon's raw reply, with the
library added for \fBheadset\-eq \-\-save\-as\fR; what
\fBprobe\fR, \fBreport\fR, \fBkeys\fR, \fBconfig\fR, \fBimport\-ghub\fR, \fBimport\-pack\fR,
\fBheadset\-spatial\fR, \fBheadset\-outputs\fR, \fBheadset\-volume\fR, \fBg432\-eq\fR,
\fBheadset\-noise\fR,
\fBheadset\-import \-\-library\fR, \fBheadset\-library\fR (but for its
\fB\-\-write\fR, which prints the daemon's reply), \fBaudio\-presets\fR and
\fBg432\-headset\-presets\fR (but for \fB\-\-pick\fR, which prints the daemon's
reply) read themselves; with
\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR, the requests an import would send; and for an import, a
list of the daemon's replies in turn, with a refusal in the place of a
profile that was not sent.
\fBgui\fR and \fBdaemon\fR ignore it. It goes on either side of the
subcommand, as in
.BR "gearbench\-ctl status \-\-json" .
The formatted output prints each character in a name that a terminal would
act on or not show as its escape, such as
.B \ex1b
for ESC or
.B \eu202e
for a right-to-left override; the JSON escapes them itself. In a name from a
G HUB file, some of those are dropped or replaced first (see
\fBimport\-ghub\fR).
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Print usage and exit. Every subcommand takes it too.
.SH COMMANDS
The groups are the daemon and its state, the keyboard, game profiles and the
two ways to import them, the mouse and its onboard memory, and the headset.
.SS Daemon and state
.TP
\fBdaemon\fR [\fB\-v\fR | \fB\-\-verbose\fR]
Run the daemon in the foreground.
.B \-\-verbose
adds the DEBUG lines, among them each press of a G key, a headset's
included, with what it is bound to, each press and release of
the mouse's G4 to G9, and
every request but
.BR status ,
.BR mouse ,
.BR headset ,
.B g432
and
.BR a50x ;
a logged request shows a macro's steps only by their type, such as text,
key or delay, never their values. Normally the
.BR systemd.service (5)
user unit runs this; see
.B FILES
below. At a login screen, in a session logind gives the class
.BR greeter ,
it logs one line and exits 0 before it opens a device. It reads the class
through python3\-gi; without that it runs at a login screen too.
Where Gearbench 1.1.0 to 1.2.1 switched spatial sound on, whose
.I gearbench\-spatial.service
was then wanted by
.BR default.target ,
it runs
.B systemctl \-\-user reenable gearbench\-spatial.service
as it starts, which has the unit wanted by
.B pipewire.service
in its place, and logs one line.
It watches
.I /dev
through
.BR inotify (7)
and sets up a keyboard, or the cable of a mouse or headset it drives through
its receiver, once udev has opened its hidraw node to this account, and looks
again every 30 seconds besides. Where the watch cannot start, as past the
account's
.IR fs.inotify.max_user_instances ,
it logs
.B cannot watch /dev for devices plugged in
and looks for those every 2 seconds.
A mouse or headset not yet set up is looked for on a timer of its own, with
the watch or without it: a mouse every 10 seconds, doubling up to 120 while
one found is not set up, and a headset every 5 seconds, doubling up to 60
while one found does not answer.
A mouse whose setup failed is also looked for as soon as the watch sees a
node come, as at the replug its error asks for.
Run as root, it logs one line and exits 1 before it opens a device: run it
as yourself.
Closing its terminal stops it as SIGTERM does, unless it was started under
.BR nohup (1).
It exits 1 without starting while another Gearbench daemon runs, the
service's included:
.BR "another Gearbench daemon is already running: it holds" ,
then the lock's path (see
.I $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/gearbench.lock
under FILES)
and how to stop the other daemon.
.TP
.B status
Show what the daemon and the devices are doing, in labeled lines, with any
further line indented under the one it belongs to:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
the keyboard, in the form of each command's
.B device
line: its node, its name and whether it is on its cable or through its
receiver; for a model not yet tested on the hardware, the note that asks for
a device report from it (see
.BR report );
whether it is answering; the problems (below); its battery, and under it
whether a low-battery notice was posted; and its firmware version, where its
connect read one;
.IP \(bu 2
the M profile; the game profile loaded and the latest held off; and what
game profiles follow: the focused program and the game profile that names
it, or, where focus is not followed, the running programs;
.IP \(bu 2
the brightness, or none on an attached keyboard with no brightness
Gearbench can set, such as the G213 Prodigy;
.IP \(bu 2
the screen lock, whether the lights go off with it and whether they are on,
dimmed or off;
.IP \(bu 2
the idle dimming's times; with
.BR "\-\-never\-dim\-with\-game\-profile on" ,
that the lights do not dim while a game profile is loaded, or, with
.BR "\-\-never\-dim on" ,
that it never dims; and whether the keyboard is dimmed or with its lights off
for idleness now, or that the G213 Prodigy has no idle times;
.IP \(bu 2
whether the tray icon shows, and why not while it is on;
.IP \(bu 2
whether the lighting is claimed and, while it is, on a keyboard whose G keys
HID++ feature 0x8010 diverts, whether they are diverted (on a G915 TKL or
PRO X TKL LIGHTSPEED, that Gearbench types its F1 to F12 while they are
unbound);
.IP \(bu 2
on a keyboard with custom assignments, whether they are on, the shortcut
that turns them the other way, and the keys set to report to the daemon and,
while they are off, what those do, or why none is yet;
.IP \(bu 2
whether Game Mode is on, or that it is not known until the keyboard's Game
Mode button is pressed;
.IP \(bu 2
the effects the attached keyboard has, which
.B effect
takes;
.IP \(bu 2
why the keyboard does not show the last apply of the lighting or the last
.BR paint ,
if it does not;
.IP \(bu 2
whether the virtual keyboard and mouse are ready;
.IP \(bu 2
each mouse attached, in the keyboard's form. Under it: the same note for a
model not yet tested; the note on a mouse whose lighting is not yet tested on
this hardware; whether a G502 HERO, a G903 LIGHTSPEED HERO or another mouse
that waits to claim its lighting shows its own lighting or the lighting
Gearbench sends it; and whether it is not answering. Then its battery,
where it lists one, and under it whether a low-battery notice was posted,
and its firmware version, where it gave one as it attached;
.IP \(bu 2
on
.BR "mouse follows" ,
whether a mouse with lighting follows the keyboard that leads, or
.BR "off, its own color" ;
.IP \(bu 2
each headset attached, in the same form, whether it is not answering and
its battery where it lists one, and under it whether a low-battery notice was
posted;
.IP \(bu 2
the macro running or counting down to its start, and the daemon's process
id.
.RE
.IP
Under
.BR problems ,
between the keyboard's line, with the lines under it, and its battery,
comes a line for each thing that is wrong, or
.B none
when nothing is:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
a keyboard: no keyboard node; a node the udev rule has not granted; a node
with no keyboard answering on it; a keyboard not answering; a G915 TKL or
PRO X TKL LIGHTSPEED left on its onboard profile while the daemon has no
virtual keyboard or the session is not active, or left in host mode where
its hand-back as the session went inactive did not finish;
.IP \(bu 2
a mouse: a mouse node not granted, or a mouse found that could not be set
up; a mouse running an onboard profile other than 1, with why the switch
back did not take or when it is tried next; a mouse on which host mode did
not take; a mouse left in host mode because its switch back to onboard mode
did not take; a mouse out of host mode whose event node the daemon cannot
read without the
\fBgearbench\fR group; a mouse whose event node has carried a keyboard's
keystrokes in host mode;
.IP \(bu 2
a headset: an A50 base station node, a LIGHTSPEED headset's receiver node or
the G522's cable node not granted (the cable's also while the headset works
through its receiver); an A50 Gen 4 base station not answering; a PRO X 2,
G522 or G325 LIGHTSPEED headset switched off or out of range behind its
receiver; a base station, receiver or cable found that could not be set up;
a node of the G432 or another headset of its family not granted, or one
found that could not be set up; a headset of the G432's family plugged in
beside the one driven, which is not driven, with the one to unplug to use
it;
.IP \(bu 2
anything else: another program that drives these devices running, each by
its name and process id, with what to do; a
\fIconfig.json\fR or loaded game profile file that could not be read or was
edited by hand; a macro file edited by hand; no virtual keyboard or mouse;
and, while
.B lock\-lights
is on, a desktop without what it needs: python3\-gi, logind, or GNOME's idle
monitor.
.RE
.IP
With more than one keyboard driven, the report gives the keyboard that leads
(see
.BR sync\-keyboard )
first. Under it is a line for each other keyboard, with whether it shows the
leader's lighting or its own, and whether it is not answering.
.IP
With
.B \-\-json
it prints every field of the reply. Its
.B firmware
is the version of the firmware the keyboard runs, such as
.BR "MPK 26.00.B0012" ,
or null where the keyboard's connect read none. It comes from the HID++
feature 0x0003 reads that tell one keyboard from another at its connect, so
nothing is sent to the keyboard for it. Its
.B keyboards_attached
lists each keyboard model plugged in. An entry's
.B switched_off
is true for one not connected whose node the daemon waits on, since nothing
answered there when it was asked, as with a keyboard switched off behind its
receiver. Nothing is asked of a keyboard for it. Its
.B headsets
names each headset attached, and
.B headsets_silent
each of those that does not answer: a base station not answering, or a
headset switched off or out of range behind its receiver.
.IP
.B keyboards_driven
lists each keyboard driven by
.BR model ,
.BR name ,
.BR product ,
.B answering
and
.BR follows .
The reply's own
.B follows
and
.B follow_leader
say whether the keyboard the reply's own fields are for follows the leader and where its
follow switch is (see
.BR keyboard\-follow ).
.B mice_attached
and
.B headsets_attached
list each mouse and headset attached by
.BR name ,
.B device
(its node, null for a headset away whose receiver is let go),
.BR route ,
.BR battery_found ,
.BR battery ,
.BR battery_at ,
.B battery_warned
(the lowest level a low-battery notice was posted below, null
for none) and
.BR answering .
Each mouse also has
.BR firmware ,
as
.B mouse
gives it, and the Astro A50 Gen 4 also has
.BR linked ,
whether its headset is on its link.
.IP
Each entry of
.B problems
about a keyboard also has
.BR keyboard ,
the model it is about, as
.B keyboards_driven
gives a model. For a node found, it is the model on that node, another
keyboard's while the leader is unplugged; for no keyboard node, it is null
before the daemon has tried to set a keyboard up. The leader's entries come
first. A request's
.B keyboard
names, by a model's name or id, the keyboard whose own fields the reply
gives, such as
.BR name ,
.B battery
and its lighting, as the window's Keyboard tab asks for the keyboard it
shows;
.B problems
lists every keyboard's entries whatever it names. With no
.BR keyboard ,
one that names no keyboard model Gearbench drives, or a model not set up
since the daemon started, those fields are the leader's.
.TP
.B config
Print the configuration on disk, each M profile's lighting mode and painted
key count included, and say when a running daemon is on another M profile
than the file names, or runs on its defaults because it could not read the
file when it started. When it cannot get the daemon's state, as with none
running or one not answering in time, it says so. An entry kept under another of a model's ids, as written
by hand, is shown as the model's name with that id in parentheses, and with
.B "not read"
after the id where the daemon reads the entry under an earlier id of the
model in its place.
Needs no daemon.
.TP
.B save
Write the daemon's live configuration to
.IR config.json ,
and a loaded game profile to its own file, even over a hand edit since the
daemon read them; nothing else writes over one.
.I config.json
is written first: when the game profile's file is not saved, as on a full
disk or when the copy of its older version cannot be made (see
.I ~/.config/gearbench/backup/
under FILES),
the reply says
.I config.json
was saved, and why the game profile was not. A
.I config.json
the daemon could not read at its start stops the whole save, the loaded
game profile's file included, and the reply says why: fix
.IR config.json ,
then
.BR reload .
A
.I config.json
the last reload could not open, as for
.IR "Permission denied" ,
is not written over either: the save reads it first for its backup and
fails the same way until the file can be read again. Fix it, then
.B reload
it, or
.B save
over it.
With a folder at either file's name, a link to one, a link that leads into
a folder not there, or a folder this account may not write, such as a
read-only mount, the save has nowhere to write: fix that, then
.BR reload .
No macro is written: a macro file edited by hand is saved over only after a
.BR reload .
.TP
.B reload
Re-read
.IR config.json ,
the loaded game profile's file and the macro files, picking up hand edits,
then re-apply the lighting and the M and MR LEDs.
A
.I config.json
that cannot be read changes nothing, and the reply and
.B status
give its error until the file changes again; when the daemon could not read
it at its start either,
.B status
gives the last refused reload's error in place of the start's, even after the
file changes again, until a reload succeeds. What is loaded
follows
.IR config.json ,
as at a start.
A game profile loaded by hand stays if
.I config.json
still names it.
If
.I config.json
names another, that one loads; if that one's file cannot be read,
nothing loads and
.I config.json
keeps the name.
While every game profile file loads, a name that no game profile has and no
game profile file is named after is taken out of
.IR config.json .
A game profile loaded by hand that
.I config.json
no longer names is unloaded. A game profile a program
loaded stays, and when focus moves away from that program, or, where focus is
not followed, when it exits, the one
.I config.json
names comes back. A loaded game profile whose file cannot be read
keeps what it held, and the reply and
.B status
give the file's error until a reload reads it or, unless the profiles folder
could not be listed, the file could not be opened, its folder cannot be
written or a folder (or a link to one) is at its name,
.B save
writes over it; one whose file is gone is unloaded and not loaded again at the
next start. A
.BR lock\-lights ,
.BR mouse\-sync ,
.BR headset\-sync ,
.BR mouse\-host ,
.BR sync\-keyboard ,
.BR keyboard\-follow ,
.BR dpi\-notices ,
.B tray
or
.B idle
setting changed in
.I config.json
takes effect as that command would: a new
.B sync_keyboard
makes the keyboard it names lead, and every keyboard connected is painted
again. A new
.B mouse_selected
selects that mouse as at a start: while it is away, the first attached mouse
takes its place until it is back. Otherwise the
mouse's onboard profile and the lighting claim are left alone; in host
mode a loaded game profile's own DPI stages and report rate follow its file,
the mouse staying on the stage it is on, unless it has not taken them since
the profile loaded (see
.BR mouse\-dpi\-stages ).
On a mouse not yet tested, while the daemon runs it in host mode, a
.B mouse_rate
changed in
.I config.json
is sent live unless the loaded game profile has a rate of its own for that
mouse.
.TP
\fBprobe\fR [\fB\-\-force\fR] [\fB\-\-keyboard\fR \fImodel\fR]
Read-only dump of the keyboard: the node, its name, product id
and device index; under them, for a model not yet tested on the hardware, the note that
asks for a device report from it (see
.BR report );
the capabilities its feature table lists; its onboard mode; and, if it has
lighting, the clusters, per-cluster effect indices and ids, the
brightness and the brightness levels the keyboard lists, and the
software-control state.
That state is two bytes, control and notify: 0x03 and 0x05 while the daemon
holds the lighting claim, 0x03 and 0x03 while it holds the claim and the
keyboard is idle, 0x00 and 0x06 after the daemon's release, and 0x00 and 0x00
when nothing has held it since the keyboard linked.
On the G515 generation it also gives the whole board, cluster 0xff, with
those of pulsar\-point, wave and ripple it lists that a counted cluster
lacks, and on the G512 X remind\-me\-of, neon and outer\-space at
pulsar\-point's index and id.
A line for something the keyboard does not have is left out. With more than one
keyboard plugged in it reads the first that answers, in the order of the
models Gearbench knows, the G915 first, so a receiver whose keyboard is off,
or a node it cannot open, gives way to the next keyboard;
.B \-\-keyboard
reads the one named instead, by its model's name or id, as
.B "G515 TKL"
or
.BR c358 .
A name that is no model's, or a model not plugged in, is an error, exit
status 1. Needs no daemon,
and refuses to run while one is up, or may be, since both would see each other's HID++
replies; the refusal prints the
.B systemctl \-\-user stop
command. Run with
.BR sudo ,
.B run0
or
.BR pkexec ,
it looks for the daemon of the user who ran it, unless
.B XDG_RUNTIME_DIR
is set to an absolute path other than root's. Run as root with neither
.B SUDO_UID
nor
.B PKEXEC_UID
set to a user other than root, as after
.B su \-
or
.BR doas ,
or under sudo from a root shell, it cannot ask the user's daemon and refuses,
exit status 1.
.B \-\-force
probes anyway, accepting a possible misread. A lighting reply too short to
read is an error, exit status 1.
.TP
.B report
Read every Logitech device on USB, writing nothing, to paste into an issue.
The report sends only the reads on one list, and stops before any other
request, with nothing sent. Two published sources call each of them a read,
except 0x1B10's two, which only G HUB was seen to send.
.IP
Nothing in it names the unit or its owner. No serial number, friendly name or
host name is asked; getDeviceInfo's unit id is zeroed as it is read; and of
0x8100's memory only the factory sectors, 0x0100 and up, are read, never a
user sector, which can hold a macro's text. It prints a block to paste into
an issue, opened by a line of three backticks and the word text and closed by
a line of three backticks;
.B \-\-json
prints the whole report, format 1, with the factory sectors and each
request and its reply in hex. On standard error it gives the form to paste
the block into, where it printed one, and names another program running that
talks to these devices too. It also says what to do for a node the daemon
holds or may open, and for one this account may not open.
.IP
For each hidraw node of vendor 046d whose report descriptor can be read
it gives, from sysfs, the product id, the USB interface, the driver bound to
it and the report descriptor.
On a node whose descriptor declares HID++ reports it adds the device index,
the HID++ version, the feature table with each feature's flags and version
(a FeatureSet v0 gives no version), getDeviceInfo with the fields of the
version its root lookup gives 0x0003, each entity's firmware, and the
device's name and type.
Of the features Gearbench knows, it reads those the feature table lists, or,
where the table could not be read, those the root lookup finds:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
0x8071's clusters and effects, with 0x8040's brightness and its levels;
.IP \(bu 2
the zones 0x8081's zone bitmap gives an LED, and 0x8070's zones;
.IP \(bu 2
0x8100's layout, mode and running profile;
.IP \(bu 2
the battery;
.IP \(bu 2
0x2201's DPI list, DPI and default DPI, and 0x8060's rates and the one in
use;
.IP \(bu 2
0x2202's flags, DPI list, DPI, Y DPI and lift-off distance;
.IP \(bu 2
0x8061's rates, every one it lists, and the one in use, a keyboard's
included;
.IP \(bu 2
0x1B0C's capabilities and the left and right buttons' tuning, as raw bytes;
.IP \(bu 2
0x1B10's control list, its count and the control id at each key id, on a
keyboard whose custom assignments need its key ids, the G515 generation;
.IP \(bu 2
0x8300's sidetone, and 0x8310's equalizer with its saved and active gains.
.RE
.IP
A page of the zone bitmap, a factory sector or a set of gains the device
refuses is given as its refusal.
Last, it sends two getFeatureID reads back to back without waiting for a
reply, five times, and gives each answer with how many of the five gave it:
the replies in order, or which request's software id each reply that came
within a second carried.
.IP
A node of a model this build drives that the report read ends with the
verdict on its model record, worked out from these reads with nothing more
sent: a
.B confirmed:
line for each of the record's assumptions the reads bear out and a
.B differs:
line for each they do not.
A node whose reads bear on none of those assumptions, as a mouse whose
feature table could not be read, gets none.
It holds against the feature table each feature a field of the record has
the daemon use:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
a keyboard's 0x8070 for zone lighting, else 0x8071 and 0x8081; 0x8040 where
its brightness is read at each setup; 0x8100 where its G keys type their own
keys in onboard mode; 0x1B05 and 0x8101 for custom assignments; 0x1B10 where
the key ids were not measured; 0x8051 for FN's reports; and the feature the
daemon finds it by where none of those is it;
.IP \(bu 2
a mouse's 0x8070 for zone lighting, 0x8100 and 0x8110 for host mode, and
0x0020 where its record trusts the cookie that says its setup was kept;
.IP \(bu 2
a headset's 0x8310 where it has an equalizer of its own, and 0x8010 where
it has G keys.
.RE
.IP
It holds each zone a keyboard's drawn board lights against 0x8081's zone
bitmap, or each lighting area's zone against 0x8070's zones. It holds the
battery feature read against the record's cell. One that gives millivolts
needs a cell, and a reading given a percentage, unless fully charged, lies
within the curve it was read on: the charging one while charging, else the
discharge one. One that gives its own charge needs none, and a record with a
cell for it differs, as it does where no battery feature is found.
In
.B \-\-json
it is each node's
.BR verdict .
.IP
Then, from one
.BR pw\-dump ,
which only reads, it gives PipeWire's card for each audio device of vendor
046d or 9886, Astro's, as the G432's adapter and the A50's base station: its
ids, its product name and profile set, its profiles' names, the active one,
and each output route's index, name, device, channel volumes and volume base.
No name PipeWire builds from the USB serial is taken, the card's and its
nodes' among them, nor a description a WirePlumber rule gave the card. No
.B pw\-dump
installed, or no PipeWire to answer it, is said on the report's PipeWire
line and is no error, unless no Logitech node was found either: the error then
gives the cause.
.IP
A node the daemon holds, and every other node on its USB device, is sent
nothing. The report asks the daemon, half a second each, for
.BR status ,
then
.B status
again by model for each keyboard driven beside the leader's, then
.BR mouse ,
then
.B mouse
again by product id for each mouse beside the selected one, then
.BR g432 .
It gives what the daemon says of each keyboard, mouse and headset of the
G432's family whose node it holds. It names the other nodes with a HID++
collection on such a node's USB device as sent nothing, and says to stop the
daemon for the full read. Of a mouse the daemon does not answer for by its id
it gives only the name, the product and whether it is answering.
.IP
While the daemon runs, or may, a model it drives is not read either, held or
not: its attach retries open and probe such a node it does not hold, and two
programs on one node take each other's replies. Run as root with neither
.B SUDO_UID
nor
.B PKEXEC_UID
set to a user other than root, it cannot ask the user's daemon and counts it
as one that may run, and in place of the stop it says to run the report as
yourself, or with sudo, run0 or pkexec from your own account.
Where the daemon's socket could not be used, which no stop changes, it gives
the cause and says to fix it and run the report again.
A receiver whose paired
devices have nodes of their own is sent nothing, also beside a device the
daemon holds, and each device is reported on its own node. A receiver the
daemon does not hold adds the stop advice only while the daemon runs or may
and the receiver has the id of a model this build drives, as the G915's
receiver (046d:c541) and the G915 TKL's (046d:c545) do. A node unplugged, or
taken by another device, after the report listed it is sent nothing from then
on, and the report says so.
.IP
Where no device answers behind a receiver, on a device's own node or on the
receiver's node of the G915 or the G915 TKL, the node's line says the device
may be asleep or switched off. Standard error then says to move it or press
one of its keys, or switch it on, then run the report again.
.IP
For a node this account may not open it prints
.BR "sudo setfacl \-m u:$USER:rw /dev/hidrawN" .
That command grants that one node until the device is unplugged.
On a node that also carries key reports, the report says first that while the
grant lasts any program of the user's can read what is typed on it.
.IP
It finds the daemon under sudo, run0 or pkexec as
.B probe
does, and runs
.B pw\-dump
as the user who ran them, on that user's PipeWire, with only
.B PATH
and
.B XDG_RUNTIME_DIR
of root's environment and none of its terminal. No Logitech node on USB and
no PipeWire card of vendor 046d or 9886 is an error, exit status 1.
.IP
For each model Gearbench drives but has not yet tested, a note says
.B "not yet tested on a"
or
.BR an ,
the model, and to run
.B report
and paste its block into the Device report form:
.IP
.I \%https://github.com/gearbench/gearbench/issues/new?template=device\-report.yml
.IP
The note is in
.B status
for the keyboard that leads or a mouse, in
.BR mouse ,
.BR probe ,
.B g432\-sidetone
and
.BR audio ,
and in the journal's line at its attach.
.B EXAMPLES
has the commands in order.
.TP
\fBkeys\fR [\fImatch\fR] [\fB\-\-modifiers\fR]
List the bindable key names and their key codes: a copy of the kernel's key table from Linux
7.0, with the names a newer kernel's
.I \%/usr/include/linux/input\-event\-codes.h
adds where that file is installed, filtered by the substring
.IR match ,
in any case.
.B \-\-modifiers
lists the modifier aliases instead, which go in front of a key, as in
.BR ctrl+f15 ,
or are bound alone.
.B \-\-json
prints each name with its key code, or with
.B \-\-modifiers
each alias with the key it stands for, such as
.B rsuper
with
.BR KEY_RIGHTMETA .
Needs no daemon.
.TP
.B apps
List the running programs started from the desktop that a game profile can
load for, each by its application's name and the process name to give it.
Helpers, the shells bash, sh, dash and zsh, and the interpreters Python and gjs
are left out, and so is a program whose desktop id
has an entry in
.I /etc/xdg/autostart
or
.IR ~/.config/autostart ,
enabled or not, even when opened by hand, and so is one that runs in a unit
the session started for an autostart entry, named
.IR app\-id@autostart.service ;
a launcher stays either way, since its games run inside it. A game started
from Steam, Lutris, Heroic, Bottles or itch is listed by its own name beside the launcher's, a Proton or Wine game by its
Windows
.BR .exe .
With none to list, it prints
.BR "nothing running to pick" .
.TP
\fBgui\fR [\fB\-\-gapplication\-service\fR]
Open the window. The other commands work without GTK; without it this
prints what to install and exits 1.
.B \-\-gapplication\-service
starts it as a D-Bus service that opens the window when asked, as D-Bus does
from
.I io.github.gearbench.Gearbench.service
(see
.BR FILES ).
.TP
\fBtray\fR \fBon\fR|\fBoff\fR
On, the default, the daemon shows an icon where the desktop has a tray: on
GNOME, an AppIndicator extension, Ubuntu AppIndicators on Ubuntu and
AppIndicator and KStatusNotifierItem Support elsewhere; on KDE Plasma, its
panel's. The icon turns into an error sign while
.B status
lists a problem other than a device that does not answer, and into a warning
sign while every problem listed is a device that does not answer, such as one
asleep, switched off or out of range. Otherwise it turns into a low battery
while a device is below the second level of its
.B battery\-notices
preset (15%, or 5% under
.BR 10/5 )
and not plugged in; the A50's headset switched off on its base station counts
as plugged in. Where the tray shows tooltips, the icon's tooltip gives the
problems and the batteries. Both it and the menu write the home folder as ~.
A click opens a menu: each problem, one still past 80 characters cut after
what is wrong; each battery; Open Gearbench; the selected mouse's DPI
stages in effect (in host mode a loaded game profile's own), the current one
marked, while it has them and answers; an M profile submenu, the active one
marked, while a keyboard with lighting and no M keys, such as the G515, is
attached;
a Game profile submenu (No game profile, each game profile, and Keep loaded while one
is loaded) once there is a game profile; and the switches
Lights off when locked, while a keyboard with lighting is attached; DPI
notices, while a mouse whose DPI it sets is attached; Mouse follows the
keyboard that leads,
while a mouse with lighting is attached; and Headset follows the keyboard that
leads, while a headset whose lights
.B g432\-lights
drives is attached, both naming that keyboard by its model; with no model
chosen to lead and no keyboard set up since the daemon started, they read
Mouse follows the keyboard and Headset follows the keyboard.
An M or game profile's line past 80 characters is cut short.
They do what
.BR mouse\-dpi ,
.BR m\-profile ,
.BR load ,
.BR unload ,
.BR keep\-loaded ,
.BR lock\-lights ,
.BR dpi\-notices ,
.B mouse\-sync
and
.B headset\-sync
do. Off, no icon. Saved to
.IR config.json .
The icon and its menu answer only the tray: the owner of
.BR org.kde.StatusNotifierWatcher ,
and while GNOME Shell runs, only when that is GNOME Shell. Where GNOME
Shell does not run, they answer the owner of a name that starts
.B org.kde.StatusNotifierHost\-
too, the program that draws the icons, such as KDE Plasma's plasmashell.
Any other caller on the session bus gets
.B org.freedesktop.DBus.Error.AccessDenied
from the icon's and the menu's methods; its
.B Get
of a property fails, and its
.B GetAll
is empty.
While GNOME Shell runs, the icon is offered to no other owner of
.BR org.kde.StatusNotifierWatcher .
The icon takes no bus name: it is on a session connection of its own,
which a Flatpak app cannot reach unless it was given the whole session bus.
Needs python3\-gi; without it or without a tray,
.B status
says why on its
.B "tray icon"
line.
.TP
\fBbattery\-notices\fR [\fIlevels\fR \fB\-\-keyboard\fR \fImodel\fR|\fB\-\-mouse\fR \fImodel\fR|\fB\-\-headset\fR \fIkey\fR]
Each device's low-battery notices. With no argument it prints a line for each
keyboard, mouse and headset attached with a battery, then for each other device
.I config.json
keeps a preset for, marked not attached where it is not. Each line gives the
device's name, its model, its preset, and
.B "GNOME also warns"
or
.B "KDE Plasma also warns"
where the kernel gives the device's node a power supply, which UPower reads,
and the desktop that runs, by
.BR XDG_CURRENT_DESKTOP ,
warns of it itself.
Given levels and one device, it sets that device's:
.B \-\-keyboard
and
.B \-\-mouse
take a model by name or any of its product ids, attached or not, and
.B \-\-headset
a headset's key in
.IR spatial.json .
.B 30/15
(the default) posts a notice below 30% and another below 15%,
.B 10/5
below 10% and 5%, and
.B off
none; the tray marks the device's low battery below its preset's second
level, 15% under
.BR off .
.B 30/15\-urgent
posts the second notice at critical urgency, which GNOME Shell shows over a
full-screen window, expanded, until it is closed; it is refused for a device
attached whose battery the desktop warns of, and wherever the desktop warns of a
device's battery its second notice stays at normal urgency, however the preset was
set. A new preset takes the notice showing
down at the device's next reading and arms both notices again. Saved to
.I config.json
as
.BR battery_notices .
.SS Keyboard
Each M profile and each game profile keeps its lighting for each keyboard
model, and each model keeps its own brightness, the G915 on its cable and its
receiver being one model. A
keyboard with none of its own shows another model's, cut to what it can show:
an effect it lacks shows as Static, or as Per-key where the copy was on Per-key
before that effect, and a key it lacks is left out. Attaching it saves no copy;
the first change saves that copy, with the change, for it alone. Until then a
change to the lighting or brightness it copies shows on it at once.
The daemon drives every keyboard plugged in that answers, one of each model,
each with its G, M and MR keys, its lighting claim and its idle notices. One
leads (see
.BR sync\-keyboard ):
each other keyboard whose follow switch is on (see
.BR keyboard\-follow )
shows the leader's lighting for the layer in effect, cut
to what it can show, at the leader's brightness, in place of its own, which
stays in the profile; a change to the leader's lighting or brightness, or a
drag's preview, shows on the leader and on each that follows. An M key on any keyboard switches the M
profile of all; MR shifts the G keys of its own keyboard, every mouse's
buttons and a headset's G keys, never another keyboard's G keys, and lights
its own MR LED. The
keyboards dim together (see
.BR idle ).
A keyboard behind its receiver that does not answer, as one switched off, is
not asked at each look: its node is kept open, the keyboard
set up once it reports in, by its power-on report or its receiver's notice that
it linked, and asked again 10 seconds later, twice that
after each further miss, up to 120 seconds, so a probe nothing answers does not hold
the other keyboards' keys. A cable's node that nothing answers on is asked
again every 2 seconds, as is any node whose setup fails otherwise, except one
whose keyboard is left on its onboard profile.
Before the
daemon has found any keyboard since it started, answering or not, a lighting or
brightness change is refused with
.BR "no keyboard has been connected since the daemon started, and each keyboard model keeps its own lighting and brightness: connect a keyboard first" .
After that, a change made with no keyboard attached is saved for the leader
where the daemon has found a keyboard of the leader's model since it started,
answering or not, else for the first keyboard model it found.
.TP
\fBm\-profile\fR \fIindex\fR (or \fBprofile\fR)
Switch the M profile.
.I index
is 1, 2 or 3, the same thing the M1 to M3 keys do. A keyboard with no M keys,
such as the G515, the PRO X TKL LIGHTSPEED, the G213 Prodigy or the G915 X
LIGHTSPEED, has the same three M profiles, switched here, in the window and
from the tray.
.B profile
is its name in Gearbench 1.1.0 and does the same.
.TP
\fBbrightness\fR \fIlevel\fR [\fB\-\-keyboard\fR \fImodel\fR]
Set the brightness of the keyboard that leads, or with
.B \-\-keyboard
of the one named, by its model's name or id, as
.B "G515 TKL"
or
.BR c358 ;
the same goes for
.BR color ,
.B preview
and
.BR effect .
One whose model has not answered since the daemon started is refused
with
.BR "no G515 TKL has answered since the daemon started: connect it or switch it on" .
The G213 Prodigy, attached or away, is refused with
.BR "the G213 Prodigy has no brightness control Gearbench can set; its own brightness key sets it" .
A change for a keyboard that follows the leader is saved for its model and
shown once it stops following, and the note says so:
.BR "saved; the G515 TKL shows the G915's lighting while it follows it; \(gagearbench\-ctl keyboard\-follow off \-\-keyboard c358\(ga gives it its own" .
.I level
is 0 to 100, rounded down to a level the keyboard keeps (on the G915: 0, 12, 25, 50
or 100). The reply gives the requested and the applied level and the
keyboard's levels, each a percent, as
.B 25%
(with
.BR \-\-json ,
the numbers alone). Saved to
.I config.json
for the keyboard's model, as applied, or as given while the keyboard is away,
since its levels are not
known then; once it is back with its lights on, the level it keeps is saved.
The keyboard's brightness key, FN+F8 on a G515, sets the level too, and the
daemon saves the level it reports. A keyboard that follows the leader takes
the leader's level: this command for the leader sets the leader's and each that follows, and the
brightness key of the leader or of one that follows sets the leader's, which each that follows then takes.
The wired G515 TKL and the G915 X LIGHTSPEED keep their brightness when
switched off, and the G515 LIGHTSPEED TKL is taken to do the same, so the
daemon writes their level only when it changes and keeps the 13 that a
G515's FN+F8 or a G915 X LIGHTSPEED's Brightness button sets, which a write
would floor to 12.
At each connect, and at each setup again after a power cycle, a link back
or a resume, the daemon reads the level such a keyboard holds and keeps it in place of the saved one, so a
level its own key set while the daemon was not driving it shows in
.BR status .
A level this command saved while the keyboard was away or not answering is
written to it in place of the one it holds, unless the daemon restarts
before the keyboard is back; one that follows the leader
is given the leader's level. One that holds the level the daemon last left
its model at, such as the leader's on one that followed and is set up again
to lead, is given its saved level; the daemon forgets those levels when it
restarts.
The G515 LIGHTSPEED TKL and the G515's newer relatives other than the G915 X
LIGHTSPEED are untested, and their brightness is written on the wired G515 TKL's evidence.
Those newer relatives are written at each repaint, as no source tells whether
they keep their level, and read at each connect all the same.
A mouse that follows the keyboard that leads
.RB ( mouse\-sync )
and a headset's lights that follow it
.RB ( headset\-sync )
show the leader's color scaled by the leader's level, so a level given for
another keyboard leaves them as they are.
.TP
\fBlock\-lights\fR \fBon\fR|\fBoff\fR
On, the default, the keyboard's lights, and those of a mouse or headset that follows it, go off
while the screen is locked or blank and before a suspend, except a cluster
the keyboard cannot turn off (the G213 Prodigy's, where it lists no Off),
which stays as it is. While it is locked, any
input brings them back, a G, M or MR key included; they dim to half after 30
seconds without input and go off again after 60, by GNOME's idle time, which
a game holding off GNOME's idleness does not stop. Unlocking brings them back.
A keyboard dimmed or turned off by its own idle dimming (see
.BR idle )
stays so until the next keypress, a lighting change, new idle times,
.BR "\-\-never\-dim on" ,
a game profile loaded with
.BR "\-\-never\-dim\-with\-game\-profile on" ,
or its setup again after a power cycle, a link back or a resume; a mouse or
a headset's lights that follow the keyboard come back with any input.
The saved brightness is not touched. For the suspend the daemon holds a logind
delay inhibitor, shown by
.B systemd\-inhibit \-\-list
as
.BR Gearbench ,
and closes it after turning the lights off. A lighting change made while the
lights are down is saved and shows in full once they are back. Off, they stay on.
Saved to
.IR config.json .
Needs python3\-gi and logind; the blank, and the relight on input other than a
G, M or MR key while locked, need GNOME.
.TP
\fBidle\fR [\fB\-\-dim\-after\fR \fIseconds\fR] [\fB\-\-off\-after\fR \fIseconds\fR] [\fB\-\-never\-dim\fR \fBon\fR|\fBoff\fR] [\fB\-\-never\-dim\-with\-game\-profile\fR \fBon\fR|\fBoff\fR]
The keyboard's idle dimming. It dims to half
.B \-\-dim\-after
seconds after the last keypress, 60 by default, its lights go off
.B \-\-off\-after
seconds after that, 300 by default, and the next keypress brings them back.
Each is 10 to 3600.
.B "\-\-never\-dim on"
keeps them from dimming or going off; the keyboard's notices are still
answered.
.B "\-\-never\-dim\-with\-game\-profile on"
does the same while a game profile is loaded, by hand, for its program or
from
.IR config.json ,
and a keyboard dimmed or off for idleness lights at the load; once no game
profile is loaded they dim again
.B \-\-dim\-after
seconds after a keypress. The screen lock
.RB ( lock\-lights )
still turns the lights off. Off by default, saved as
.BR never_dim_with_game_profile .
A lighting change from the window or
.B gearbench\-ctl
counts as a keypress:
it brings them back, and made while they are lit it starts the count again.
With more than one keyboard driven they dim together: an idle notice dims
nothing while another keyboard has been typed on since its own, the last to
go idle dims them all, and a key on any of them, a lighting change, or one
back after it was off or unplugged lights them all, each woken by its idle
times written again. A keyboard that does not answer is passed by.
With no option it prints the settings, or, on the G213 Prodigy with no other
keyboard driven that dims, that it has no idle times. Saved to
.IR config.json ;
new times go to the keyboard at once, lighting it as a keypress would, or
when it is back if it is away or not answering. The brightness and the claim
are not touched. With the screen lock the darker of the two shows.
A mouse that follows the keyboard
.RB ( mouse\-sync )
stays lit while it is in use. Once nothing on the desktop, keyboard or mouse,
has been touched for
.B \-\-dim\-after
seconds (GNOME's idle time, which a game holding off GNOME's idleness does not
stop), it takes the keyboard's idle level, dimmed, off or lit, and follows it
until the next input, which lights it; a key lights the keyboard too. With the
keyboard away, sending no idle notices (as a G213, or one without lighting
Gearbench can drive), or with no report from it and not set up again
since it last stopped answering, it goes by the keyboard's times instead:
dimmed after
.B \-\-dim\-after
and off
.B \-\-off\-after
seconds after that. New
.B \-\-dim\-after
or
.B \-\-never\-dim
start it over lit. Where it follows the keyboard, it lights with the keyboard
at a new
.BR \-\-off\-after .
Going by the keyboard's times, a new
.B \-\-off\-after
alone moves only the mouse's off: a mouse that is off already stays off if the
new time has passed, and otherwise dims again and goes off when it passes. The
same values change nothing.
.B "\-\-never\-dim on"
keeps it lit, and so does a game profile loaded with
.BR "\-\-never\-dim\-with\-game\-profile on" .
Without python3\-gi or GNOME's idle monitor it stays lit.
A headset's lights that follow the keyboard
.RB ( headset\-sync )
go by the same level, with
.B mouse\-sync
on or off.
.TP
\fBcolor\fR \fI#RRGGBB\fR [\fB\-\-keyboard\fR \fImodel\fR]
Set the color of the profile in effect and save it, for the keyboard that
leads, or the one named (see
.BR brightness ).
.I #RRGGBB
is six hex digits, with or without the
.BR # ,
like
.BR '#0080ff' ;
.B '#fff'
is refused.
It leaves the lighting mode and the effect alone: in per-key mode with no
animation it is the background under the painted keys, and the Static color
is kept for the next switch to Static; in static mode, and under an
animation, it is the Static color, and the background is kept.
With a game profile loaded this edits that game profile and saves its file,
not the M profile.
It is refused while the file that would save it was edited by hand, will not
load, or has another file read in its place. Refused as the release of a drag
(see
.BR preview ),
it puts back the lighting the daemon holds.
.TP
\fBpreview\fR \fI#RRGGBB\fR [\fB\-\-keyboard\fR \fImodel\fR]
Repaint only, on the keyboard that leads and each that follows it, or on the
one named (see
.BR brightness ):
one per-key frame from zone 1 to the highest zone the keyboard's HID++ 0x8081 zone
bitmap sets, as read from it: 210, the logo, on the G915, and 254 on the G515 TKL.
Where that read was refused, the frame ends at the logo, or at the highest zone below it
on a keyboard with no logo, 111 on the G515 TKL. It sends no
zone effects and no M or MR LEDs, and no frame under an animation or while the
lights are down, nor to a keyboard that follows while the leader's layer runs an
animation in per-key mode: that keyboard shows its painted keys, and the
release changes only the Static color.
With the keyboard not attached or not answering, or its lights down, it sends
no frame and says why; an attached keyboard with no lighting Gearbench drives
is refused.
The G213 Prodigy, which has no per-key lighting, gets no frame: the color
reaches it only when
.B color
saves it.
Like any lighting change, a preview
first counts as a keypress (see
.BR idle ),
and one that brings the lights back applies the profile in effect first, zone
effects and the M and MR LEDs included.
A mouse and a headset's lights that follow the keyboard take it too, unless
the loaded game profile has a mouse color of its own. Outside Per-key this
is what dragging a color in the window sends; it saves nothing. The
release of the drag sends
.BR color ,
which saves.
It is refused as
.B color
would be for the file that would save it. A refusal part way through a
drag puts back the lighting the daemon holds; any other writes nothing.
.TP
\fBeffect\fR \fIname\fR [\fB\-\-speed\fR \fIms\fR] [\fB\-\-direction\fR \fIdirection\fR] [\fB\-\-second\-color\fR \fI#RRGGBB\fR] [\fB\-\-keyboard\fR \fImodel\fR]
Set the effect of the profile in effect and save it: the loaded game profile,
or the M profile, for the keyboard that leads, or the one named (see
.BR brightness ).
.I name
is one of \fBbreathe\fR, \fBcycle\fR, \fBdisabled\fR, \fBneon\fR,
\fBouter\-space\fR, \fBpulsar\-point\fR, \fBremind\-me\-of\fR,
\fBripple\fR, \fBstatic\fR or \fBwave\fR, and one that keyboard has:
one it lacks is
refused, naming the keyboard and the effects it has, which
.B status
lists on its
.B effects
line for the keyboard that leads.
While the keyboard is away the effect is saved without that check, and one it
lacks shows as told below once it is back.
The G915 has all but pulsar\-point,
remind\-me\-of, neon and outer\-space, and the wired G515 TKL all but the
last three; the
G515 runs cycle, wave
and ripple by effect ids of its own, 0x0015 to 0x0017, at full saturation,
each checked on a wired G515 TKL on 2026-10-18.
.B pulsar\-point
is G HUB's Pulsar Point, effect 0x0018 on 0x8071 cluster 0xff, which G HUB's
records give the G515s, the G915 X family and the G512 X pair: on those and the
G316 X pair the daemon reads that cluster where the keyboard's device
info counts effects on it, and the effect is offered where the cluster lists
0x0018, as the G915 X LIGHTSPEED's and the wired G515 TKL's do. It is one
write on that cluster, none on the keys' and the logo's, as G HUB writes it,
and has run on a G915 X LIGHTSPEED.
.BR remind\-me\-of ,
.B neon
and
.B outer\-space
are G HUB's Remind Me Of, Neon and Outer Space, which G HUB's records list for
the G512 X 75 and G512 X 98 alone: the same effect 0x0018 on the same cluster,
each with its signature index, 1, 2 and 3, in byte 9 of the parameters, where
pulsar\-point's is 0, and one color, bytes 3 to 5 sent 0. No read of the
keyboard tells them apart, so they are offered on a G512 X where its cluster
0xff lists 0x0018, and on no other model. None has run on the hardware.
A profile whose lighting of its own for the keyboard has an effect the
keyboard lacks shows Static on it, or Per-key where the profile is on
Per-key, and the journal says so once. A keyboard with no lighting of its own
in the profile, which shows another model's, shows such an effect the same
way, with no journal line.
.B disabled
turns the board black, with a per-key layout's painted keys still showing on
it. Every effect but static and disabled is an animation: one shows in place of the static or per-key board,
whose painted keys are kept and come back with
.BR "effect static" ,
and breathe, ripple, remind\-me\-of, neon and outer\-space run in the profile's
Static color, pulsar\-point in it and its second color. The G915's logo waves
with the keys under wave, and stays steady in the Static
color under ripple. On a keyboard whose logo's cluster lacks wave and ripple and
whose cluster 0xff lists them, as the G915 X LIGHTSPEED's does, each is one
write on cluster 0xff, none on the keys' and the logo's, and the logo takes part
in both.
.B \-\-speed
is how long one cycle takes, in milliseconds, so lower is faster: 100 to 1000
for ripple, and 1000 to 20000 for breathe, cycle and wave.
Pulsar\-point and the three on its effect take no speed, and a speed given
with one is refused: the keyboard runs each at its own pace.
Each is written with a period of 3000 ms, or the one set by hand in an
M profile's \fBparams\fR.
A speed an earlier build saved for one is left out as the file is read, said
once at info.
.B \-\-second\-color
is pulsar\-point's second color, magenta, #ff00ab, until one is chosen.
While the keyboard dims, pulsar\-point runs at half its intensity in the same
two colors, as G HUB dims it, and the three on its effect at half theirs in the
same color, as G HUB's encoder would dim them.
.B \-\-direction
is wave's, one the keyboard's model takes (a refusal of one it lacks names those it takes):
.B horizontal
(the default),
.BR vertical ,
.BR center\-out ,
.BR reverse\-horizontal ,
.B reverse\-vertical
or
.BR center\-in ;
the G213 Prodigy takes all but vertical and reverse\-vertical.
A speed is saved for each animation that takes one, the direction for wave
and the second color for pulsar\-point, so an effect named without them runs
with what was saved, or the default: for the speed, cycle and wave 2100 ms,
ripple 1000 and breathe 3000. A speed outside its range, a speed for
.BR static ,
.B disabled
or an animation that takes none,
a direction for anything but wave, or a second color for anything but
pulsar\-point is refused.
It is refused as
.B color
would be for the file that would save it.
.TP
\fBbind\fR \fIkey\fR \fIbinding\fR [\fB\-\-g\-shift\fR (or \fB\-\-mr\fR, \fB\-\-shift\fR)]
Bind a G key and save it.
.I key
is G1 to G5, F1 to F12 on a G915 TKL, G515\-F1 to G515\-F12 on a G515 TKL
or G515 LIGHTSPEED TKL, or PROXTKL\-F1 to PROXTKL\-F12 on a PRO X TKL
LIGHTSPEED.
On the G515's newer relatives it is G915X\-G1 to G915X\-G9 (the G915 X and
G915 X LIGHTSPEED), G915XTKL\-F1 to G915XTKL\-F12, G316X\-F1 to G316X\-F12
(the G316 X and G316 X 98), G512X75\-F1 to G512X75\-F12 or G512X98\-F1 to
G512X98\-F12.
A headset's G1 to G3 go under its model's name: G933\-G1 to G933\-G3 on a
G933, G935\-G1 to G935\-G3, G633\-G1 to G633\-G3 or G635\-G1 to G635\-G3.
A key name matches in any case.
An unbound F key types its own key on a G915 TKL or PRO X TKL LIGHTSPEED,
where Gearbench types it, and on a G515, whose keyboard's BASE layer of custom
assignments leaves it out once its write is tested. The keys of the G515's newer relatives do the same, each
doing what it does without the daemon, but the G915 X's G keys, while their
write is untested, send what the keyboard sends for them in its host mode,
which the daemon puts it in. A G915 X LIGHTSPEED's G key is the
exception: that BASE layer sets it to report, bound or not, so it does nothing
while unbound, as G1 to G5 do. On a model
whose custom assignments' write
is not yet tested on the hardware, the binding is saved and the reply's note
says what the keys do until then. Once that write is tested, a G515's key,
or one of its newer relatives' but the G915 X pair's, is set to report only
while it is bound on the base layer, so one bound with
.B \-\-g\-shift
and not without types its own key while G-Shift is held. The reply to such a
bind already has a note that says to bind it on the base layer too.
.I binding
is a key spec like
.BR ctrl+shift+f5 ,
a modifier alone like
.B shift
(any name \fBkeys \-\-modifiers\fR lists, which go in front of a key too, as
in
.BR rsuper+f15 ),
several keys held together like
.B a+b+c
or
.BR ctrl+a+b ,
or
.BI macro: name
to run a macro, or
.BR none ,
.B clear
or
.BR off ,
in any case, to clear it. Any key also takes
.BR g\-shift ,
which holds G-Shift while the key is held: a keyboard's key shifts that
keyboard's G keys, every mouse's buttons and a headset's G keys to their
G-Shift layer's bindings, as MR does,
and a headset's key shifts every keyboard too; a key of a keyboard with MR lights that
keyboard's MR LED while held, as MR does, and a headset's key the MR LED of
each keyboard that has one, as a mouse button does. A key bound to
.B g\-shift
takes no binding with
.BR \-\-g\-shift ,
.B g\-shift
is refused with
.BR \-\-g\-shift ,
and a key with a G-Shift layer binding is not bound to it until that is
cleared. Any key, on either layer, also takes
.B balance\-chat
and
.BR balance\-game :
each press sends the balance's word
.B chat
or
.B game
to the Outputs unit's socket, as
.B headset\-outputs balance
does, without waiting; while the unit is off the press does nothing, and the
daemon's journal says so once until a word goes through. While the session is not active, when another account or a text console has the screen, the press sends nothing. A headset's key also takes
.B brightness\-cycle
(the lighting stepped down to off and back to full, as G HUB's G1 does on a
G933),
.B lights\-toggle
(the lighting off, or back on at full; while the lights follow the keyboard,
see
.BR headset\-sync ,
each steps the keyboard's brightness in their place),
.B eq\-next
(the next preset of the headset's equalizer library, round from the last,
past any preset whose bands or gains the headset does not take;
on a G635, whose equalizer runs on the computer, a press does nothing and the
daemon's journal says why),
.B sidetone\-toggle
(sidetone off, or back on at its level, 50 where none was turned off) and
.BR none ,
in any case. Unbound, a headset's G1, as G933\-G1, runs
.B brightness\-cycle
and its G2 and G3 do nothing, so a cleared key is bound to
.BR none ,
as a mouse button is.
With
.BR \-\-g\-shift ,
.B clear
or
.B off
unbinds a headset's key there, so it does what it does without G-Shift, and
.B none
binds it to nothing there, as on a keyboard's key.
The Clear key cannot be bound by any name,
.B key_clear
included, since it is too easily confused with
.BR clear .
.B \-\-g\-shift
(\fB\-\-mr\fR, its name in Gearbench 1.2.0, or \fB\-\-shift\fR, its name in
1.1.0) binds the G-Shift layer instead, used while MR or a key
or button bound to
.B g\-shift
is held; a G key unbound there
does what it does without G-Shift. The binding is held as long as the G key is:
the spec's keys go down on the press in the order named and come up on the
release in reverse. A spec that names a key twice, as
.BR a+a ,
is refused, as is a
.B +
with no key beside it, as
.BR ctrl++ ;
the plus key is
.B shift+equal
on a US layout, or
.B kpplus
on the keypad. Spaces or a line break around a binding, here and in
.BR mouse\-bind ,
are not saved; a hand-edited file with them still loads. A key held
by two things at once, another G key, a mouse button or a running macro, stays
down until the last of them lets go. Once SysRq goes down with Alt held,
a binding or a macro presses only Alt, Shift or SysRq until Alt comes up,
and the journal names the key it did not press, as the
.B b
of
.BR alt+sysrq+b :
the kernel would run that key as a magic SysRq command, and
.B b
reboots at once under Ubuntu's kernel.sysrq default. A macro's key step of
.B alt+sysrq+b
presses none of its keys, and the journal names the keys of the step that
nothing else already holds; for a text
step it gives the character's place in the text. A screen lock, GNOME's screen blank, a
suspend, or another account or a text console taking the screen lets go of
what a G key holds and of G-Shift, whatever key or button holds it,
and a release after that does nothing: a G key or MR still held
through it needs a new press. The spec is
parsed before it is sent. A macro name is not checked against the library,
since a binding may name a macro written later; one over 1,000 characters or
holding a control character is refused. With a game profile loaded this
writes to that game profile.
.TP
.B macros
List the macros in
.IR ~/.config/gearbench/macros/ ,
with their step count and repeat mode, marking the one running now with
.BR * .
One played with a delay is not marked while it counts down; a line under the
list says when it starts, in whole seconds rounded up, as in
.B "Macro Spam starts in 3 s. Click into the window it is for."
With no macro saved it says so, and still names a macro running: the window's
Run plays the editor's macro unsaved.
Macros
are written in the window's Macros tab, or as JSON by hand. A file edited by
hand runs as edited at the next press, and the daemon does not save over it,
rename it or delete it until a
.BR reload .
One deleted by hand is written again when the macro is saved.
.TP
\fBmacro\-run\fR \fIname\fR [\fB\-\-delay\fR \fIseconds\fR]
Run the saved macro
.I name
after a countdown of
.IR seconds ,
3 by default, 0 to 60: time to click into the window the macro is for, since
it types wherever the focus is. The daemon counts down, so the prompt comes
back at once and
.B status
shows the countdown; with the terminal still in front at the end, or no
delay, the macro types into the terminal. No key is held, so a
.B once
or
.B while_held
macro runs one pass and a
.B toggle
macro runs until
.BR macro\-stop ,
the window's Stop, another macro, a save or delete of it, a screen lock,
GNOME's screen blank or a suspend, or the daemon stopping; a rebind or a profile switch
leaves it running. The window's Run does the same with what its editor
holds, saved or not. While the screen is locked, while GNOME has blanked it, or the machine is
going to sleep, both are refused,
.BR "macro \(dqSpam\(dq not started: the screen is locked" ,
and a G key or mouse button bound to a macro starts nothing. The same holds
while the session is not active, when another account or a text console has
the screen
.RB ( "macro \(dqSpam\(dq not started: the session is not active" );
then no G key or mouse button bound to a key, or to back or forward, types
either, a G915 TKL or PRO X TKL LIGHTSPEED types its F1 to F12 from its own
profile, and switching
away stops a running macro.
Without python3\-gi none of this is refused, and without logind only a blank
is.
.TP
.B macro\-stop
Stop the macro that is running or counting down, whatever started it: a G
key, a mouse button,
.B macro\-run
or the window's Run. Says
.B no macro was running
when none was.
.SS Game profiles
A game profile that names programs is loaded while a window of one of them has
focus: with two games running, the one in front has its profile loaded, and
focus anywhere else brings back the M profile or a profile loaded by hand. The
daemon that
.I gearbench.service
runs reads focus from the Gearbench focus extension for GNOME Shell,
which gives the
focused window's process id only to a program of the same user in a cgroup
named
.IR gearbench.service .
While the extension is not running, and
for a daemon started by hand with
.BR "gearbench\-ctl daemon" ,
the daemon reads Xwayland, which sees X11 programs, and Proton games unless
they run Wine's Wayland driver (GE-Proton's
.BR PROTON_ENABLE_WAYLAND=1 ),
and a native
Wayland window counts as no program. With neither, the daemon follows running
programs instead: a profile loads while its program runs. A focus change
counts once focus has stayed on a window for 0.3 seconds, or 1 second after
the first of a run of changes that does not settle.
.TP
.B profiles
List the game profiles, marking the loaded one, with each one's mode, its
painted key count and the programs that load it. A profile whose effect is an
animation or disabled shows that effect in place of its mode.
.TP
\fBload\fR \fIname\fR
Load the game profile called \fIname\fR, in any case and with punctuation
and symbols counted as spaces (a name with no letter or digit keeps its
symbols), over the active M profile.
It prints the profile's name and its effect as
.B profiles
names it;
.B \-\-json
gives its mode and effect both.
The daemon loads it again at its next start. One a program loaded stays that
program's instead: when that program loses focus or exits, it gives way to the
profile it was loaded over, and that is the one the next start loads. On a
mouse in host mode its own DPI stages and report rate, where it has them,
come into force (see
.BR mouse\-dpi\-stages ).
.TP
.B unload
Drop the loaded game profile, back to the M profile's lighting and bindings
and, on a mouse in host mode, profile 1's DPI stages and report rate, or on a
mouse whose onboard profile is not written, the report rate kept for its
model, where one was set live (see
.BR mouse\-dpi\-stages ).
.TP
.B let\-load
End every hold-off by hand and make the daemon scan at once. The profile whose
program has focus, or runs where focus is not followed, then loads, unless
.B keep\-loaded
holds a profile loaded by hand. It names the profiles no longer held off
.RB ( "no longer held off: Doom" );
with nothing held off it changes nothing, and says
.BR "nothing was held off" .
A game profile is held off when
.BR load ,
.B unload
or
.B promote
makes another choice by hand while it is loaded and its program has focus, or
runs where focus is not followed, whether its program loaded it or it was
loaded by hand.
It stays off until focus leaves that profile's programs or, where focus is not
followed, until none of them runs. Several can be held off at once;
.B status
names the latest on its game profile line, with the program that holds it now.
.TP
\fBkeep\-loaded\fR \fBon\fR|\fBoff\fR
With
.BR on ,
a game profile loaded by hand stays loaded when a window of a program that
would load another one gets focus, or, where focus is not followed, when that
program starts. With
.BR off ,
as at every start of the daemon, it is replaced and comes back when that
program loses focus or exits. It does not stop an auto-load over the M profile.
.B status
adds
.B kept loaded
to its game profile line while the switch is on and a profile loaded by hand
is loaded, and with
.B \-\-json
always gives the switch as
.BR keep_loaded .
.TP
\fBsave\-profile\fR \fIname\fR [\fB\-\-process\fR \fIprocess\fR]... [\fB\-\-mouse\-color\fR \fI#RRGGBB\fR|\fBnone\fR]
Save the live lighting of the keyboard that leads, for its model, with the
G-key bindings, G-Shift layer and mouse bindings, as a game profile in
.IR ~/.config/gearbench/profiles/ ;
a profile written over keeps its lighting for each other keyboard model.
It loads and unloads nothing itself:
.B load
loads the profile saved, and the daemon's next scan, within 5 seconds, acts on
its programs.
The painted keys, the background and the Static color go with it in the mode
they are in, so in static mode the layout and the background are saved hidden.
An animation is saved with its speed where it takes one, wave's direction and
pulsar\-point's second color, the speed and the direction including ones set by
hand in an M profile's
.BR params ,
and the keys and the background stay hidden under it.
A board under
.B disabled
is saved with the effect static and both colors black.
.B \-\-process
names a program that loads it by itself, and is repeatable; a launcher is
set anyway, with the warning
.B profile\-programs
gives. A game profile
of that name is written over, keeping its programs unless
.B \-\-process
names others. A loaded game profile's own DPI stages and report rate go with
it; from an M profile none do, and a game profile written over loses its own.
.B \-\-mouse\-color
gives the profile its mouse color, as
.B profile\-mouse\-color
sets it; without it the profile takes the loaded game profile's mouse color,
or none from an M profile. The report gives the profile's mouse color, or
.BR none .
A color that is not six hex digits is refused, and nothing is saved.
The name is trimmed, and a blank one refused, as is a name over 1,000
characters, a name or program holding a control character, a blank program
name, a program name over 255 bytes, or more than 64 programs. A save under a
name no game profile has is refused too where the file that name calls for is
already there and holds another game profile or cannot be read, and so is a
save over the loaded profile while its file was edited by hand, could not be
read when the daemon last tried, or has another file read in its place.
.TP
\fBprofile\-programs\fR \fIname\fR [\fIprogram\fR]...
Set the programs that load a game profile, loaded or not, by themselves while
a window of theirs has focus, or while they run where focus is not followed;
with no program given, it loads by hand only. It loads and unloads
nothing itself; the daemon's next scan, within 5 seconds, acts on the new
list. A name matches a running program's whole name, in any
case, with
.B .exe
optional on either side. A launcher, such as Steam, is set anyway, with a
warning on standard error: the profile would load while the launcher itself
has focus, or, where focus is not followed, whenever it runs. A profile loads
for at most 64 programs, each name at most 255 bytes, the longest file name,
and none may be blank or hold a control character; a list that breaks any of
these is refused, and a profile file that breaks any cannot be read. For the
loaded profile it is refused, too, while its file was edited by hand, could
not be read when the daemon last tried, or has another file read in its place.
.TP
\fBprofile\-mouse\-color\fR \fIname\fR \fI#RRGGBB\fR|\fBnone\fR
Set a game profile's mouse color, loaded or not, and nothing else of it, as the
window's Game profiles dialog does. While the profile is loaded, a mouse and a
headset's lights that follow the keyboard show that color at the keyboard's
brightness. The color is six hex digits, as
.B color
takes, or
.B none
for no color of its own, so that the mouse and the headset's lights show the
keyboard's color around its painted keys, the background in per-key mode. A
loaded profile's color shows
at once. The report gives the profile and its mouse color. A name no game
profile has, and a color that is not six hex digits, are refused, and nothing
is written; for the loaded profile it is refused, too, while its file was
edited by hand, could not be read when the daemon last tried, or has another
file read in its place.
.TP
.B promote
Copy the loaded game profile into the active M profile, save it, and unload
the game profile. Its lighting replaces the M profile's for every keyboard
model, each in the mode it is in, with its painted keys and background, hidden
ones included, its Static color, and its effect with its speeds, direction and
second color; its G-key, G-Shift and mouse button bindings replace the M profile's
too.
A keyboard or mouse model the game profile has none for loses the M profile's own
and keeps what it had under the game profile. Its own DPI stages, report rate
and mouse color stay with it: an M profile has none.
With no game profile loaded, it saves the active M profile as it shows, and a
game profile that
.I config.json
names but that is not loaded, because its file could not be read or its folder
could not be listed, stays named: a reload or a later start loads it once the
file can be read or the folder listed.
.TP
\fBrename\-profile\fR \fIold\fR \fInew\fR
Rename a game profile. Its file moves to the one the new name calls for, and
whatever named it follows: a loaded profile stays loaded, one a program loaded
stays that program's, and a held-off one stays held off, under the new
name. Nothing is loaded or unloaded. Where the file is a link, the link moves
and the rename writes through it. The new name is trimmed, and a blank one
is refused, as is one over 1,000 characters or holding a control character,
the name of another game profile, or one whose file is already there and
holds another game profile or cannot be read. A name matches in any case,
with punctuation and symbols counted as spaces (a name with no letter or
digit keeps its symbols). A rename of a loaded profile is refused
too while its file was edited by hand, could not be read when the daemon last
tried, or has another file read in its place, and so is a rename of the
profile that
.I config.json
names while that file was edited by hand or could not be read.
.TP
\fBdelete\-profile\fR \fIname\fR
Delete a game profile's file and clear every reference to it. A loaded one is
unloaded; one a program loaded gives way to the profile it was loaded over.
Where the file is a link, the delete removes the link and leaves the file it
names.
.SS Importing from G HUB
.TP
\fBimport\-ghub\fR \fIfile\fR [\fB\-\-list\fR] [\fB\-\-profile\fR \fIname\fR]... [\fB\-\-all\fR] [\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR] [\fB\-\-on\-clash\fR \fBreplace\fR|\fBkeep\-both\fR|\fBkeep\-mine\fR] [\fB\-\-with\-programs\fR]
Import profiles, bindings, lighting and macros from G HUB's settings.
.I file
is G HUB's
.IR settings.db ,
or a JSON file holding the same settings. On Windows, close G HUB, then copy
.I settings.db
from
.IR %LOCALAPPDATA%\eLGHUB ,
with its
.I settings.db\-wal
beside it if there is one, which can hold the newest changes. The database is read from a private copy and
never written. Each file must be a regular file, or a link to one. When
.I settings.db
is a link, the
.I settings.db\-wal
beside the link is read, or with none there, the one beside the file it links
to.
.I settings.db\-wal
must belong to the account that owns the entry it sits beside: the link's own
owner beside the link, the file's owner beside the file.
.I settings.db
and
.I settings.db\-wal
may each be at most 64 MiB; a JSON file, and the settings the database holds,
at most 16 MiB. A file of more than 10,000 profiles or
10,000 cards (what G HUB puts on a key, a button or a profile, such as a
keystroke, a macro, an action or the profile's lighting) is refused,
naming the cap. The database must keep its settings in a plain table, not a
view or a virtual table and with no generated column, and no entry of its
schema may be over 16 KiB. SQLite's read of it is stopped after 10 seconds,
or once SQLite holds 256 MiB of memory.
.IP
.B \-\-list
shows what the file holds: the G HUB profiles, named by their application,
with their G keys, mouse buttons, lighting and macros, every step of each
macro a line under it, and what cannot be imported and why. A G915 TKL's G1
to G12, where a profile has one, come in as its F1 to F12, and a PRO X TKL
LIGHTSPEED's as its PROXTKL\-F1 to PROXTKL\-F12, beside the
G915's G1 to G5. A G815's G1 to G5 come in as the G915's; where both
keyboards hold different cards of the user's on one key, the G915's comes in
and the G815's is listed as not imported. A profile with
a name of its own, in an application G HUB has more than one profile for,
is named by both, such as
.IR "Apex Legends: Ranked" .
At most 64
programs come over per profile, and a program name over 255 bytes is left
out and listed as
.BR "Program names over 255 bytes: no file name is that long" .
Control characters in G HUB's names are dropped; an error that quotes the
file drops each one or shows it as its escape, such as \ex1b. A JSON escape
in a name that is not a whole character, such as \eud800, becomes a question
mark, and a line or paragraph separator (U+2028, U+2029) or a character that
turns the direction of the text after it (U+202A to U+202E, U+2066 to U+2069)
becomes U+FFFD.
Application, profile and card names are cut at 256 characters,
each cut listed, as
.B "Application name after 256 characters"
and
.B "G1: its card's name after 256 characters"
at each key or button the card is on.
Once a file's Not imported text passes 16,777,216 characters, each profile
from there on, the one that passed it included, shows one line in place of its
own, such as
.BR "112 lines not shown: the file's Not imported text is over 16 million characters" .
Each profile lists again every step of each macro it binds, so once the
lines under the profiles pass 16,777,216 characters as printed, each profile
from there on, the one that passed it included, is listed by name alone, and
a line after them says how many, such as
.BR "3 profiles listed by name alone: the listing is over 16 million characters" ,
and that
.B \-\-profile
.I name
.B \-\-dry\-run
says what importing one would write.
Each profile repeats its application's name, so once the profiles' name
lines pass 16,777,216 characters as printed, the profile that passed them
and each after it are not listed, and a line after them says how many, such as
.BR "3,000 profiles not listed: the listing's names are over 16 million characters" .
A last line names the file's other devices, which are not imported: 20 at
most, then how many more.
.B \-\-list
writes nothing and needs no daemon. It is also what runs when neither
.B \-\-profile
nor
.B \-\-all
is given.
.B \-\-profile
imports one profile, as
.B \-\-list
names it, and is repeatable;
.B \-\-all
imports every game profile. The desktop default, named
.BR Desktop ,
is never in
.BR \-\-all ,
so on a file with no other profile
.B \-\-all
writes nothing and says so;
every other profile imported becomes a game profile. When
.B \-\-profile
names
.BR Desktop ,
its three banks' G keys and G-Shift layers replace those of M1, M2 and M3,
and its per-key lighting, if it has any, replaces the G915's on all three, and other
keyboards keep theirs.
Each mouse it has buttons for gets them on all three, and other mice keep
theirs. A G915 TKL's F1 to F12, and a PRO X TKL LIGHTSPEED's PROXTKL\-F1 to
PROXTKL\-F12, are each replaced only where the desktop default has that
keyboard.
A file can hold more than one desktop default, the second listed as
.BR "Desktop (2)" ;
naming two is an error, and
nothing is written, since both would go onto M1, M2 and M3.
.B \-\-dry\-run
says what would be written, each macro's steps included, and writes
nothing; a clash over a name, as below, stops it as it stops an import
unless
.B \-\-on\-clash
says what to do.
.IP
A game profile imported loads by hand, with no programs, unless
.B \-\-with\-programs
is given: then each loads for the programs G HUB names for it. One that
replaces a game profile of yours without it, or with it where G HUB names
no programs, keeps that profile's programs. A program
such as
.I firefox.exe
also loads the profile for a Linux program of that name without
.IR .exe ,
so a file from someone else can name a program you run every day. The last
of a game profile's notes in
.B \-\-list
says it loads by hand, or with
.B \-\-with\-programs
what it loads for (the first eight programs, then how many more), and
.B \-\-dry\-run
ends each game profile's line with
.BR "keeps your programs" ,
.B loads by hand
or
.BR "loads for" " and its programs."
.IP
A name Gearbench already has for a game profile or a macro stops the import
before anything is written, unless
.B \-\-on\-clash
says what to do.
.B replace
writes over it, though a game profile keeps its own DPI stages, report rate,
mouse color and lighting on keyboards other than the G915, and
.B \-\-dry\-run
adds
.B replacing yours
to its line.
.B keep\-both
imports it beside yours under a new name such as
.IR "Apex Legends (2)" ;
a game profile kept this way loads by hand.
.B keep\-mine
leaves it out, and a binding to a macro left out uses yours.
.B keep
and
.BR skip ,
the words of Gearbench 1.1.0, are taken for
.B keep\-both
and
.BR keep\-mine .
A macro that is
already there with the same steps and repeat mode is used as it is. Two
imported macros of one name are asked about once at most and are never both
written under it: the second, when written, takes a new name.
A game profile of a name Gearbench has that holds the same G keys, G-Shift layer,
mouse buttons, lighting and macros, whatever its programs, is already there:
no clash, nothing written, and one line says so. An imported macro with other
steps or another repeat mode, under the name of one of yours that such a
profile binds, takes a new name and is not asked about, so that profile keeps
playing yours.
.IP
With
.BR \-\-with\-programs ,
a program another game profile already loads stays with it:
the import comes without that program and says which profile keeps it.
Where two replaces each wait for the other's to free a program, the line on
the program that the one sent first cannot write says only that another game
profile loads it.
Two names are one program when one running process would match both: the
kernel keeps the first 15 bytes of a process's name, so two names over 15
bytes that share those are one. A
program two imported profiles would share goes to the one G HUB had active for
its application, else to the one
.B \-\-list
shows first, and the import says which.
Once the lines on programs a profile comes without pass 16,777,216
characters, each profile from there on, the one that passed them included, has
one line in place of its own, such as
.BR "Game profile \(dqRacer (2)\(dq is imported without 64 programs, not listed: the import's lines about programs are over 16 million characters" .
.IP
A game profile, or an M profile from the desktop default, that binds a macro
which could not be saved is not written, since its key would play your own
macro of that name, or none: the import prints
.B not sent, its macro \(dqNAME\(dq was not saved
for it and exits 1.
The import's lines on what became of a name or a program, already there, not
imported, imported under a new name or without a program, are printed by
.BR \-\-dry\-run ,
and by an import only when the daemon wrote every request; after an error it
prints what it wrote and the errors. A line on a game profile already there is
also printed when a clash stops the import before anything is written.
An import onto the loaded game profile or the active M profile is saved even
when the keyboard does not show it in full, as when it is away, dimmed or dark,
or refuses the write: a
.B note:
line gives the daemon's reason, and the exit status is 0.
.IP
With
.BR \-\-json ,
.B \-\-list
prints the profiles found,
.B \-\-dry\-run
the requests it would send, and an import the daemon's replies, with
.B {\(dqok\(dq: false, \(dqerror\(dq: \(dqnot sent, its macro \e\(dqNAME\e\(dq was not saved\(dq}
in the place of a profile that was not sent. Once the
.BR processes ,
.BR summary ,
.BR macros ,
.B skipped
and
.B notes
of the profiles that
.B \-\-list
prints with
.B \-\-json
pass 16,777,216 characters as JSON writes them, each profile from
there on, the one that passed them included, keeps only its
.BR name ,
.BR application ,
.B ghub_name
and
.BR desktop ,
with
.B \(dqlisted_by_name\(dq: true
in place of those five.
Once those four fields pass 16,777,216 characters as JSON writes them, the
profile that passed them and each after it are left out of the list, and
the line that says how many goes to standard error.
A clash that stops the import
prints nothing on standard output under
.BR \-\-json :
the clash goes to standard error and the exit status is 1, as for a file it
cannot read.
.SS Importing the profiles and macros pack
.TP
\fBimport\-pack\fR [\fIname\fR...] [\fB\-\-list\fR] [\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR] [\fB\-\-replace\fR \fIname\fR]... [\fB\-\-without\-programs\fR]
Import game profiles and macros from the pack that comes with
Gearbench: all of them, or for each
.I name
the pack's game profile or macro of that name, or both when a profile and a
macro share it. A name matches in any case, with punctuation and symbols
counted as spaces (a name with no letter or digit keeps its symbols). A name
the pack does not
have is an error, and nothing is written.
.B \-\-list
uses neither the names nor
.B \-\-replace
and shows the whole pack. Nothing from the pack is imported
until this is run or the window's Import pack is used.
.IP
The pack is
.IR profiles/*.json " and " macros/*.json
under
.I /usr/share/gearbench/pack/
(from a git checkout, its
.IR pack/ ),
in the formats of
.I ~/.config/gearbench/profiles/
and
.IR macros/ .
Each file is read as Gearbench reads your own. One that cannot be read, a
game profile that binds a G key or a mouse button or holds DPI stages or a
report rate, and a second file of a name another already has are left out:
listed under Left out, never imported. The pack's
.I notes.json
holds a line to show under a game profile or macro, such as a program a game
profile leaves out. A
.I notes.json
or a part of it that Gearbench refuses is left out too, with its reason; a
note is one line of at most 240 characters.
.IP
.B \-\-list
shows each game profile with its lighting and programs, each macro with its
steps, the pack's note under any that has one, and the Left out list, with
.RB \(dq "Use at your own risk: some games forbid macros." \(dq
above the macros; it writes nothing and needs no daemon.
.B \-\-dry\-run
says what would be written, and writes nothing. A pack game profile is its
name, programs, lighting and mouse color only, its mouse color a color it paints on
the most keys. It loads for the programs the pack names
for it unless
.B \-\-without\-programs
is given or the pack names none for it; then a new one loads by hand, and
one that replaces yours keeps your programs. A program another game
profile of yours already loads stays with it, and the import says which.
.IP
A name Gearbench already has, for a game profile or a macro, keeps yours.
The import prints
.B Game profile \(dqNAME\(dq is not imported; yours is kept.\&
for a game profile, or the same line starting
.B Macro
for a macro, and ends with a line on
.BR \-\-replace .
.B \-\-replace
.I name
takes the pack's instead, and is repeatable; a name the pack does not have
is an error here too. For a game profile it writes
the name as the pack spells it, the pack's lighting for the G915, the one
keyboard the pack has lighting for, its mouse color and,
without
.BR \-\-without\-programs ,
its programs if it names any, and leaves your lighting on any other keyboard,
your G keys, G-Shift layer, mouse buttons, DPI stages and report rate;
.B \-\-dry\-run
says
.BR "replacing your lighting and mouse color" .
For a macro it writes the name as the pack spells it, and the pack's steps and
repeat mode, over yours. With
names given, a
.B \-\-replace
name must be one of them, since only those are imported; any other is an
error, and nothing is written. A game
profile of yours with the same lighting for the G915 and the same mouse color,
or a macro with the same steps and repeat mode, is already there: no clash,
nothing written, and one line says so. One with the same lighting and no mouse color of its own,
as one imported before the pack had mouse colors, takes the pack's mouse
color unasked, through
.BR profile_mouse_color ,
and nothing else of it is written; the import says so, and
.B \-\-dry\-run
lists it as
.BR "the pack's mouse color" .
A daemon of Gearbench 1.1.0 or older does not know
.BR profile_mouse_color :
each game profile of yours that takes the pack's mouse color unasked fails with
.BR "the daemon running is older than this Gearbench and wrote nothing" ,
and the last error line is
.BR "restart the daemon: \(gasystemctl \-\-user restart gearbench\(ga" .
A daemon of Gearbench 1.2.1 or older writes none of the pack's game profiles:
they are sent as version 4, and each fails with
.BR "version 4 is not supported (expected 1, 2 or 3)" ,
or, from a daemon of Gearbench 1.0.1 or older,
.BR "version 4 is not supported (expected 1)" .
Restarting the daemon is the fix here too, though the restart line is printed
only when the daemon did not know a command.
As with
.BR import\-ghub ,
the lines on a name you have, a program another game profile loads and
.B \-\-replace
are printed by
.BR \-\-dry\-run ,
and by an import only when the daemon wrote every request; a
.B note:
line says why the keyboard does not show an import onto the loaded game
profile in full.
With
.BR \-\-json ,
.B \-\-list
prints the pack as an object of
.BR directory ,
.BR profiles ,
.BR macros ,
.B macros_note
(the risk line above)
and
.BR broken ,
.B \-\-dry\-run
the requests it would send, and an import the daemon's replies.
.SS Mouse
.BR mouse ,
.BR mouse\-dpi ,
.BR mouse\-bind ,
.BR mouse\-factory\-bindings ,
.BR mouse\-color ,
.B mouse\-effect
and the five under
.B Mouse onboard memory
take
.BI \-\-mouse " model"
for the mouse they act on, in place of the selected one (see
.BR mouse\-select ),
which the window or another command can move between two commands.
.I model
is a model name or any of the model's product ids, in any case, as
.B mouse
lists them:
.B 4099
and
.B c095
each name a G502 X PLUS, on its receiver or its cable. The mouse with the
product id given comes first, then the first attached of its model. One not
attached is refused with nothing sent, and the refusal names the mice that
are;
.B mouse\-bind
and
.B mouse\-factory\-bindings
take any model Gearbench drives, attached or not. On success, the reply to
.BR mouse\-dpi ,
.BR mouse\-color ,
.B mouse\-effect
and the five under
.B Mouse onboard memory
starts with the mouse it is for, as in
.BR "mouse: G502 X PLUS (4099)" ,
which
.B \-\-json
gives as the key
.B mouse
after
.BR ok .
A mouse with a cable and a receiver is driven on its cable while both are
plugged in: attached through its receiver, it moves to its cable as the
cable's node appears (see
.BR daemon ),
and the journal says
.B moved from
the receiver's node to the cable's; with its cable pulled, it is looked for
on its receiver at once.
.TP
\fBmouse\fR [\fB\-\-mouse\fR \fImodel\fR]
Show the state of the selected mouse, or the one
.B \-\-mouse
names, and, with more than one attached, a list of them with the selected one
starred. Under its name, a model not yet tested on the hardware
gets the note that asks for a device report from it (see
.BR report ).
It shows only what the mouse has: the
battery; the firmware version, where the mouse gave one; the DPI and its
range; the report rate; the
lighting clusters, the effects of theirs that
.B mouse\-effect
takes, and the mouse's color and the effect last set, or that it shows its
own lighting until a color or effect is sent, or, while
.B mouse\-sync
is on, that it follows the keyboard that leads, in the loaded game profile's
mouse color where it has one; and onboard profile 1
with its report rate, CRC
and DPI stages. On a model with a button table it also shows profile 1's
button table and the bindings of its buttons, and where the model's event node
is read, the G502 X PLUS alone, the evdev nodes the daemon reads its buttons
from, the one it may not read and why, or that none is open. With a game
profile loaded, the
.B game profile:
line gives that profile's own DPI stages and report rate for this mouse and
whether they are in effect. In host mode, profile 1's stand in for those it
lacks, but on a model whose writes are not tested a rate kept for the model,
where there is one, stands in for its rate. Where
.B mouse\-dpi\-stages
and
.B mouse\-rate
would give the profile its own, the line also shows for a profile that has
none, and says so. The
.B mode:
line says whether the mouse is in host mode, and if host mode did not take,
why (see
.BR mouse\-host ).
A mouse whose switch back to onboard mode was refused, or answered and not
taken, after host mode did not take or at
.BR "mouse\-host off" ,
is
.BR "host, stuck there" :
its programmable buttons do nothing until it is switched off and on,
reconnects, or
.B "mouse\-host on"
arms it again.
A mouse not answering at the time is
.BR "host until the mouse answers" :
the daemon finishes the switch, or with
.B "mouse\-host on"
arms it again, once it answers.
The
.B "programmable buttons:"
line, the Mouse tab's Programmable buttons row, says what profile 1 holds
for G4 to G9 (enabled, fallback, partly enabled or not enabled) and the
write to run, if any:
.B mouse\-fallback\-buttons
while Gearbench drives the buttons in host mode, otherwise
.BR mouse\-enable\-buttons .
On a model whose onboard profile Gearbench does not write until that is
tested on this hardware, it names no write, and it gives each
of the model's buttons and its job in profile 1. On such a model, with
programmable buttons or none, the report's last line,
.BR writes: ,
says why. On any other model, while the
.B "onboard profile 1:"
line says
.B not known
or
.BR "CRC fails" ,
the line says the buttons are not known and names neither write: both writes
refuse a profile 1 that fails its CRC, and one not known is shown as read
before the failed write. On a model
whose event node Gearbench does not read it never names
.BR mouse\-enable\-buttons .
The
.B bindings:
lines list the mouse's own buttons under its own names, each with its
G-Shift binding where it has one; on a mouse whose buttons the daemon does not drive, the
.B bindings:
line says why instead, and the
.B "in profile 1:"
lines under it give what profile 1 has each button do.
The
.B running:
line names the onboard profile the mouse runs; in host mode, unless stuck
there, it adds the one that runs when Gearbench stops. Otherwise, on any other
than profile 1, what is shown of profile 1 is not in effect, and
.B status
says why the switch back did not take, that a mouse not yet tested is not switched
until that is tested, or, with no failed switch to name, that the mouse
switches back when it is next switched on. A profile 1 whose CRC
fails is never switched to:
.B status
says so and, on a model whose writes are tested, to write the newest good
backup over it with
.B mouse\-restore
before switching the mouse off; on a mouse not yet tested, that a blank
profile 1 fails its CRC too and nothing needs doing. In host mode no onboard
profile runs
(see
.BR mouse\-host ).
It attaches nothing itself;
asking with no mouse attached and no
.BR \-\-mouse ,
or with
.B \-\-mouse
naming a mouse not attached, or about a mouse not answering, pulls the daemon's
next attempt forward to within 30 seconds, unless the mouse's receiver has said
its link is down (switched off or out of range): that mouse is not asked again
until the receiver says the link is back, the mouse is heard from, or the
machine resumes from a suspend. Asking
does not wake a mouse nobody is touching: the readings are the daemon's last
ones. They are taken again only once the mouse has been heard from since
(motion through its receiver, a button the daemon reads, a command it
answered, or a report, though a battery report only from a mouse shown not
answering), once they are five minutes old, once the mouse has gone into or
out of host mode since, or while a failed write has left profile 1 unknown.
Once the mouse has reported its
battery by itself, that report is shown and the battery is no longer asked
for. A mouse that did not answer is shown, under its device line, as
.BR "not answering: asleep or switched off" ,
with the time its readings were taken, or that they are its last where that
time is not known, and its battery with the time it was
last read or reported, unless that reading gives neither a level nor a state,
shown as
.B "not read"
alone; a time from an earlier day has the date before it. A
reading not taken yet, or one the
mouse refused, is shown as
.BR "not read" ,
except the battery, which has no line until it is first read or reported and
keeps its last reading through a refused one.
With
.BR \-\-json ,
its
.B firmware
is the version of the firmware the mouse runs, such as
.BR "U1 69.00.B0009" ,
read with the root's lookup of HID++ feature 0x0003, then its getDeviceInfo
and getFwInfo, once at each attach; null for a model without that feature,
which is sent the lookup alone, for one that names no running main
application, and for one that refused the read, answered it short or did not
answer it, which the journal says once.
.TP
\fBmouse\-select\fR \fImodel\fR
Choose which attached mouse
.BR mouse ,
.BR mouse\-dpi ,
.BR mouse\-bind ,
.BR mouse\-factory\-bindings ,
.BR mouse\-color ,
.B mouse\-effect
and the onboard memory commands act on without
.BR \-\-mouse .
.I model
names a mouse as
.B \-\-mouse
does. Every attached mouse is driven whichever is selected; this decides
only which one the commands mean and the window shows, and whose DPI stages
the tray menu lists. A pick on the window's switcher is the same choice. The
choice is kept in
.I config.json
as
.BR mouse_selected :
after a restart the daemon selects that mouse again once it attaches, and
until then, or with no choice kept, the first mouse to attach. While the
chosen mouse is away, the first attached mouse takes its place, until the
chosen one is back, on its receiver or its cable.
.TP
\fBmouse\-dpi\fR \fIvalue\fR [\fB\-\-mouse\fR \fImodel\fR]
Set the DPI live over HID++ 0x2201, or 0x2202 on a mouse that has it in its
place, clamped to the sensor's range and snapped to its step, which can change
from one part of the range to the next; a mouse that reports no range takes 1
to 65534 and refuses
any other value. A mouse with 0x2202 takes a DPI in host mode alone, and
refuses one outside it, which the refusal says. The applied value is kept in
.I config.json
as
.BR mouse_dpi ,
per mouse model, and put back when that mouse attaches or reports being
switched on: a mouse comes back from a power cycle on the onboard stage it
was last on (in host mode, on the profile's default stage), not necessarily
this value. A stage a mouse's own DPI button moves it to, while the mouse runs
profile 1 outside host mode, as the G305 always does, is kept the same way.
A write that changes the stage list, with
.B mouse\-dpi\-stages
or
.BR mouse\-restore ,
replaces the kept value with the DPI the mouse is on after the write: the new
value when the mouse was on the edited stage, where in onboard mode the write
moves the mouse and in host mode the daemon does, without the stage's flash.
With the DPI shift held, the kept value becomes the new list's DPI at
the stage the mouse was on before the shift, which the release puts back.
The kept value is forgotten if the daemon cannot read or move the DPI after
the write, if the mouse has stopped answering by then, or if the value to keep
is outside 1 to 65534. While a loaded game profile has set the mouse's DPI, the
kept value instead follows the edit with nothing read, and is forgotten where
the new list ends before its place. With no value
kept, such a write stores nothing, and the mouse stays on its own stage. Under
a loaded game profile's own stages in host mode, the value is that profile's,
kept in memory only, and
.B mouse_dpi
keeps the DPI outside them. It is put back after a power cycle while the
profile stays loaded, and at the profile's next load before the daemon stops
if it is still one of the profile's stages. Over 0x2201, a value that is one of
the stages in effect sends its stage number, which on the G502 X PLUS flashes
the stage's color; 0x2202's write carries no stage number.
.TP
\fBmouse\-bind\fR \fIbutton\fR \fIbinding\fR [\fB\-\-g\-shift\fR] [\fB\-\-mouse\fR \fImodel\fR]
Bind a button of the selected mouse in the active M profile and save it, for
that mouse's model alone: a binding on the G502 X PLUS's G4 leaves another
model's G4 as it was. With
.BR \-\-mouse ,
it is for that model, connected or not. With no mouse connected, it is for
the mouse chosen with
.BR mouse\-select ,
kept across a restart, or else the first connected since the daemon started;
with neither, it is refused.
.I button
is one of the selected mouse's buttons, G4 to G9 in any case, as
.B mouse
lists them under the mouse's own names. On the G502 X PLUS they are G4 (side,
rear), G5 (side, front), G6 (paddle), G7 (DPI down), G8 (DPI up) and G9
(behind the wheel); a button the model lacks is refused with its buttons
named.
.I binding
is
.BR back ,
.BR forward ,
.BR dpi\-shift ,
.BR dpi\-up ,
.BR dpi\-down ,
.B dpi\-cycle
(up through the stages, and from the last back to the first, as the PRO
WIRELESS's DPI button does from the factory),
.B g\-shift
(G-Shift held while the button is: every keyboard's G keys, every mouse's
buttons and a headset's G keys send their G-Shift layer's bindings, and the MR
LED of each keyboard that has one is lit; refused with
.BR \-\-g\-shift ,
and on a button with a G-Shift binding until that is cleared),
.B balance\-chat
or
.B balance\-game
(each press moves Outputs' balance one stop, as with
.BR bind ),
a key spec, held for as long as the button is,
.BI macro: name \fR,
or
.BR none ,
.B clear
or
.B off
to clear it, as with
.BR bind .
The actions match in any case, so
.B Back
is the mouse's back;
.B key_back
binds the Back key.
A screen lock, GNOME's screen blank, a suspend, or another account or a text console
taking the screen lets go of a held key,
.BR back ,
.B forward
or
.BR g\-shift ,
as it does a G key's. A
.BI macro: name
whose name runs over 1,000 characters or holds a control character is
refused, as with
.BR bind .
With a game profile loaded this writes to that game profile instead.
.B \-\-g\-shift
binds the button on the mouse model's G-Shift layer, used while MR or a key
or button bound to
.B g\-shift
is held; a button with no binding there sends its base one.
With
.BR \-\-g\-shift ,
.B clear
or
.B off
takes its binding there away, and
.B none
binds it to nothing there. A button bound to
.B g\-shift
takes no binding there.
.TP
\fBmouse\-factory\-bindings\fR [\fB\-\-mouse\fR \fImodel\fR] (or \fBmouse\-defaults\fR)
Put the selected mouse's buttons in the active M profile back to their
factory bindings: each button's factory job, or none where Gearbench has no
such action, as for a G9 that switches onboard profiles. On the G502 X PLUS
they are back, forward, dpi\-shift, dpi\-down, dpi\-up and none. The other
M profiles and the other mouse models keep theirs, and so does the G-Shift
layer. It touches no onboard
memory. With a game profile loaded it writes to that game profile instead.
.TP
\fBmouse\-color\fR \fI#RRGGBB\fR [\fB\-\-mouse\fR \fImodel\fR]
Set the mouse's color on every cluster that has a static effect, then, on a
mouse with per-key lighting (0x8081) such as the G502 X PLUS, a per-key fill.
.I #RRGGBB
is six hex digits, as with
.BR color .
Refused while
.B mouse\-sync
is on, and on a mouse without lights, such as the G305. The daemon keeps the
color while it runs and puts it back after a power cycle; it is not saved.
.TP
\fBmouse\-effect\fR \fIname\fR [\fB\-\-color\fR \fI#RRGGBB\fR] | \fB\-\-release\fR [\fB\-\-mouse\fR \fImodel\fR]
Set a zone effect on whichever mouse clusters have it.
.I name
is any effect
.B gearbench\-ctl mouse
lists: those the mouse reports that Gearbench has a name for.
.B \-\-color
defaults to the color the mouse was last sent, as the keyboard's
.B effect
keeps its color, or
.B '#0080ff'
where it was sent none;
an effect that takes no color, such as cycle or wave, leaves it unused.
Refused while
.B mouse\-sync
is on, on a mouse without lights, and for an effect no cluster has. It is
kept as the color is.
.B \-\-release
gives its own lighting back to a mouse that claims its lighting only once it
has a color to write, and forgets the color and effect set for it, as
.B "mouse\-sync off"
does with none set; it takes no
.I name
or
.BR \-\-color .
It is refused while
.B mouse\-sync
is on, on a mouse without lights, and for a mouse claimed at its setup, such
as the G502 X PLUS.
.TP
\fBmouse\-sync\fR \fBon\fR | \fBoff\fR
On: the mouse follows the keyboard's lighting:
the keyboard's color, the one
.B color
sets (black under
.BR disabled ),
live previews included, scaled by the keyboard's
.BR brightness ,
since the mouse has no brightness control, or whole while a G213 Prodigy
leads, which has no brightness Gearbench can set;
.BR lock\-lights ;
and the
.B idle
dimming while nothing on the desktop is touched. While a game profile with a
mouse color of its own is loaded (see
.BR save\-profile ),
the mouse shows that color in its place, at the keyboard's brightness, and a
change to the keyboard's color leaves the mouse as it is. Its own color and
effect commands are refused, and
.B "mouse\-sync on"
while it is on sends the color only to a mouse not known to show it. A mouse
with no lighting Gearbench drives is
left as it is, and
.B "mouse\-sync on"
names it. Off: each mouse goes back to its own color or
effect, the one last set with
.B mouse\-color
or
.BR mouse\-effect ;
one with none keeps the color it followed (the keyboard's, or a loaded game
profile's mouse color) at the keyboard's brightness as its own, and goes back
to that color at every later switch off while the daemon runs.
A mouse with no color or effect of its own that does not follow the keyboard would stay dark
after a DPI flash, so once the flash is over it takes the keyboard's color at
the keyboard's brightness. A G502 HERO, G502 LIGHTSPEED, PRO WIRELESS,
G903 LIGHTSPEED HERO or G203 LIGHTSYNC with none takes no color in either
case and shows its own lighting. Saved to
.IR config.json .
.TP
\fBsync\-keyboard\fR \fImodel\fR
The keyboard model that leads, by its name or its id, as
.B G915
or
.BR c33e ;
or
.B first
for the one connected first since the daemon started of those still connected,
the default.
Each other keyboard connected whose follow switch is on follows the leader,
and so does a mouse while
.B mouse\-sync
is on and a headset's lights while
.B headset\-sync
is on; a keyboard with no lighting of its own in a profile, or no
brightness of its own, starts from another model's, the leader's first (see
.BR Keyboard ).
The
model chosen leads while it is connected; else the one connected first
leads while it is connected, else another connected. With none connected,
the model chosen leads, else the one connected first, and the mouse shows
its lighting and brightness. Each keyboard connected that the choice
changes, as one that now follows another, is woken and painted at once, the
leader first; nothing is handed back, and one that is away or not answering
is painted when it is back. A model Gearbench does not drive is refused,
with the models it does. Saved to
.I config.json
as
.BR sync_keyboard .
.TP
\fBkeyboard\-follow\fR \fBon\fR | \fBoff\fR \fB\-\-keyboard\fR \fImodel\fR
The follow switch of the keyboard model named, by its name or its id. On,
the default: while another keyboard leads (see
.BR sync\-keyboard ),
it shows the leader's lighting for the layer in effect, cut to what it can
show, at the leader's brightness. Off: it shows its own lighting at its own
brightness, which the leader's changes leave alone unless it has none of its
own and shows a copy of the leader's (see
.BR Keyboard ).
A keyboard of that model
connected is woken and painted where the switch changes what it shows.
Saved to
.I config.json
as
.BR follow_leader ,
for a model not plugged in too.
.TP
\fBdpi\-notices\fR \fBon\fR | \fBoff\fR
On, the default: when a mouse button bound to
.BR dpi\-up ,
.B dpi\-down
or
.B dpi\-cycle
steps the DPI, G8 and G7 on the G502 X PLUS unless rebound, or a mouse's own
DPI button, such as the G305's, moves it to another stage of onboard profile 1,
a desktop notice
says the new value, like
.BR "G502 X PLUS: 1600 DPI" .
A step within 1.5 seconds of the last replaces the notice in place; a later
one closes it and posts a new one.
The notice is posted as transient, and the daemon takes it down 5 seconds
after the last step. A DPI set with
.BR mouse\-dpi ,
from the window or from the tray menu, the DPI shift and the DPI put
back when a mouse is switched on post none. Off: no notice. Saved to
.IR config.json .
Needs python3\-gi and a notification server. The switch for Gearbench in
GNOME Settings, Notifications, silences these together with the low-battery
notices, the custom assignments notice and the notice a refused tray menu
choice posts.
.TP
\fBmouse\-host\fR \fBon\fR | \fBoff\fR
On, the default: while
the daemon runs, a G502 X PLUS, on its receiver or its cable, is in host mode,
where no onboard profile runs: its button spy (0x8110) is armed, and G4 to G9
reach the daemon as the spy's masks, with no onboard profile write; the DPI stages and report rate are
profile 1's, or a loaded game profile's own, and the rate is sent live. A move
between its receiver and its cable while the daemon runs arms it again on the
new route. The G502 HERO and every mouse not yet tested are in host mode the
same way, each spy armed only when it counts as many
controls as the model's onboard button table holds; their event nodes are
not read, so with the switch off their buttons do what their onboard profile
holds.
A mouse whose lookup of its spy was refused or found no 0x8110, or that has
no 0x8100, stays in onboard mode. When the daemon stops, a mouse that answers goes back to onboard
mode on profile 1 (a mouse not yet tested, or one whose profile 1 fails its
CRC, on the profile it runs) at its remembered DPI or the one it had; a mouse
that takes a DPI in host mode alone runs at the DPI its onboard profile sets.
One that does not answer is left in host mode, its programmable buttons and
any wheel tilts dead until its next power cycle or the daemon's next start.
A mouse that
refuses host mode runs profile 1 as with the switch off (or, for a mouse not yet tested,
one whose profile 1 fails its CRC, or if the switch to profile 1 does not take, the
profile it was on), and
.B status
says why under problems. Off: a G502 X PLUS or G502 HERO runs onboard profile 1
all the time, and the G502 X PLUS's G4 to G9 need
.BR mouse\-enable\-buttons ;
a mouse not yet tested keeps the profile it runs.
A mouse off or out of range when the switch is turned off stays in host mode
until it is back, and goes to onboard mode then, unless the switch is on
again by then. The G305 runs profile 1 either way. Saved to
.IR config.json .
In host mode a wheel tilt reaches the daemon only through the spy, and the
daemon scrolls sideways for it on its virtual mouse, a notch at the tilt and
one every 100 ms while held, as the mouse does by itself otherwise.
.SS Mouse onboard memory
These five rewrite the mouse's onboard profile 1; with a game profile loaded
and
.B mouse\-host
on, on a mouse with a button spy (see
.BR mouse\-rate ),
.B mouse\-rate
and
.B mouse\-dpi\-stages
save that profile's own instead and write nothing to the mouse's onboard
memory. Each write
backs up the profile directory and every profile sector to
.I ~/.config/gearbench/backup/
first, and reads the sector back byte for byte after writing. The four that
edit refuse to write when the sector, read now, fails its CRC;
.B mouse\-restore
writes over it. In onboard mode a written profile takes effect at the write's
commit, with no power cycle. A mouse runs whichever of its onboard
profiles is current, and G HUB can leave the G502 X PLUS on profile 2; the
daemon switches it back to profile 1 at connect and after each power-on, with
0x8100 setCurrentProfile, which writes nothing to onboard memory, and puts
the remembered DPI back after it. Each reply's note says a write is in
effect, naming the mouse it wrote:
.RB \(dq "Written to onboard profile 1 on the G502 X PLUS; it is in effect now." \(dq
On a mouse last read running another onboard profile, it says instead that
the write is not in effect until the mouse runs profile 1. In host mode,
where no profile runs, it says instead that Gearbench uses profile 1's DPI
stages and report rate now, except those a loaded game profile has of its
own, which it names, and that the mouse runs profile 1 when Gearbench stops.
After
.B mouse\-enable\-buttons
or
.B mouse\-fallback\-buttons
in host mode, unless the mouse is stuck there, it says instead what G4 to G9
do now and once Gearbench stops. On a mouse stuck in host mode, which runs no
profile, it says the write is not in effect until the mouse runs profile 1.
In host mode it also says if the mouse stopped answering after the write, so
the report rate is sent once it answers, or if the live send of the rate
failed; the rate is sent again at the mouse's next setup.
.TP
\fBmouse\-rate\fR \fIvalue\fR | \fB\-\-remove\-own\fR [\fB\-\-mouse\fR \fImodel\fR]
Set the report rate in Hz.
.I value
is 1000, 500, 250 or 125. Sent live, it goes over 0x8060, or over 0x8061 on a
mouse that has 0x8061 in place of 0x8060. 0x8061 lists rates up to 8000 Hz; Gearbench sets and
offers only these four, and reads the rate in use whichever it is. On a mouse the daemon runs in host mode, or one it
could not switch back to onboard mode, it writes profile 1 and then sends the
rate live. On any other mouse in host mode, such as one another program left
there whose switch to onboard mode at connect did not take, it falls back to a live rate write, which takes no
backup and verifies nothing; the reply says which happened. On a mouse in
host mode whose onboard profile is not written, a mouse not yet tested, it sends the
rate live alone, stores nothing on the mouse, and keeps it for the model in
.IR config.json 's
.BR mouse_rate ,
sent again at each setup in host mode: after a power-on or a replug, when
host mode is turned on, and at the next start;
the reply's
.B written
is
.BR live ,
and its note says so. Outside host mode such a mouse refuses it with the other
onboard profile writes.
Unless it is saved as a game profile's own (below), a rate the mouse does not
list is refused before anything is written, and one it refuses live is refused
where the rate is sent live alone; where profile 1 is written first, the write
stands and its note says the live send failed (above).
With a game profile loaded and
.B mouse\-host
on, on a mouse with a button spy (any model but the G305, unless the lookup of its
spy was refused or found no 0x8110, or it has no 0x8100), it is saved as that profile's own rate for this mouse
model and sent live while the mouse is in host mode; profile 1 is not
written, and the reply's
.B written
is
.BR game_profile .
Its note says when the rate is in effect: now, once the mouse answers, once
it is in host mode where host mode did not take, or not, where the mouse
refused it; a refused one is sent again at the mouse's next setup or the
profile's next load. The same goes for
.BR mouse\-dpi\-stages .
Where the mouse took the rate and refused the DPI for the stages in effect,
the note says the rate is in effect now and that the DPI is sent again at the
mouse's next setup or the profile's next load.
.B \-\-remove\-own
(\fB\-\-reset\fR, its name in Gearbench 1.1.0)
drops the loaded game profile's own rate, and profile 1's is used again, or
on a mouse not yet tested with a rate kept for its model, that rate; with
no game profile loaded, on a mouse without a button spy or with
.B mouse\-host
off, it is refused.
.TP
\fBmouse\-dpi\-stages\fR \fIstages\fR [\fB\-\-default\fR \fIdpi\fR] [\fB\-\-shift\fR \fIdpi\fR] | \fB\-\-remove\-own\fR | \fB\-\-factory\fR [\fB\-\-mouse\fR \fImodel\fR]
Write a new DPI stage list.
.I stages
is one to five DPI values, comma separated, with space around a comma
ignored; more than five, or stages separated only by spaces, is refused before
anything is sent. A stage outside the sensor's range is refused, naming the
range, and one inside is snapped to its step, as
.B mouse\-dpi
snaps; they must stay distinct. They are written lowest first, in whatever order
given.
.B \-\-default
is the onboard profile's default stage and
.B \-\-shift
the stage the DPI shift goes to while held; both take DPI values, not
indices, snapped as
the stages are and refused outside the range, and a value the list does not
hold is refused. A value under 100, which no supported sensor takes, is
refused before anything is sent
.RB ( "give a DPI, not a stage number" ).
Leave either out and it stays on its value
if the new list holds it, else on its position in the list as given, taking the
value now there, or the last stage if the list is shorter. The mouse
starts on the default stage when it switches to profile 1, as when the daemon
switches it back from another profile, and in host mode at a power cycle.
When the list
changes, the reply's
.B dpi
is the DPI the mouse is on after the write, left out where the daemon cannot
read or move it then or the mouse has stopped answering (see
.BR mouse\-dpi ).
When the list is a game profile's own (below), it is there only if the mouse
is in host mode and the edit set its DPI.
.IP
With a game profile loaded and
.B mouse\-host
on, on a mouse with a button spy (see
.BR mouse\-rate ),
the list is saved as that profile's own for this mouse
model, in its file, in effect while the mouse is in host mode, and profile 1 is
not written. The list it starts from is the profile's own, or profile 1's when it
has none. The DPI buttons and the DPI shift use the profile's own stages while
they are in effect, and profile 1's otherwise. When the game profile loads,
the DPI goes back to the one last chosen under them (see
.BR mouse\-dpi ),
else to its default stage; at its unload the
DPI goes back to where it was before, none of these with the stage's flash.
An edit leaves the mouse on its DPI while the new list still holds it. When
the stage it is on changes value or is removed, it goes to the stage at the same place in the
list, and it stays on its DPI where the new list ends before that place, unless
the mouse has not taken the profile's stages and rate since the profile loaded:
then the edit moves it as a load does. With the DPI shift held,
letting go puts the mouse on the DPI it had before the shift, followed through
the edit the same way, as on profile 1.
.B \-\-remove\-own
(\fB\-\-reset\fR, its name in Gearbench 1.1.0)
drops the loaded game profile's own stages, and profile 1's are used again,
the mouse staying on its DPI where profile 1's list holds it, else going to the
stage at its place there, or staying as it is where that list ends before it;
with no game profile loaded it is refused.
.B \-\-factory
writes the stages, default and shift of the mouse's factory profile, read
from its ROM copy of profile 1, which nothing writes, where a list would go:
to profile 1, or to the loaded game profile's own. On the G502 X PLUS that is
800, 1200, 1600, 2400 and 3200 DPI, default 1600, shift 800. On a mouse without a
button spy or with
.B mouse\-host
off, a game profile's own stages and rate do not apply: this goes on as with
no profile loaded, writing profile 1 where Gearbench writes the model's
onboard profile, and
.B \-\-remove\-own
is refused.
.TP
\fBmouse\-enable\-buttons\fR [\fB\-\-mouse\fR \fImodel\fR]
Rewrite onboard profile 1 so G4 to G9 send mouse buttons 11 to 16, which arrive
on the mouse's evdev node as key codes 0x11a to 0x11f. From the factory G4
and G5 send back and forward, which the daemon does not read, and G6 to G9 are
bound to firmware functions and emit nothing, so with host mode off none of
the six can be bound without this; in host mode, unless the mouse is stuck
there, they reach the daemon through the button spy either way. G502 X PLUS
only: on the G305, which has no button table, and on a model whose onboard
profile writes are not tested on this hardware, it is refused and nothing is
sent. It is refused too, with nothing sent, on a model whose event node
Gearbench does not read: its buttons reach the daemon in host mode alone, so,
enabled, they would do nothing outside host mode.
.TP
\fBmouse\-fallback\-buttons\fR [\fB\-\-mouse\fR \fImodel\fR]
Rewrite onboard profile 1 so G4 to G8 work without Gearbench, at the login
screen or on another system: G4
and G5 back and forward, G6 the DPI shift, G7 and G8 DPI down and up, as from
the factory, and G9 nothing, where from the factory it cycles the onboard
profiles. In host mode, unless the mouse is stuck there, G4 to G9 still reach
the daemon through the button spy and keep their bindings. In onboard mode, with
.B mouse\-host
off or where host mode did not take, they do these things themselves and
their bindings do not apply;
.B mouse\-enable\-buttons
gives them back on the G502 X PLUS, and host mode
.RB ( "mouse\-host on" )
on the G502 HERO, which refuses that write. G502 X PLUS and G502 HERO only:
on the G305, which has no button table, and on a model whose onboard profile
writes are not tested on this hardware, it is refused and nothing is sent.
.TP
\fBmouse\-restore\fR [\fB\-\-mouse\fR \fImodel\fR]
Write onboard profile 1 back from the newest
.I .bin
backup of sector 0x0001 for the mouse's model, whichever mouse of that model
wrote it. It writes over a sector whose CRC fails, the case it is
for, and never writes a backup whose own CRC fails.
.B RECOVERY
below covers a write or a restore that fails.
.SS Headset
The commands from
.B headset
to
.B headset\-revert
are for the Astro A50 Gen 4's base station, in PC mode (USB 9886:002c).
Those from
.B g432\-sidetone
to
.B g432\-eq
are for the G432, through its USB adapter (USB 046d:0a9c), and the ten other
headsets of its family, the G431, G433, G533, G535, G633, G635, G733, G933,
G935 and PRO X Wireless (their ids are listed under
.BR g432\-sidetone ).
Those from
.B audio
to
.B audio\-presets
are for the Astro A50 X (USB 046d:0b0b) or A50 Gen 5 (USB 046d:0b1c), through
its base station, and the PRO X 2, G522 and G325 LIGHTSPEED, through their
receiver, the G522 on its cable too.
.B headset\-spatial
plays to the A50 Gen 4, to the Logitech G432 and to the other headsets it
names.
.BR headset\-outputs ,
.B headset\-volume
and
.B headset\-noise
take the same headsets. Of them,
.B headset\-noise
refuses the PRO X 2 and G325 LIGHTSPEED, whose noise suppression is in the
headset.
.PP
A change to the A50 Gen 4's
base station goes to its memory and is in effect at once. Its
flash, which it starts from after a power cycle, is written only by
.BR headset\-save :
a save takes over two seconds and the flash's wear limit is not known. Every
value is checked before it is sent, since the base station clamps
nothing; one out of range is refused and nothing is sent.
.TP
\fBheadset\fR [\fB\-\-read\fR]
Show the headset's state and settings, each on a line of its own.
The device line gives the base station's node.
The headset line says the headset is
.BR on " or " "switched off or out of range" ,
or
.BR "on, docked" " or " "switched off, docked"
while it sits on its base station.
While the base station is not answering, it says so, and either when its settings
were last read in full or that they have not all been read yet.
The battery shows once the headset has been on since the daemon connected to the
base station.
While the headset is off or the base station is not answering, it shows the time
of the reading, with the date before the time for a reading from an earlier day.
The firmware line gives the base station's version and, once the headset has been
on, the headset's, each where the base station gave one.
While the headset is on, the balance line gives the Game and Voice balance its
buttons set, as its number of 255 and then Voice's and Game's shares, the same way as
.BR default\-balance .
The settings follow in the order of the window's Headset tab: the microphone's
settings, the active equalizer preset, each preset's name and five bands, each with
its frequency, gain and width ("shelf" for bands 1 and 5), then the stream mix's
settings and the base station's.
Each setting is named as
.B headset\-set
takes it, except
.BR auto\-shut\-off ,
which shows as
.BR "auto shut\-off" .
Where the flash holds another value, the setting, preset name or band is followed by
.RI "\(dq(saved: " value )\(dq.
A difference that reads the same, such as two width words that both read as 1.7,
shows only on the unsaved line.
Until the daemon has read every setting once, a setting it has not read shows as
.BR "not read" .
The unsaved line gives what is not saved to flash, or that the settings have not
been read yet, and the save line a save in progress or the last one's error.
A setting the base station has not given once every
setting has been read, or a preset it refused to report, is left out. It asks the base station whether the headset is on and,
while it is, the balance and the active preset, as the daemon does every five
seconds; while the headset is on it also asks for the battery when its reading
is a minute old or the headset was docked or lifted off its base station since;
.B \-\-read
has every setting and preset read again first.
While a save runs or the base station is not answering it asks nothing,
.B \-\-read
included, and shows what the daemon last read.
.TP
\fBheadset\-set\fR \fIsetting\fR \fIvalue\fR
Change one setting in the base station's memory and show it as read back.
.I setting
is one of
.B mic\-level
and
.B sidetone
(0 to 100);
.BR stream\-game ,
.B stream\-voice
(\fBstream\-chat\fR, its name in Gearbench 1.2.1),
.B stream\-mic
and
.BR stream\-aux ,
the stream mix's Game, Voice, Mic and Aux (0 to 100);
.B noise\-gate
.RB ( streaming ,
.BR night ,
.B home
or
.BR tournament );
.B mic\-eq
.RB ( bright ,
.B airy
or
.BR warm );
.B default\-balance
(0, all Voice, to 255, all Game);
.B alert\-volume
(0 to 100);
.B auto\-shut\-off
or
.B auto\-off
(10, 15, 20, 25 or 30 minutes); or
.B preset
(1, 2 or 3), which switches the active equalizer preset. The preset is shown as sent,
with its name as last read: for a moment after a switch the base station still
reports the old one.
.TP
\fBheadset\-eq\fR [\fB\-\-slot\fR \fIn\fR] [\fB\-\-name\fR \fIname\fR] [\fB\-\-gains\fR \fIg1,g2,g3,g4,g5\fR] [\fB\-\-band\fR \fIband\fR:\fIfreq\fR[:\fIwidth\fR]]... [\fB\-\-reset\fR] [\fB\-\-save\-as\fR [\fIname\fR]]
Change an equalizer preset, the active slot's unless
.B \-\-slot
(\fB\-\-preset\fR, its name in Gearbench 1.1.0) names another, 1, 2 or 3, and
show it as read back.
.B \-\-name
is 1 to 49 bytes of UTF-8, not spaces alone, with no control character; the
command refuses any
other before anything is sent.
.B \-\-gains
is the five bands' gains, whole dB from \-7 to +7.
.B \-\-band
sets one band's frequency, 80 to 15000 Hz. Bands 2, 3 and 4 also need a
width, 0.1 to 3.0, which the daemon rounds to a tenth, as fine as Gearbench
reads a width back; bands 1 and 5 are shelves and take no width. It can be
given once for each band.
.B \-\-reset
first puts the slot's default back, its name, gains and all five bands:
Media in slot 1, Gaming in 2 and Everyday in 3, Gearbench's
own presets, designed from published measurements of the A50; the other
options then change it. Without
.BR \-\-slot ,
the daemon is asked first which slot is active. Like any change it is
not saved until
.BR headset\-save .
.B \-\-save\-as
\fIname\fR, after the rest, saves the preset, as the base station reads it
back, into the library Gearbench keeps (see
.BR headset\-library ),
as \fIname\fR, or under its own name when \fIname\fR is left out; alone, it
sends the preset nothing and saves it as the daemon last read it. A preset
not read whole, or one with a gain or band a library preset cannot take, is
refused, and so is one whose own name it cannot take when \fIname\fR is left
out. With
.BR \-\-json ,
it prints the daemon's reply and the library; where the preset was changed
and the save does not go through, it prints the reply alone, with exit
status 1. At least one of
.BR \-\-name ,
.BR \-\-gains ,
.BR \-\-band ,
.B \-\-reset
or
.B \-\-save\-as
is needed, and all of them, the library's file included, are checked before
the first is written.
.TP
\fBheadset\-import\fR \fIfile\fR [\fB\-\-slot\fR \fIn\fR|\fB\-\-library\fR]
Load an Astro Command Center preset file,
.IR .astroeq ,
into an equalizer preset, the active slot's unless
.B \-\-slot
(\fB\-\-preset\fR, its name in Gearbench 1.1.0) names another: its name, its
five gains and all five bands.
.B \-\-library
adds it to the library after its last preset in place of sending it, and
needs no daemon. The command reads
the file itself, in either the Windows or the Mac form, and refuses one that
does not parse or holds a value out of range before anything is sent. It
also refuses a path that is not a regular file, such as a named pipe or a
device, a file over 16 KiB, and a
.B Name
that holds a control character, such as one written
.B \ex1b
in the file.
A line or paragraph separator (U+2028, U+2029) or a character that turns
the direction of the text after it (U+202A to U+202E, U+2066 to U+2069) in
the name becomes U+FFFD, except a separator written as itself, outside quotes,
at the start or end, which is trimmed with the white space there.
.TP
\fBheadset\-library\fR [\fInumber\fR] [\fB\-\-from\-slot\fR [\fIslot\fR]] [\fB\-\-gains\fR \fIg1,g2,g3,g4,g5\fR] [\fB\-\-band\fR \fIband\fR:\fIfreq\fR[:\fIwidth\fR]]... [\fB\-\-name\fR \fIname\fR] [\fB\-\-write\fR \fIslot\fR] [\fB\-\-delete\fR] [\fB\-\-save\-as\fR [\fIname\fR]]
Show or change the A50's preset library, up to 16 presets Gearbench keeps
in
.IR a50\-eq.json ,
apart from the base station's three slots, each a name, five gains and five
bands as the base station takes them. Alone it lists them by number, each as
.B headset
prints a slot; with a \fInumber\fR it shows that one (with \fB\-\-json\fR,
every preset), after the options
change it in this order:
.B \-\-from\-slot
takes the gains and bands of slot 1, 2 or 3 as the daemon last read them,
the active slot when \fIslot\fR is left out, and keeps the name;
.B \-\-gains
and
.B \-\-band
change them as
.B headset\-eq
takes them; and
.B \-\-name
renames it.
.BR \-\-delete ,
which takes no other option but \fB\-\-json\fR, deletes it, those after it
a number lower,
and lists the rest; a slot it was written to keeps it.
.B \-\-save\-as
\fIname\fR, which takes no \fInumber\fR and no other option but
.B \-\-from\-slot
and \fB\-\-json\fR, saves the slot
.B \-\-from\-slot
names, else the active one, as the daemon last read it, as a new preset after
the last, named \fIname\fR or its own name when \fIname\fR is left out, and
shows it, as
.B headset\-eq \-\-save\-as
alone does. Everything given is
checked, the slot read, before anything is saved.
.B \-\-write
\fIslot\fR then writes the preset to slot 1, 2 or 3 of the base station's
memory, its name, gains and all five bands, and shows the slot as read back
in the preset's place (with \fB\-\-json\fR, the daemon's reply), unsaved until
.BR headset\-save .
It asks the daemon only for
.BR \-\-from\-slot ,
.B \-\-save\-as
and
.BR \-\-write .
A library file that cannot be read or is of another version is refused,
and never written over. One with presets left out (see FILES) is listed, with
a
.B warning:
line on standard error of what was left out, and refuses every change to it.
.TP
.B headset\-save
Save every setting and equalizer preset to the base station's flash. The save runs in
the daemon, which sends it once, and once more when the base station asks for
a retry;
.B headset
shows it in progress, then what is still not saved, or why the save failed.
A change sent while it runs is refused.
.TP
.B headset\-revert
Put every setting and equalizer preset that differs back to what the flash holds.
Which preset is active stays as it is.
The flash's value for
.B auto\-shut\-off
cannot be read, so a change to it since the
last save stays. The reply's note says so when the daemon sent that change
since it last connected to the base station; after a daemon restart, a
change made before it gets no note. A value the
base station refuses, one whose answer Gearbench cannot read, or one
Gearbench refuses before sending it counts as refused. So does a flash value
Gearbench cannot write back as stored (a band width off the tenths it reads,
a width on band 1 or 5, or a name that is not UTF-8), which it does not send.
A refused value is named in the note unless reading everything again finds
it back at its flash value; every other value that differs goes back. Anything
that still differs from the flash afterward, a refused value included, is
named on standard error (with \fB\-\-json\fR, in the reply's \fBunsaved\fR),
and the exit status is 1. A refused value that could not be read again is in
no such list: it too makes the exit status 1, and standard error names it
when nothing still differs. With \fB\-\-json\fR, the reply's
.B not_reverted
lists each refused value the note names. A change to
.B auto\-shut\-off
alone leaves the exit status 0.
.TP
\fBheadset\-spatial\fR [\fBon\fR|\fBoff\fR] [\fB\-\-headset\fR \fIkey\fR|\fBfirst\fR] [\fB\-\-head\fR \fBku100\fR|\fBkemar\fR] [\fB\-\-room\fR \fBcompetitive\fR|\fBimmersive\fR]
Show or set Gearbench Spatial, a 7.1 output rendered for headphones, for games
that send 5.1 or 7.1. It plays on the Astro A50 Gen 4's or the Astro A50 X's
Game output, on the Logitech G432 or another headset of its family, or on the
first output of an Astro A50 Gen 5, a PRO X 2 LIGHTSPEED, a G522 LIGHTSPEED or
a G325 LIGHTSPEED, taken to be the A50 Gen 5's Game output and the others'
only one until one is tested. It
sends nothing to a headset and needs no daemon: it runs
.BR systemctl ,
.B pw\-dump
and
.B pw\-cli
itself. The report is a line each: its state; the output's name, with its node
name in parentheses; the headset picked; the head; the room, and the room
running where that differs; the games playing into it, each with its channel
count where PipeWire gives it as a whole number; what to do about one of them
that sends 2 channels, and about a game whose launch option still names
.BR gearbench\-a50\-spatial ;
the Steam launch option;
and, for the headset it plays to, whether the A50 Gen 4's or the A50 X's Game and
Chat show as two outputs, whether the G432, or the headset of its family it
plays to, shows one analog output, or whether the A50 Gen 5, PRO X 2, G522 or
G325 is on a profile set other than PipeWire's default.
While no headset is there to play to, that line names an A50 Gen 4 or A50 X plugged
in on a profile set other than the one Gearbench's rule gives it, with no Game
output, which is why the chain waits. A line for a card on another set ends
with the WirePlumber restart that puts the rule's set in place: always on the
A50 Gen 4 and the A50 X, and on the G432's family while that set left the chain
nothing to play to. On the G432's family it ends with
.B until WirePlumber restarts
while the output it plays to is still there, as the G432's analog output
under PipeWire's default set; on the A50 Gen 5, PRO X 2, G522 and G325 it
says the set comes from a rule outside Gearbench.
The state names the output it plays to while it is linked and nothing plays
into it, and says
.B "in use"
while a game does. A name past 100 characters, a game's or that output's, is
cut to its first 99 and an ellipsis, and the line names the first eight games,
then how many more.
.B on
enables
.I gearbench\-spatial.service
and starts it; from then on it runs from each login, and plays while a
headset's output is in PipeWire.
.B off
stops it and disables it; a
.BR \-\-headset ,
.B \-\-head
or
.B \-\-room
given with it is only saved, for its next start.
.B \-\-headset
is the headset it, and the three outputs of
.BR headset\-outputs ,
play to while more than one is plugged in:
.BR a50 ,
.BR g432 ,
another of the G432's family by its key
.RB ( g431 ,
.BR g433 ,
.BR g533 ,
.BR g535 ,
.BR g633 ,
.BR g635 ,
.BR g733 ,
.BR g933 ,
.BR g935 ,
.BR pro_x_wireless ),
.BR a50x ,
.BR a50gen5 ,
.BR pro_x_2 ,
.BR g522 ,
.BR g325 ,
or
.B first
(the default), the one connected first. With the one picked unplugged they
play to the one connected first of the others. It restarts a running Gearbench
Spatial. On the G432, or another of its family whose equalizer runs on the
computer, it plays through that headset's equalizer, as Logitech G432
Equalizer on the G432, while the equalizer is on, and straight to the headset
otherwise; it plays straight to a G633, G933 or G935, whose equalizer is in
the headset. While Outputs is
on, it plays into Gearbench Game instead, on any headset, and Gearbench Game
plays on that headset, so Game's volume and the balance of
.B headset\-outputs
apply to it too.
.B \-\-head
is the dummy head it is rendered for, from the SADIE II database:
.B ku100
(the default), the Neumann KU100 (subject D1), or
.B kemar
(subject D2). It restarts a running Gearbench Spatial, even for the head it
has. Every headset has the same head and room.
.B \-\-room
is
.B competitive
(the default), direction alone, or
.BR immersive ,
a short, dry room about 11 dB under the sound. The room changes while it
plays, with no restart. The headset picked, the head and the room are saved to
.I spatial.json
and kept while it is off. With
.B \-\-head
or
.BR \-\-headset ,
the room is only saved, and one restart takes what was given; a unit that
failed is not restarted and stays failed until it is switched on. Without
either, and without
.BR off ,
a room set while Gearbench Spatial is starting waits up to 2 seconds for it to
come up and is set then. If the start fails in that wait, the room is taken at
the next start and the command exits 0; if it is still starting after 2
seconds, the command prints that it keeps the room it started with until its
next start and exits 1. One set while it is off, while it waits to start again
after a failure, while no headset is there, or while it runs no chain because
it could not write one, is taken when the chain next starts, with no wait. With
.B on
it then switches on, and the report follows; after
.BR on ,
.B \-\-head
or
.B \-\-headset
it waits up to 2 seconds while the state is starting. A step that fails
prints systemctl's or PipeWire's own error when it prints one, and
Gearbench's words otherwise, as for a program that is not installed or a
.I spatial.json
that cannot be saved, and exits 1, with a line on installing Gearbench when
the unit file is missing. When the room did not change while Gearbench
Spatial runs, the command says so and that the room takes effect at the next
start, with PipeWire's error in parentheses when it printed one, or
.B "pw\-cli exited N"
or
.B "pw\-cli was killed by signal N"
when it failed without one, and exits 1.
When it could not write the chain for a headset that is there, the state
says
.B "on, but no chain runs:"
and why, such as a file the chain needs that is missing, as the unit's
status in systemd gives it;
.BR \-\-json 's
.B status
holds those words, and is null without them. It tries again when the output
it plays to changes, or at its next start, as when it is switched off and on
or restarted by
.B \-\-head
or
.BR \-\-headset .
.IP
In Steam, a game plays on it with the launch option
.BR "\%PULSE_SINK=gearbench\-spatial \%%command%" ;
from another launcher, with
.B \%PULSE_SINK=gearbench\-spatial
in the game's environment. Or pick Gearbench Spatial as your output before
the game starts, and your usual output again after the game quits: until
then everything on the default output plays on Gearbench
Spatial. Set the game's own audio output to surround or System Default.
A launch option that still names
.BR gearbench\-a50\-spatial ,
the A50's output before Gearbench Spatial, names no output now, so the game
plays on the default output until the option names
.BR gearbench\-spatial .
A game started while it is off, while no headset's output is in PipeWire, or
while no chain runs, plays on the default output in stereo, and stays in
stereo until the game is started again. While it plays on the A50, set the
headset's Dolby button to the star, or the base station processes the sound
a second time.
.TP
\fBheadset\-outputs\fR [\fBon\fR|\fBoff\fR|\fBbalance\fR \fIword\fR|\fBapp\fR \fIkey\fR \fIoutput\fR]
Show or set Outputs: Gearbench Game, Gearbench Chat and Gearbench Media, three
outputs for games, voice chat and music that play on the headset Gearbench
Spatial plays to, through its equalizer while that runs. On the Astro A50 Gen 4
and the Astro A50 X, Chat plays on the headset's own Chat output and Game and
Media on its Game output; on every other headset all three play on its
output. When that headset goes away they wait 2 seconds, then play on the
default output you had before Outputs was on, or else on the sound card that
ranks highest, and go back when it returns. With no headset's output there
since the unit started, they play on that output at once. The default output
itself is left alone. It sends nothing to a headset and needs no daemon: it runs
.BR systemctl ,
.B pw\-dump
and
.B pw\-metadata
itself. The report is a line each: its state; where each output plays while
it runs; the balance, from 0, all chat, to 255, all game, as its number of
255, then Gearbench Chat's and Gearbench Game's levels as the window's
Balance row gives them; where the outputs
play while the headset is away; and one line for each program playing, or one
for the programs that share a key, with where it plays and the key
.B app
takes. A name past 100 characters, a program's or an output's, is cut to its
first 99 and an ellipsis, and a line of programs that share a key names the
first eight it shares with, then how many more.
.B on
saves the default output you picked, one unplugged then included, enables
.I gearbench\-outputs.service
and starts it, and makes Gearbench Game the default output; from then on it
runs from each login. With the unit running already, it only enables it.
.B off
gives back the default output you had while one of the three is still the
default output, and leaves one you picked since alone, then stops the unit and
disables it.
One you had that is unplugged then is your pick again after the stop, and
is the default output once it returns; the sound card that ranks highest
plays meanwhile.
One you picked since that is unplugged then stays your pick the same way,
and what was given back plays until then.
Each program keeps the output it was moved to, for its next start. Its report
has no line for the programs, which WirePlumber moves to the default output
seconds after the stop.
A step of
.B on
or
.B off
that fails prints systemctl's or PipeWire's own error when it prints one, and
Gearbench's words otherwise, and exits 1, with a line on installing Gearbench
when the unit file is missing. After a stop that fails, that error is followed
by any note
.B off
had made of the default output by then, such as that the one you had is the
default output again. Where
.B on
or
.B off
moved the default output, or why a move failed, is a
.I note:
line after the report, which
.B \-\-json
gives as
.IR notes ,
and the command exits 0.
.B balance
sends the word to the unit's process:
.B game
or
.B chat
moves the balance one of its 13 stops, and a number from 0 to 255 takes the
stop nearest it; it prints the balance after it. The unit's process sets
Gearbench Game's and Gearbench Chat's gains for it with
.BR "pw\-cli set\-param" :
both at full at 128, the middle stop, Game's lower on each stop toward 0,
where it is silent, and Chat's on each stop toward 255; Media's is always at
full. On an
A50 Gen 4, A50 X or A50 Gen 5 the headset sets its own balance: Game and Chat
play at full, and the word is refused: an error says so, and the command exits
1, as it does while Outputs is off.
.B app
moves each stream of the programs the report lists under
.I key
to
.BR game ,
.BR chat ,
.B media
or
.BR default ,
the default output, which WirePlumber then keeps as their choice, in
.I ~/.local/state/wireplumber/stream\-properties
(under
.B $XDG_STATE_HOME
where that is set); a program that names its output itself is refused, since
only its own settings move it.
A program not seen playing on its new output within 2 seconds, or one whose
move could not be read back from PipeWire, is a
.I note:
line after the report, which
.B \-\-json
gives as
.IR notes ,
and the command exits 0.
.B default
works with Outputs off too, and clears a choice the program keeps for the
next time Outputs is on. At each start of the unit, its process reads that
file, never writing it, and moves each program playing whose kept choice is
Gearbench Game, Chat or Media back there, each move a line in its journal;
it moves no program by itself at any other time.
.TP
\fBheadset\-volume\fR [\fIkey\fR | \fB\-\-headset\fR \fIkey\fR] [\fBgame\fR|\fBchat\fR|\fBmic\fR [\fIlevel\fR]]
Show or set a headset's microphone volume, and the volume of the Game and
Chat outputs that Gearbench's profile sets give the cards of the Astro A50
Gen 4 and the Astro A50 X.
Each volume is shown and set as GNOME's Sound settings show and set it: the
cube root of the loudest channel's volume, in percent. The
.I key
is the headset's, as
.B headset\-spatial \-\-headset
takes it, given first or with
.BR \-\-headset ;
a volume needs a headset, and a level a volume. Each headset has
.BR mic ,
the microphone that the window's Input level reads, and
.B a50
and
.B a50x
have
.B game
and
.B chat
too. It sends nothing to a headset and needs no daemon: it runs
.B pw\-dump
and
.B pw\-cli
itself. Alone it prints a line for each volume of each headset with one in
PipeWire,
.B "a50 game:"
and the rest, with its level or
.BR "not in PipeWire" ,
or
.B "no headset volume in PipeWire"
where none has one; with a headset, each of that headset's; with a volume too, that
one. With a
.IR level ,
0 to 100, it sets that volume first: the loudest channel to the level and the
others in proportion, as GNOME's slider does, its mute left as it is. At 1 to 3%,
the quieter channel of a balance can drop to 0, and it stays at 0 after that. A volume
whose card has a route for it, as PipeWire's own
.I usb\-gaming\-headset.conf
gives each output of the A50 Gen 4, has the route set and saved, as GNOME sets
it; else its
node's volume is set. Then it prints the line read back.
.B \-\-json
prints each level by headset and volume, null for one not in PipeWire. An
output the headset does not have, a level outside 0 to 100, a volume to set
that is not in PipeWire or is there with no volume, a set that fails, and a
pw\-dump that fails print the
error and exit 1.
.TP
\fBheadset\-noise\fR [\fBon\fR|\fBoff\fR|\fBtest\fR] [\fB\-\-headset\fR \fIkey\fR] (or \fBg432\-noise\fR)
Show or set a headset's noise suppression: an input made from the headset's
microphone with PipeWire's own echo\-cancel module and its webrtc plugin, its
noise suppression on and its automatic gain off, the default input while it
is on, G432 Microphone (Noise Filter) on the G432. The headset's own
microphone stays as it is.
Built from what PipeWire documents, heard on the G432 on 2026-10-18 and on
the G933, the Astro A50 Gen 4 and the A50 X on 2026-10-28; not
yet tested in a call.
It sends nothing to the headset and needs no daemon: it runs
.BR systemctl ,
.BR systemd\-run ,
.B pw\-metadata
and
.B pw\-dump
itself. The report is a line each: its state; the input's name, with its
node name in parentheses,
.B gearbench\-g432\-mic
on the G432; and the microphone it cleans; then a
.I note:
line for each of the default input's notes, which
.B \-\-json
gives as
.IR notes .
.B on
writes its PipeWire configuration, aimed at the headset's microphone, starts
it as a transient user unit,
.I gearbench\-g432\-mic.service
on the G432, and waits up to 2 seconds while it starts. It saves the default
input there was, your pick, even while it is unplugged, else the one
WirePlumber picked, to
.I $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/gearbench\-g432\-mic.json
on the G432, then makes the input the default input and waits up to 2 seconds
for WirePlumber to show it; a note after the report says whether it is the
default input and, where it is not, why. It runs until
.BR off ,
the account's last logout (with lingering on, a restart), or PipeWire's
stop; only
.BR off ,
here or in the window, gives the default input back.
While noise suppression runs or starts,
.B on
changes nothing and prints the report alone; otherwise it refuses while the
headset is not plugged in, or while its profile has no microphone
(Configuration in GNOME's Sound settings, Profile in KDE Plasma's).
Noise suppression that stops as it starts is an error, which
names the journalctl command that says why.
.B off
stops noise suppression, which removes the input. Before the stop, while
the input is still the default, it gives back the default input saved at
.BR on ;
one that is unplugged is written as your pick after the stop instead, and
is the default again once it is back. With no input saved, it gives back
the headset's own microphone, and with another input picked since, nothing.
One picked since that is unplugged then is written as your pick after the
stop the same way, and takes the place of the saved input when that is
unplugged too.
A pw\-dump or a pick that fails is a note, and it stops all the same.
A step that fails prints systemctl's,
systemd\-run's or PipeWire's own error when it prints one, and Gearbench's
words otherwise, and exits 1.
.B g432\-noise
is its name in Gearbench 1.2.0 and does the same.
.IP
.B test
counts 3, one a second, then records 5 seconds of the input, once it is on
and linked, and plays them back on the default output. It prints
.I recording NAME in 3
to 1, then
.I recording NAME: 5 s left
to 1 s as the samples come, then that it plays them back: on a terminal
each line takes the place of the one before, and into a pipe or a file each
is a line of its own. It records with
.B pw\-record
into memory, then plays with
.B pw\-play
from an anonymous file in memory, so nothing is written to disk, and says
how much it recorded. It refuses while the input is not on and linked, and
says so where no sound came within 8 seconds or
.B pw\-play
failed or did not end in time. With
.BR \-\-json ,
it counts without a line and prints only the seconds recorded, under
.BR recorded .
.IP
The G432's family, the Astro A50 Gen 4, the A50 X, the A50 Gen 5 and the
G522 LIGHTSPEED have it, each under its own names made from its name and its
key: Astro A50 X Microphone (Noise Filter) and
.I gearbench\-a50x\-mic.service
on the A50 X.
The A50 Gen 5, the G522 and the rest of the G432's
family but the G933 were built with none on hand. The
PRO X 2 LIGHTSPEED and the G325 LIGHTSPEED are refused: their noise
suppression is in the headset (see
.BR audio ).
The microphone is taken to be mono: read off the G432 and the G933, mapped so
by the profile set Gearbench gives the A50 Gen 4 and the A50 X, and assumed
on the others. The A50 Gen 5 and the G522 play under PipeWire's own
profiles, and are refused while their card lists more than one input, each
named, until a device report shows which is the microphone.
.B \-\-headset
names the headset by its key, any that
.B headset\-spatial \-\-headset
takes but
.BR first .
Left out, it takes a headset plugged in: of the G432's family the one
the daemon drives, asked with its
.B g432
request, or, where it drives none or no daemon answers, the first found by
its USB ids in sysfs, the one it would drive, with nothing opened, as
.B g432\-eq
does; then the Astro A50 Gen 4, then the others, one whose noise
suppression is in the headset last; and the G432 with none.
.TP
\fBg432\-sidetone\fR [\fIlevel\fR|\fBput\-back\fR (or \fBreset\fR)]
The Logitech G432's sidetone, through its USB adapter (USB 046d:0a9c): your
voice in the earcups, how loud the microphone is in them. Checked on a G432
on 2026-10-17 and 2026-10-18.
The sidetone may be heard only while a program has the microphone open.
Alone, it reads the
level from the headset and shows it with the level the daemon first read,
under the firmware version the daemon read when the adapter attached
(HID++ feature 0x0003's getDeviceInfo and getFwInfo: the running main
application, such as
.BR "U1 42.00.B0001" ),
a line left out where the adapter lists no 0x0003 or names no running main
application, or the read failed, which the journal says with its cause. A
.I level
is a whole number from 0 to 100, checked before anything is sent; it is
written to the headset and read back.
.B put\-back
(\fBreset\fR, its name in Gearbench 1.1.0)
writes back the level the daemon first read since it started, kept across an
unplug, or 0 when no read found one before the first write. The G432's adapter
kept its level through an unplug when tested on 2026-10-17. A headset that lists
no sidetone is refused with that reason.
.IP
It takes the other headsets of the G432's family alike, none of them yet
checked on one but the G933: the G431 (046d:0a9b) and G433 (046d:0a6d) on
their USB adapters, the G633 (046d:0a5c) and G635 (046d:0a89) on their cables,
and through their receivers the G533 (046d:0a66), G535 (046d:0ac4),
G733 (046d:0ab5, 046d:0afe and 046d:0b1f), G933 (046d:0a5b), G935
(046d:0a87) and PRO X Wireless (046d:0aba). The daemon drives one of the
family at a time, the first it finds; one more plugged in is sent nothing,
and
.B status
lists it under
.BR problems ,
such as
.BR "G933 not driven; unplug the G432's USB adapter to use it" .
The daemon reads each one's
feature table (HID++ feature 0x0001) first and asks only for what it lists.
For one not yet tested, a line under
.B device:
asks for a device report. One that lists 0x1F20, ADCMeasurement, also gets
.B battery:
and
.BR "auto shut\-off:" .
.B battery:
is its last reading, in volts and, against Logitech's battery curve for its
id, as a percentage. The daemon reads it at the attach, every minute, after a
resume and each time this command runs alone, and takes it from each
broadcast but one of no state from its receiver. Once the daemon's read every
minute or after a resume fails on one through its receiver, or its receiver
reports it off, as on a headset switched off behind it, the line gives the
time of the reading; a reading with neither a percentage nor a state shows as
.B "not read"
with no time.
.B "auto shut\-off:"
is the minutes of silence it powers off after, or
.BR never ,
read from 0x1F20's version 2 on and set with
.BR g432\-auto\-off .
Where the daemon has
marked a headset through its receiver not answering, and the command's
own read, with no level given and without
.BR \-\-json ,
fails too, it shows the last readings, with
.B "not answering: asleep or switched off; the readings below are its last"
under the device line, in place of the error. One that lists no sidetone keeps the error. On
a G933, G935, G633 or G635, a line for each of its G keys follows, as
.BR "G933\-G1: brightness\-cycle" :
its binding on the layer in force, or what it does unbound; see
.BR bind .
The daemon turns the G keys' reports on (HID++ feature 0x8010, function 2,
.BR 01 )
when the headset attaches, as G HUB does on a G933, and again each time it
comes back while attached (after a resume, or as it answers again after it
was switched off or out of reach behind its receiver), and off
.RB ( 00 )
when it lets the headset go. A turn\-on that fails is sent again, until the
headset answers it, after each battery read that goes through and each time
this command, run alone, reads the headset. The
G935, G633 and G635 are taken to have the
G933's G keys and feature, which no source names for them.
.TP
\fBg432\-auto\-off\fR [\fBnever\fR|\fIminutes\fR]
The minutes of silence a wireless headset of the G432's family powers off
after, which it keeps: HID++ feature 0x1F20 from version 2 on, as the G933
and the G733 list it, the G733 not yet checked on one. Alone, it shows the
time the daemon last read, as
.B g432\-sidetone
does under
.BR "auto shut\-off:" .
.B never
or a
.I minutes
of 5, 15, 30 or 60, the times G HUB offers a G933, is checked before
anything is sent, written with 0x1F20 fn 2 and read back with fn 1, and the
time read back is shown. The headset is taken to keep it through a power-off,
since G HUB never sends it again when the headset reconnects, so on a model
not yet tested on its hardware, every one but the G432 and the G933, the
write is refused with nothing sent and its reason printed as the error;
alone, the command gives that reason under
.BR writes: .
It is refused too on a headset that lists no 0x1F20 or an older version.
.TP
\fBg432\-headset\-eq\fR [\fB\-\-gains\fR \fIg1\fR,\fIg2\fR,...|\fB\-\-flat\fR]
The equalizer in the headset of a G633, G933 or G935, the G633 and the G935
not yet checked on one: HID++ feature 0x8310, whose bands, range and gains
the daemon reads when the headset attaches and, where that read fails, after
each battery read that goes through and each time
.BR g432\-sidetone ,
run alone, reads the headset, until they read. Alone, this command reads
them again and shows under
.B playing:
each band's frequency with its gain, as
.B audio
gives a band
.RB ( "bands: none listed"
where it lists no band). The gain is that of the last write the headset
answered for the bands it lists since it attached or since it answered again
after it was switched off or out of reach behind its receiver, else 0 dB: it plays flat as it
switches on, and no location gives the gains it plays. Under
.B picked:
is the preset picked with
.BR g432\-headset\-presets ,
which the daemon sends again each time the headset attaches, once it reads an
equalizer the attach could not, and each time the headset comes back while
attached: after a resume, or when it answers again after it was switched off
or out of reach behind its receiver. With none picked, the daemon sends 0 dB on
every band to the RAM each time the headset attaches, or once it reads an
equalizer the attach could not, since the headset may still play gains sent
before, as across a restart of the daemon. Where a run of this command reads
an equalizer the attach could not before the daemon does, the preset picked
waits for the headset's next return, and 0 dB, with none picked, for its
next attach.
.B \-\-gains
sets a whole dB for each band, low to high, in the headset's range, checked
before anything is sent, with persistence 0, RAM alone: nothing the headset
keeps changes, a preset picked stays picked, and the gains play until the
headset switches off or the daemon sends the preset picked, or 0 dB, again
as above.
.B \-\-flat
writes 0 dB to every band, to RAM, as the headset plays once it switches on,
and forgets the preset picked, so each attach sends 0 dB in its place. The
gains the headset keeps (location 0,
EEPROM), which a G933 does not play as it switches on, are in the
.B saved
field of
.BR \-\-json 's
.BR equalizer .
On a version 0 feature, which has no RAM gains to read back and may keep
what it is sent, every write is refused with nothing sent, the reason given
as its error; the command alone shows that reason under
.BR writes: .
Any other model is refused, naming
.BR g432\-eq :
its equalizer runs on the computer.
.TP
\fBg432\-headset\-presets\fR [\fInumber\fR] [\fB\-\-save\-as\fR \fIname\fR] [\fB\-\-name\fR \fIname\fR] [\fB\-\-pick\fR] [\fB\-\-delete\fR]
The preset library on this computer of the equalizer in the headset the
daemon names, a G933, G935 or G633, up to 16 presets, in a file of that
model's own (see \fIg933\-eq.json\fR under FILES): each a name and its bands,
each a frequency and a whole dB. Alone it lists them by number; with a
\fInumber\fR it shows that
one (with \fB\-\-json\fR, every preset).
.B \-\-save\-as
saves as a new preset the gains the headset plays, as
.B g432\-headset\-eq
shows them under
.BR playing: ,
at the bands it lists, and refuses bands a preset has already;
.B \-\-name
renames preset \fInumber\fR;
.B \-\-delete
deletes it, alone, those after it a number lower; and
.BR \-\-pick ,
after
.BR \-\-name ,
sends its name and bands to the daemon, which writes its gains to the
headset's RAM, persistence 0, and keeps the preset to send again each time
the headset attaches or comes back, until
.BR "g432\-headset\-eq \-\-flat" ;
the report of
.B g432\-headset\-eq
follows. The daemon refuses a pick whose bands are not the headset's, or on
a version 0 feature. Each model's library starts with Media, Gaming and
Everyday, made to sound like the A50 X's presets from published measurements
of the G933 and the G935; the G633's are the G933's, inferred, since no
measurement of a G633 is published. Any other model is refused, naming
.BR g432\-eq .
.TP
\fBg432\-lights\fR [\fIeffect\fR [\fB\-\-color\fR \fI#RRGGBB\fR]|\fB\-\-color\fR \fI#RRGGBB\fR|\fB\-\-release\fR|\fB\-\-store\fR|\fB\-\-startup\fR \fBon\fR|\fBoff\fR]
The lights of a headset of the G432's family that lists HID++ feature 0x8070,
COLOR_LED_EFFECTS: the G733, G933, G935, G633 and G635, of which only the
G933 has been tested on its hardware. Alone, it shows the zones the headset lists,
the effects every zone takes and what it shows:
.B its own lighting
or the effect Gearbench last sent, with its color where the effect takes one,
and where a headset's G key set its brightness, as at
.BR "50%" ,
or turned it
.BR off ,
or, while
.B headset\-sync
is on and Gearbench sends the lights its color,
.BR "follows the keyboard that leads" ,
which
.BR \-\-json 's
.B follows
gives too.
On lights whose fn 0 lists the startup effect's setting, as the G933's
does,
.B startup:
gives
.B on
or
.BR off ,
whether they show their startup effect as the headset switches on, read when
the headset attaches (fn 4 00 01). Where that read fails, the rest of the
lights still works,
.B startup:
gives
.B "not read"
with why under
.BR note: ,
which
.BR \-\-json 's
.B startup_unread
gives too, and
.B \-\-startup
is refused in those words; the setting is read again, alone, after a
battery read that goes through and at a run of
.B g432\-sidetone
alone.
.B stored:
gives the effect and color the daemon last stored in them since it started, which
no read gives back, left out where it stored none or the last store it sent
failed. Where the zones share no effect,
the effects read
.B none every zone takes
and an effect is refused. A headset that lists no lights shows
.B "lights: none listed"
and each write is refused. Where the lights could not be read when the
headset attached, nor since, after a battery read that went through or at a run of
.B g432\-sidetone
alone, it shows
.B "lights: not read"
and, under
.BR note: ,
why, with the step to unplug the headset's receiver or cable and plug it
back in, the sentence a write is refused with, which
.BR \-\-json 's
.B lights_unread
gives too. An
.I effect
goes to every zone, with
.I color
where it takes one, each right after the host's
claim (fn 8 01 01, as G HUB sends it to a G933), as a zone effect in RAM alone
(fn 3, persistence byte 0): it shows until the headset powers off. Left
out, the
.I color
is the one Gearbench shows on the lights, or last showed on them since the
daemon started, as the
keyboard's
.B effect
keeps its color, or #0080ff where it showed none;
.B \-\-color
alone sends the effect shown, or last shown, static where none was, in that
color where the effect takes one. With
.B headset\-sync
off, once the headset is back from switched off, or the machine from a
suspend, it is not sent again and
.B g432\-lights
shows
.BR "its own lighting" ;
a G key then steps the lighting from full, with the effect last sent. Each color
goes out corrected as G HUB corrects a G933's, each channel through the sRGB
curve and cut to a whole value, after a G key's step has scaled it: #00b4ff
goes as 00 74 ff. What
.B g432\-lights
shows stays the color asked for.
.B \-\-release
gives the lights back to the headset (fn 8 00), as the daemon does when it
lets the headset go. It takes no
.I effect
or
.BR \-\-color :
either is refused before anything is sent.
.B \-\-store
writes the effect and color Gearbench shows now, at full even where a G key
dimmed them or turned them off, to every zone for the
headset to keep, each right after the claim, as a zone effect with
persistence byte 2, the color as asked for, not corrected, as G HUB's
"effects on device" writes a G933's: the headset then shows it without
Gearbench, after a power-off too. It is refused where Gearbench shows no lighting.
.B \-\-startup
sets the startup effect on or off (fn 5 00 01 01 or 00 01 02, as G HUB writes a
G933's) and reads it back; it is refused on lights that list no startup effect.
.B \-\-store
and
.B \-\-startup
take no
.IR effect ,
.BR \-\-color ,
.B \-\-release
or each other. The headset keeps
both, so on a model not yet tested on its hardware each write is refused
with nothing sent, the reason under
.B writes:
beside the
.B startup:
line, or, on lights that list no startup effect or whose startup effect could
not be read, in the refusal of
.BR \-\-store .
A headset that refuses the
claim as a function it lacks takes no host control: every write to its lights
is then refused, the effect never sent, since whether an effect stays in it is
not known, its reason printed as the error;
.B g432\-lights
alone gives that reason under
.BR writes: .
While
.B headset\-sync
is on, an
.IR effect ,
.BR \-\-color ,
.B \-\-release
and
.B \-\-store
are refused with nothing sent;
.B \-\-startup
is not.
.TP
\fBheadset\-sync\fR \fBon\fR | \fBoff\fR
On: the lights of a headset of the G432's family that has them, the G733,
G933, G935, G633 or G635, follow the keyboard as a mouse's lighting does (see
.BR mouse\-sync ):
the keyboard's color, or a loaded game profile's mouse color in its place,
with the keyboard's
.BR brightness ,
.B lock\-lights
and the
.B idle
dimming. Every zone that
has a static effect takes the color, corrected as
.B g432\-lights
corrects it, as one in RAM, right after the claim
(fn 8 01 01, then fn 3, persistence byte 0), at each change of it, at the
attach, and as the headset comes back from switched off or the machine from a
suspend. A headset switched off is sent nothing until it is back, and a write
it refuses is sent again at the next change of that color, or at the next
battery read on a headset that lists a battery, or at a run of
.B g432\-sidetone
alone. At a shutdown or reboot (see
.BR GEARBENCH_ASSUME_SHUTDOWN )
each such zone is written black, static #000000 in RAM, and the claim is
kept in place of the release (fn 8 00), as a mouse that follows the keyboard stays dark. While
.B headset\-sync
is on, a G key bound to the brightness cycle or the lighting on or off sets
the brightness of the keyboard that leads in place of the lights', as
.B brightness
does, and the lights and the mice that follow the keyboard take it. The cycle
sets the next step below the keyboard's level, and full after 0; the lighting
on or off sets 0, or full where the level is 0.
.B "headset\-sync on"
while it is on sends the color only to lights not known to show it, such as
lights whose last write of it failed. A headset of the G432's family whose
lights Gearbench does not drive, such as the G432, is left as it is, and
.B "headset\-sync on"
names it, as it says of lights none of whose zones has a static effect,
which are sent nothing, the claim included; a headset of another family,
such as the Astro A50 X, is left as it is and not named. Off: the lights get back the effect and color
.B g432\-lights
or a G key last sent, at the brightness the key left, or, where Gearbench sent
none since the headset attached or since a
.BR \-\-release ,
or forgot it at a return (see
.BR g432\-lights ),
the headset's own lighting (fn 8 00); a headset switched off is sent
nothing, and shows its own lighting as it switches on. One setting for every
headset and every profile, saved to
.I config.json
as
.BR headset_sync_color .
.TP
\fBg432\-eq\fR [\fBon\fR|\fBoff\fR] [\fB\-\-headset\fR \fIkey\fR] [\fB\-\-pick\fR \fIn\fR (or \fB\-\-select\fR)] [\fB\-\-preset\fR \fIn\fR] [\fB\-\-name\fR \fIname\fR] [\fB\-\-reset\fR] [\fB\-\-flat\fR] [\fB\-\-gain\fR \fIfreq\fR:\fIgain\fR (or \fB\-\-band\fR)]... [\fB\-\-bass\fR \fIgain\fR] [\fB\-\-treble\fR \fIgain\fR] [\fB\-\-save\-as\fR \fIname\fR] [\fB\-\-delete\fR]
Show or set Logitech G432 Equalizer, a separate output that plays to the
G432 and no other: ten bands, 32, 64, 125, 250, 500, 1000, 2000, 4000, 8000
and 16000 Hz, a bass shelf at 100 Hz and a treble shelf at 8 kHz, each a
whole number of dB from \-12 to +12. A boost raises its band on top of the
volume, so near full volume a boosted band can clip. Built from
G HUB's bands and published measurements of the headset, and checked on one
on 2026-10-17 and 2026-10-18. It sends nothing
to the headset and needs no daemon: it runs
.BR systemctl ,
.BR systemd\-run ,
.BR pw\-dump ,
.B pw\-metadata
and
.B pw\-cli
itself. It plays the active one of its presets, each a name and its gains:
the three shipped, 1 to 3, Media, Gaming and Everyday, with
Everyday active at first, then those saved as new, numbered after them, up
to 16 presets in all. The report is a line each: its state; the output's name,
with its node name in parentheses; the presets, each with its number; the
preset shown, the active one unless
.B \-\-preset
names another without
.B \-\-save\-as
or
.BR \-\-delete ,
which the line says; and that preset's gains: the bass shelf's with its
frequency, the ten bands' together on one line, each after its frequency,
and the treble shelf's with its frequency.
.B \-\-pick
(\fB\-\-select\fR, its name in Gearbench 1.1.0)
makes a preset the active one, first.
.B \-\-preset
alone only chooses the preset shown. The other options change the active
preset, or the one
.B \-\-preset
names, in this order:
.B \-\-reset
puts a shipped preset's default back, its name and its gains, and refuses
one saved as new, which has none;
.B \-\-flat
puts every gain at 0;
.B \-\-gain
(\fB\-\-band\fR, its name in Gearbench 1.1.0 to 1.2.1)
sets one band's gain and can be given once for each band, and
.B \-\-bass
and
.B \-\-treble
the shelves';
.B \-\-name
renames it, 1 to 49 bytes in UTF\-8, not spaces alone, with no control
character; and
.B \-\-save\-as
\fIname\fR saves it, after the rest, as a new preset named \fIname\fR,
which becomes the active one, refused once there are 16.
.BR \-\-delete ,
which takes no \fB\-\-pick\fR and no other change, deletes it, one saved as new, and those after
it go a number lower; with the active one deleted, the one before it plays.
A shipped preset is refused; \fB\-\-reset\fR puts its default back.
A change to a
.I g432\-eq.json
that does not read or was read in part is refused (see FILES).
Otherwise every option is checked before anything is saved; the change is
then saved to
.I g432\-eq.json
and, where it changes the preset that plays or that preset's gains, set at
once while the equalizer runs.
.B on
and
.B off
run after that save, so a refusal or failure of either leaves the change
saved.
.B on
writes its PipeWire configuration for the active preset's gains, aimed at
the G432's output, and starts the equalizer as the transient user unit
.IR gearbench\-g432\-eq.service .
It gives the equalizer a volume as loud as the G432's own, at most 100%, and
the G432's mute, and makes the equalizer the default output while the G432's
own output is the default.
Once the volume and any such pick went through, and PipeWire gives a volume
for the G432's own output, it waits up to 2 seconds for
a Gearbench Spatial that plays straight to the G432 to move to the equalizer,
and up to 2 seconds more for anything else that plays straight to the G432's
own output to leave it.
It then holds the G432's own output at 0 dB, the adapter's volume with no
cut, and unmuted, so the equalizer's volume and mute are the ones that apply,
and starts the transient user unit
.IR gearbench\-g432\-eq\-watch.service ,
which holds a G432 plugged back in at 0 dB again and stops with the
equalizer. A pick that WirePlumber had not carried out within 2 seconds
counts as one that did not go through. Gearbench Spatial or other sound
still on the G432's own output after its wait, as from an app with the G432
picked as its output, or a look at either that could not be read, leaves the
G432 unheld and unwatched as well, so nothing on that output gets louder.
Something that starts playing straight to the G432's own output after the
hold plays at 0 dB. While the
equalizer runs, Gearbench Spatial on the G432 plays through it, and after
.B off
straight to the G432 again. While Outputs is on, Gearbench Spatial plays
into Gearbench Game and stays there at
.B on
and
.BR off ;
Game plays through the equalizer while it runs.
While the equalizer runs or starts,
.B on
does nothing; otherwise it refuses while the G432 is
not plugged in, or while its profile has no output (Configuration in GNOME's
Sound settings, Profile in KDE Plasma's). An equalizer
that stops as it starts is an error, which
names the journalctl command that says why, and the default output and
the G432's own volume are left as they were. The equalizer runs
until
.BR off ,
the account's last logout (with lingering on, a restart), or PipeWire's stop.
.B off
gives a held G432's own output a volume as loud as the equalizer's, muted
while the equalizer is, and, while
the equalizer is the default output, makes the G432's own output the default
before it stops the equalizer, which removes Logitech G432 Equalizer from the
outputs; with the G432 unplugged then,
or on a profile with no output, the next
.B on
makes the equalizer the default again, unless Gearbench Game is the default;
Outputs plays Game through the equalizer when it plays to the G432.
With the G432's own output there,
.B off
moves those of Gearbench Game, Chat and Media that play through the equalizer
to the G432's own output before the stop, and waits up to 2 seconds for them
to leave the equalizer, so the stop pauses no player on them.
Changes given with
.B off
are only saved. A volume set, a pick of the default output, a hold, the
watch's start or a put-back that fails, a Gearbench Spatial that did not
move to the equalizer or could not be read, sound still straight on the
G432's own output or not read, or Gearbench Game, Chat or Media not moved
off the equalizer or not read, is a
.B note:
line after the report, and the command exits 0. A
step that fails prints systemctl's, systemd\-run's or PipeWire's own error
when it prints one, and Gearbench's words otherwise, and exits 1. A change
saved that could not
be set on the running equalizer, as when systemctl, pw\-dump or pw\-cli
failed after the save, or the output of an equalizer that runs or starts did
not show in PipeWire within 2 seconds, says it is saved, with that error,
and exits 1: it applies at the next start. Where
.B on
does not make the equalizer the default output, pick it in the desktop's
sound settings or in a program.
.IP
The other headsets of the G432's family with no equalizer of their own, the
G431, G433, G533, G535, G635, G733 and PRO X Wireless, each have an
equalizer like the G432's under their own names, built with none of them on
hand: on the G733, Logitech G733 Equalizer, its presets in
.I g733\-eq.json
and its units
.I gearbench\-g733\-eq.service
and
.IR gearbench\-g733\-eq\-watch.service .
Each has the G432's three shipped presets, made for the G432 and not
measured on the others, and finds its headset's card in PipeWire by any of
the headset's USB ids, such as the G733's three.
.B \-\-headset
names the headset by its key, as
.B headset\-spatial
does:
.BR g432 ,
.BR g431 ,
.BR g433 ,
.BR g533 ,
.BR g535 ,
.BR g635 ,
.B g733
or
.BR pro_x_wireless .
Left out, it acts on the headset of the family the daemon drives, with two
plugged in too, or, where it drives none or no daemon answers, the first
found by its USB ids in sysfs, the one it would drive, with nothing opened,
and on the G432 with none. The
G933, G935 and G633 so found are refused: their
equalizer is the headset's own (see
.BR g432\-headset\-eq ).
.TP
\fBaudio\fR [\fB\-\-read\fR]
The Astro A50 X or A50 Gen 5, through its base station's control interface,
over Logitech's Centurion protocol: report 0x02 with the byte 0x0c after it,
where its report descriptor declares it. The A50 X's reads were checked on
one on 2026-10-21 and its writes read from G HUB's traffic with it. The PRO X 2 LIGHTSPEED (USB 046d:0af7) is read
through its receiver's control interface, 03, on report 0x51, over the
receiver's bridge, CentPPBridge (0x0003). The
G522 LIGHTSPEED (046d:0b18) and the G325 LIGHTSPEED (046d:0b21) are read the
same way through their receiver on report 0x50 with the byte 0x23 after it,
and the G522 on its cable (046d:0b19) on that report with no bridge. With
the G522's cable and its receiver both plugged in,
the daemon tries the cable first; attached through the receiver, it moves
to the cable as the cable's node appears, and the journal says
.B moved from its receiver
once. The A50 X's
writes are tested on this hardware; the other models, built with none on hand
from published accounts of their frames and features, are not tested. The
report is what the daemon
last read of the base station or the headset and what the headset's
events said since: the node and the model, and under them, for a model not
yet tested on the hardware, the note that asks for a device report from it
(see
.BR report );
the frames' prefix, and the
bridge's index through a receiver; the features the root lookups found, by
id (0x0104
battery, 0x0604 sidetone, 0x0200 volume, 0x0609 Game and Voice balance,
0x0801 noise gate, 0x0108 auto sleep, 0x0115 the A50 X's deep sleep mode,
0x0110 the base station's brightness, 0x060b tones, 0x020d equalizer, 0x0800
the stream mix and the microphone's boom, on the PRO X 2 and the G325
0x0602 the microphone's noise suppression, on the PRO X 2 0x0636 its onboard
equalizer, read alone, and on the G522 and the G325 0x0618 the eco mode);
the battery; then, in the order of the window's Headset tab, on the A50s
the balance and the microphone's boom, the microphone's settings, each
instance's equalizer, the preset it plays or
.BR custom ,
and each band's frequency and gain, on the A50s the stream mix, and the
other settings, each setting its word or a number with its range, and the
value its first read found where that differs; and the refusal every write
gets while the model is not tested.
.B \-\-read
reads every feature again first. A value whose read the headset refuses, or
answers with what Gearbench cannot read, is given as
.B not read
until a read goes through or an event gives it, though it was read before; an event Gearbench
cannot read has its feature read again. The A50 X's base answers for the headset switched off. A read with no
answer stops the rest, and the command is refused with
.BR "did not answer" .
A receiver whose headset is off or out of reach when the daemon attaches it
passes on the lookups and gets no answer: the daemon lets it go, says so in
.BR status ,
and asks again from 5 seconds to a minute later; the receiver's word that
its headset went away, or a read its headset does not answer once attached,
does the same, though it asks first within 5 seconds. Meanwhile
.B audio
reports the headset as
.B "not answering: asleep or switched off"
under its device line, with its last readings where it went away while its
receiver was attached, else each value as
.BR "not read" ,
and every write is refused.
.TP
\fBaudio\-set\fR \fIsetting\fR [\fIvalue\fR]
Set one of the headset's settings and read it back. On the A50s:
.B sidetone
0 to 6,
.B volume
0 to 21,
.B balance
0 (all Voice) to 12 (all Game),
.B noise\-gate
.BR off ,
.BR night ,
.B home
or
.BR tournament ,
.B auto\-sleep
.BR never " (or 0), 15, 30 or 60 minutes,"
.B brightness
0 to 100, the base station's light,
.B tones
.BR none ,
.B all
or
.BR minimal ,
and on the A50 X
.B deep\-sleep
.B on
or
.BR off ,
the base station asleep after 15 minutes idle.
On the PRO X 2, the G522 and the G325:
.B sidetone
0 to 100 on the PRO X 2 and 0 to 9 on the others,
.B auto\-sleep
.BR never " (or 0) or any minute from 1 to 255,"
.B noise\-suppression
.B on
or
.B off
on the PRO X 2 and the G325, and
.B eco\-mode
.B on
or
.BR off ,
the battery saver, on the G522 and the G325.
The value is checked before anything is sent, and a setting the attached
model lacks is refused before anything is written. A setting whose write or read
back failed is given as
.B not read
until it is read again.
.BR put\-back ,
with no value, writes back each value the daemon first read since it started, kept across an unplug;
it sends none while the base station does not list one of those settings.
The A50 X keeps each value it is sent through a power cycle, and its write
test let each through. On every other model every write is refused, with
nothing sent, until it is tested there. A write to a feature the headset lists at
another version than the one whose layout Gearbench writes is refused, with
nothing sent.
.TP
\fBaudio\-eq\fR \fBheadphones\fR|\fBmicrophone\fR [\fB\-\-gains\fR \fIg1\fR,\fIg2\fR,...] [\fB\-\-pick\fR \fIname\fR]
Alone, the instance's equalizer as the daemon read it: the preset it plays,
or
.BR custom ,
and each of its bands, under the device line and the note
.B audio
gives while the headset is away; an instance the model has no equalizer of is
refused. With
.BR \-\-gains ,
ten gains split by commas, \-6 to +6 dB, lowest band
first (the ten given bare, its form in Gearbench 1.2.1, still work), it writes the slot the headset plays: 3 on the A50s, and 0 on the G522
and the G325, whose only equalizer is the headphones'. It keeps each band's
frequency and Q as read, then reads the slot back. In the headset a raised
band boosts on top of the volume, with no cut elsewhere. With
.BR \-\-pick ,
it copies one of the A50 X's presets, which slots 0 to 2 hold, into slot 3
with G HUB's Q of 0x16, as G HUB does:
.BR default ,
.B gaming
or
.B media
for the headphones,
.BR default ,
.B competition
or
.B broadcast
for the microphone. The A50 Gen 5's presets are not read. The PRO X 2's
equalizer, five bands from \-12 to +12 dB, is its onboard one, 0x0636, which
is read and never written.
A write is refused, with nothing sent, until it is tested on the model, and
for a feature listed at another version, as for
.BR audio\-set .
.TP
\fBaudio\-mix\fR \fIsource\fR [\fIlevel\fR] [\fB\-\-mute\fR|\fB\-\-unmute\fR]
The A50 X's stream mix, what the base station's stream port sends (the Stream Out
input in the desktop's sound settings): the level of
.BR stream ,
the stream port itself, or of a source,
.BR game ,
.BR voice ,
.B mic
or
.BR bluetooth ,
0 to 32, G HUB's 0 to 100%, or a source muted or unmuted. The mix is read
with function 5 of 0x0800 and written whole with function 6, the rest as it
was read, then read back. The report gives the mix, and
.BR boom ,
whether the microphone's boom is down and it is live, which the base station
says when the boom moves. The A50 Gen 5's is the A50 X's, assumed. Refused, with
nothing sent, until it is tested on the model, and for a feature listed at
another version, as for
.BR audio\-set ,
and for a source the mix as read does not list.
.TP
\fBaudio\-presets\fR [\fInumber\fR] [\fB\-\-save\-as\fR \fIname\fR [\fB\-\-from\fR \fBheadphones\fR|\fBmicrophone\fR]] [\fB\-\-name\fR \fIname\fR] [\fB\-\-pick\fR] [\fB\-\-delete\fR]
The preset library on this computer of the model the daemon names, up to
16 presets, in a file of that model's own (see
.I a50x\-eq.json
under FILES), apart from the
headset and from any presets it holds: each a name,
the equalizer it is of, one of the model's, and its bands, each a
frequency, a Q byte and a gain: ten, as \fBaudio\-eq\fR takes them, or on
the PRO X 2 the five of its onboard equalizer, at 80, 240, 750, 2200 and
6600 Hz with a Q byte of 1 and a whole dB from \-12 to +12. A preset of
another count or format for the model is refused. Alone it lists them by number; with a \fInumber\fR it shows that one
(with \fB\-\-json\fR, every preset).
.B \-\-save\-as
saves as a new preset the bands that the equalizer
.B \-\-from
names (the headphones' when left out) plays, as the daemon last read
them, and refuses bands a preset of that equalizer already has, by their
frequencies and gains;
.B \-\-name
renames preset \fInumber\fR;
.B \-\-delete
deletes it, alone, those after it a number lower; and
.BR \-\-pick ,
after
.BR \-\-name ,
writes its bands into the slot its equalizer plays, slot 3 on the A50s,
and the report of
.B audio
follows. On the A50s, slots 0 to 2, G HUB's presets, are never written. The
daemon refuses a pick, with nothing sent, as it refuses an
\fBaudio\-eq\fR write.
Each model's library starts with three presets for the headphones,
Media, Gaming and Everyday: the A50 X's tuned by ear on an A50 X, and each
other model's but the G325's fitted from that model's published
measurements to sound like the A50 X's. The G325's three are untuned: no measurement of a G325 is
published, so they are the A50 X's bands as they are.
.SH DEVICES
These have been tested on the hardware: the G915, the G502 X PLUS and the
G305, which Gearbench was built on; the wired G515 TKL; and the G915 X
LIGHTSPEED, on its cable and through its receiver. The G502 HERO, the Astro
A50 Gen 4, the Logitech G432, the G933 and the Astro A50 X have been tested
in part. Below is each keyboard and mouse model but the G915, the G502 X PLUS and the G305:
what it was built from, what is tested on it and what Gearbench writes to
it. The headsets are under
.B Headset
in
.BR COMMANDS .
.PP
Gearbench speaks HID++ 2.0
over the keyboards' and the mice's vendor hidraw interfaces, on a cable or
through a LIGHTSPEED receiver, and over the one HID interface of the G432
and its family.
It speaks Centurion, HID++ 2.0 in frames of its own, over the control
interface of the A50 X's and A50 Gen 5's base station, through the receiver
of the PRO X 2, G522 and G325, and on the G522's cable.
It speaks the A50 Gen 4 base station's own protocol, not HID++, over its
one HID interface.
.SS Mice
The G502 HERO, on its cable, was built from published logs, its firmware
image and G HUB's records. It was tested on the hardware on 2026-10-17 and,
its lighting included, on 2026-10-18. It is
read, and its DPI set live. Its onboard profile 1 is written as the G502 X
PLUS's is, each write after a backup:
.BR mouse\-dpi\-stages ,
tested on this hardware with host mode off (2026-10-21), and
.BR mouse\-rate ,
.B mouse\-fallback\-buttons
and
.BR mouse\-restore ,
let through for their test. It is switched to profile 1 when it runs another.
.B mouse\-enable\-buttons
is refused with nothing sent: the daemon does not read its event node, so
its buttons, enabled, would do nothing outside host mode. Its two
lighting zones take
.BR mouse\-color ,
.B mouse\-effect
and
.BR mouse\-sync ,
each sent for the mouse's working memory alone, which a replug clears (checked
on 2026-10-18).
The daemon claims its lighting (HID++ feature 0x8070) only once it has a
color to write: one set with
.B mouse\-color
or
.BR mouse\-effect ,
or, with
.B mouse\-sync
on, the keyboard's or a loaded game profile's mouse color. Until then the mouse
shows its own lighting and gets no lighting write,
after a replug or a resume too. The one exception is a release (fn 8
.BR 00 )
at each attach, which ends a claim left by a daemon killed before its stop ran.
.B "mouse\-sync off"
with no color or effect of its own set releases the claim, so its own lighting
comes back, as does
.BR "mouse\-effect \-\-release" .
.B status
says which lighting it shows.
In host mode (see
.BR mouse\-host )
its G4 to G9 reach the daemon through its button spy, G6 its sniper button.
Its buttons in host mode are not yet tested on the hardware.
When the mouse leaves host mode, its firmware writes the running profile's two
lighting records, unchanged, into its settings memory.
.PP
The G502 X LIGHTSPEED, the G502 X PLUS without lighting, through its
receiver and on its cable, is built from G HUB's description of it
and a published listing of its features, and not yet tested on the
hardware. It is read, its battery included, and its DPI set live. Every
write to its onboard profile is refused with nothing written, since that
write is not tested on this hardware, and the refusal says what stays as it
is for that write.
The commands that write it are
.BR mouse\-dpi\-stages ,
.BR mouse\-enable\-buttons ,
.BR mouse\-fallback\-buttons ,
.BR mouse\-restore ,
and
.B mouse\-rate
outside host mode. In host
mode, the default,
.B mouse\-rate
sets the rate live, with nothing stored on the mouse. It is not switched to
profile 1, since whether that switch persists is not known. In host mode its
G4 to G9 reach the daemon as the G502 X PLUS's do, with the X PLUS's button
layout assumed.
.PP
The PRO X SUPERLIGHT, through its receiver and on its cable, is
built from its firmware, a published listing of its features and G HUB's
description of it, and not yet tested on the hardware. It has no lighting
the daemon drives.
It is read, its battery included, and its DPI set live. Its onboard profile
writes are refused as the G502 X LIGHTSPEED's are, and it is not switched to
profile 1. In host mode its side buttons, G4 and G5, reach the daemon through its button
spy and
.B mouse\-rate
sets the rate live.
.PP
The G502 X, wired, with no lighting the daemon drives and no battery, is
built from a published log of one unit and published listings of its
features, and not
yet tested on the hardware. It is read and its DPI set live. Its onboard
profile writes are refused as the G502 X LIGHTSPEED's are, and it is not
switched to profile 1. In host mode its G4 to G9 reach the daemon as the G502 X PLUS's do.
.PP
The G502 X PLUS MILLENNIUM FALCON, through its receiver and on its cable, is
built with every feature assumed from the G502 X PLUS by G HUB's
description of it, and not yet tested on the hardware. It is read, its
battery included, and its DPI set live. Its lighting takes
.BR mouse\-color ,
.B mouse\-effect
and
.BR mouse\-sync
for the mouse's working memory alone, with its two signature effects named
.B millennium\-falcon
and
.BR millennium\-falcon\-inactive ,
the second the one kept for power saving, after G HUB's name for both, Millennium Falcon;
.B status
says its lighting is not yet tested. Its onboard profile writes are refused as
the G502 X LIGHTSPEED's are, and it is not switched to profile 1. In host mode its G4 to
G9 reach the daemon as the G502 X PLUS's do.
.PP
The G502 LIGHTSPEED, through its receiver and on its cable, is built
from G HUB's description of it and published logs of the mouse, and not yet
tested on the hardware. It is read, its battery as a percentage from G HUB's
curves for its cell, and its DPI set live. Its onboard profile writes are
refused as the PRO X SUPERLIGHT's are, and it is not switched to profile 1. In
host mode its G4 to G9 reach the daemon as the G502 HERO's do; G9, which
shows the battery on its factory profile, is bound to nothing by default.
Its two lighting zones are driven as the G502 HERO's, with one difference. Its
0x8070 is taken to store an effect written while the host does not hold the
lighting, and to drop that hold at a power-state change, as the PRO
WIRELESS's firmware does. So the daemon claims the lighting again right
before each effect it writes.
.PP
The PRO WIRELESS, through its receiver and on its cable, is built from
its firmware, a published log of the mouse and G HUB's description of it, and
not yet tested on the hardware. It is driven as the G502 LIGHTSPEED: its
battery from the same curves, its onboard profile writes refused, and its
two zones claimed right before each effect, since its firmware stores an
effect written otherwise. In host mode its G4 to G8, back and forward on each
side and its DPI button, which cycles the DPI by default, reach the daemon
through its button spy, and a report rate is set live.
.PP
The G903 LIGHTSPEED HERO, through its receiver and on its cable, is
built from its firmware, a published log of the mouse and G HUB's
description of it, and not yet tested on the hardware. It is not the older
G903, which is not matched. It is read, its battery as a percentage from G
HUB's curves for its cell, and its DPI set live. Its onboard profile writes
are refused as the PRO X SUPERLIGHT's are, and it is not switched to profile 1.
In host mode its G4 to G9, back and forward on each side and its two DPI
buttons, reach the daemon through its button spy; its wheel tilts scroll, its
profile button does nothing, and a report rate is set live. Its lighting
(HID++ feature 0x8071, with no per-key 0x8081) is claimed only once there is
a color to write, and released once at each attach with nothing to write, as
the G502 HERO's is. That is because the claim takes the mouse's power modes
too: given no color, the mouse keeps its own power saving. A color is each zone's static effect alone.
.PP
The PRO X SUPERLIGHT 2 (the SE on its ids), the PRO X SUPERLIGHT 2 DEX, the
PRO X SUPERLIGHT 2c and the PRO X2 SUPERSTRIKE, through their receiver,
046d:c54d, and on their cables, are built from published listings and
logs of their features and G HUB's description of them. They are not yet
tested on the hardware; the 2c's features are assumed to be the SUPERLIGHT 2's. The
SUPERSTRIKE's HID++ feature 0x1B0C, its main buttons' actuation point, rapid
trigger and haptics, is written nothing and read by the device report alone,
and its 0x80E0 is sent nothing. They have no lighting the
daemon drives. Their DPI is HID++ feature 0x2202's and their rate 0x8061's,
which the mouse takes in host mode alone. Outside it,
.B mouse\-dpi
says the mouse refused the DPI, the hand-back at a stop puts
back no DPI, and an attach in onboard mode sends none. Their onboard profile
writes are refused as the PRO X SUPERLIGHT's are, and they are not switched to
profile 1. In host mode their side buttons, G4 and G5, reach the daemon
through the button spy, and
.B mouse\-rate
sets the rate live.
.PP
The G203 LIGHTSYNC, and the G102 LIGHTSYNC on its ids, 046d:c092 and
046d:c09d, cabled alone, is built from a published listing of its
features, published logs of two units and G HUB's description of it, and not
yet tested on the hardware. Its HID++ interface, 01, carries the keystrokes
its onboard profile binds to its buttons too, as the G502 HERO's does. Its DPI
is set live from 200 to 8000. Its onboard profile writes are refused, and it
is not switched to profile 1. Its lighting, 0x8071's logo zone and 0x8081's
three lights, is driven as the G502 X PLUS's, claimed only once there is a
color to write and released once at each attach with nothing to write. In
host mode its G4 and G5, back and forward, and its DPI button, G6, which
cycles the DPI by default, reach the daemon through its button spy, and
.B mouse\-rate
sets the rate live.
.SS Keyboards
The G915 TKL, on its cable (046d:c343) or its receiver (046d:c545, or
046d:408e behind it on a kernel that gives the keyboard a node of its own),
is built from its firmware image, G HUB's records and published
listings of its features, and not yet tested on the hardware. The G913 TKL
has the same ids. Its G1 to
G12 are its F1 to F12, which type only through its onboard profile, so in
host mode the daemon types each through its virtual keyboard while nothing
is bound to it on the layer in effect. While it does,
.B status
says
.BR "F1 to F12 diverted, typed by Gearbench while unbound" .
They bind as G1 to G5 do, under their own names
.RB ( "bind F1" ),
so a binding for G1 leaves F1 typing F1. While the daemon has no virtual keyboard (see
.I /dev/uinput
under FILES), it leaves the keyboard on its onboard profile, so F1 to F12 work,
and drives none of its lighting or keys. It hands back a keyboard it finds
in host mode, as a stop does, and
.B status
says why under its problems. It sets the keyboard up once the virtual
keyboard opens. It does the same while the session is not active, when
another account or a text console has the screen, so F1 to F12 type there.
Going inactive hands the keyboard back as a stop does, and it is set up
again once the session is active. The daemon's stop puts it back in onboard
mode, at a shutdown too, unless the keyboard is not answering then. Nothing
is written to its onboard memory.
.PP
The G515 TKL and the G515 LIGHTSPEED TKL bind their F1 to F12 through the
keyboard's custom assignments (HID++ features 0x1B05 and 0x8101), under
their own names
.RB ( "bind G515\-F1" ),
so a binding for a G915 TKL's F1 is not theirs. Writing those assignments
has not passed its test on the hardware yet, so it is refused with nothing
sent. A bind of one of those keys is saved, with a note that says so, and
the keys type their own. Once that write passes its test, the daemon writes the
keyboard's BASE layer at each attach and whenever the keys bound in the
layer in effect change. In that layer each bound F key is set to report to
the daemon, which runs its binding, and every other F key is left typing its
own. The keyboard
keeps that layer until it loses power, and the daemon's stop writes it back
empty. With no virtual keyboard, or while the session is not active, it sets
no key.
.B status
says whether the keyboard's custom assignments are on, the keyboard's
shortcut that turns them on and off, and whether Game Mode is on, which the
keyboard reports as its Game Mode button is pressed. The daemon writes
neither, nor the keys Game Mode blocks. Each time the keyboard says its
shortcut turned the custom assignments to the other state (HID++ feature
0x1B05, event 6), the daemon posts a transient desktop notice, like
.BR "G915 X LIGHTSPEED: custom assignments off" .
Under that line the notice gives what the keys set to report do until the
shortcut turns them back, or the shortcut alone where no key is set to
report. Another notice
within 1.5 seconds of the last replaces it in place, and the daemon takes
it down 5 seconds after the last, as it does the DPI notice (see
.BR dpi\-notices ).
A connect posts none, nor an event saying the state the daemon holds, and
the notice has no switch of its own. The daemon reads the keyboard's
control list (HID++ feature 0x1B10) and names each key of the shortcut,
as FN+F1, by the control id the list holds at the key, or as key 0x69 where
the list names none it knows or could not be read. On the G515s and on each
of their newer relatives below, the daemon finds a keyboard's G keys, or the
F keys that stand for them, in that list by their control ids, where their
key ids were not measured on one.
.PP
The G515's newer relatives, each on its cable, are the G915 X LIGHTSPEED
(046d:c356, and 046d:40b5 through its receiver), tested on the hardware on
2026-10-23, and these, not yet tested: the G915 X (046d:c359), the G915 X LIGHTSPEED TKL
(046d:c357, and 046d:40b6 through its receiver), the G316 X (046d:c361),
the G316 X 98 (046d:c36b), the G512 X 75 (046d:c35f) and the G512 X 98
(046d:c360). They were built from G HUB's records and key layouts, published
listings of the G915 X's and G915 X LIGHTSPEED's features, which the G915 X
LIGHTSPEED TKL is taken to share, and the firmware images of the G316 X and
G512 X models. They are driven as the G515s: probed for HID++ feature 0x8071,
on interface 02 of the cable, with their lighting, brightness, FN's reports
and FN keys, custom assignments and Game Mode driven by feature. Their keys bind through the keyboard's
custom assignments under names of their own (see
.BR bind ),
and that write is refused with nothing sent until it is tested on each
model. It passed on the G915 X LIGHTSPEED, whose G1 to G9 take their
bindings on either route. There the keyboard's BASE layer sets all nine to report to
the daemon, bound or not, so a bind writes nothing to the keyboard and an
unbound G key does nothing while the layer is set.
Without the layer, the keyboard sends F13 to F21 for G1 to G9, which GNOME
takes for Settings (F13), mic mute (F20) and the touchpad (F21).
The keyboard is without the layer, for example, after the daemon's
stop, while the daemon has no virtual keyboard or the session is not active,
or with the custom assignments turned off by their shortcut.
The HID usages of F13 to F21, 0x68 to 0x70, were read on 2026-10-23 with no
layer, G3 to G7's in host mode only; with the custom assignments off, G1
opened Settings on 2026-10-30.
At each setup, after the lighting claim, the daemon sets each keyboard of
the G515 generation, the G515s included, to its host mode (HID++ feature
0x8101, function 6, 05); a refusal is logged once, and the keyboard runs on
in the mode it is in.
When it stops or lets the keyboard go, it sets the keyboard to its onboard
mode (0f), which the keyboard powers up in.
A shutdown leaves the keyboard in host mode.
On the G915 X
LIGHTSPEED its own lighting shows whenever the claim drops,
as at a link change, until the daemon claims and paints it again. Through
its receiver a LIGHTSPEED model is driven on its node of its own, as the G515
LIGHTSPEED TKL is, behind a 046d:c547, as the G915 X LIGHTSPEED was on
2026-10-23. The TKL's receiver is taken to be a 046d:c547 too, since G HUB's
records give the receiver's kind and not its id. That node
carries what is typed on it beside HID++. Over Bluetooth it is not driven. The
G512 X's analog keys (0x1B08) are not set. Nothing is written to their onboard memory.
.PP
The G815, on its cable (046d:c33f), is built from published listings
and logs of its features and its quick-start guide, and not yet tested on
the hardware; the G813 has the same id. It is driven as the G915: its G1 to
G5 and G-Shift layer, M1 to M3, lighting and brightness. Its HID++ shares
interface 01 with what is typed on it and declares long reports alone, so
every request to it goes out long. Nothing is written to its onboard memory.
.PP
The PRO X TKL LIGHTSPEED, on its cable (046d:c352) or through its receiver
(046d:40b0, its node of its own behind a 046d:c547), is built from a
published listing of its features, G HUB's records and the key layout G HUB
draws for it, and not yet tested on the hardware. Its G1 to G12 are its F1
to F12 and work as the G915 TKL's do: typed by the daemon while unbound,
bound under their own names
.RB ( "bind PROXTKL\-F1" ),
left to the keyboard's own profile while the daemon has no virtual keyboard
or the session is not active, and handed back to it at the daemon's stop.
It has no M keys or MR. Its lighting is set and its battery read as the G515
LIGHTSPEED TKL's are, with three differences. Its cluster 0xff is not read, so
none of \fBpulsar\-point\fR, \fBwave\fR and \fBripple\fR is taken from
it. Its brightness is written at each repaint and not read at its connect, as
the G915's is. It has no HID++ feature 0x8051, so its FN keys do not light.
Its HID++ interface on the cable is taken to be 02.
It may store the brightness it is sent, as a G515 does; nothing else is
written to its onboard memory. Where this page says a G915 TKL
types its F1 to F12, is left on its own profile or is put back in onboard
mode, the same holds for it.
.PP
The G213 Prodigy, on its cable (046d:c336), is built from a
published listing of its features and G HUB's records, and not yet tested
on the hardware. Its lighting is HID++ feature 0x8070's zones, the whole
board and its five areas; it is the only keyboard lit that way. The daemon
claims them (fn 8
.BR 01 )
at its setup and, as it does a PRO WIRELESS's, again right before each
effect write (fn 3, its persistence byte
.BR 00 ,
working memory alone). It releases them (fn 8
.BR 00 )
at its stop. A color goes to every zone, and
an animation to the zones that list it, with nothing written to the others.
Zone 0 is taken to be the whole board and zones 1 to 5 its areas in the
order G HUB lists them, which
.B "status \-\-json"
names among its capabilities as
.BR areas :
a profile's painted keys show as their areas' colors. A static write sends
the background to zone 0, then to each area the color most of its painted
keys have, or the background where none of its keys is painted. Where two
colors tie, the area gets the one met first row by row from the top left.
.B paint
shows a key's color on its area this way, and the window paints every key
of an area at a click; where its zones do not list every area,
.B paint
is refused. It has no per-key lighting, so a key preview is refused, and no
brightness, which
.B brightness
refuses. It has no idle times or idle notices either:
.B idle
saves the times with a note, the keyboard never dims for its own idleness,
and a mouse that follows the keyboard follows the desktop's alone. Its wave takes horizontal,
reverse\-horizontal, center\-out and center\-in. Its HID++ is on interface 01,
long reports alone, beside what is typed on it. Its F1 to F12, which G
HUB maps through HID++ feature 0x1BC0, are not driven. Whether its firmware
keeps a claimed zone effect out of its memory is not known.
.PP
On the G515 TKL and the G515 LIGHTSPEED TKL the daemon turns the keyboard's FN
reports on at each setup (HID++ feature 0x8051 LogiModifiers function 3 with
.BR "03 ff" ).
The keyboard keeps them on until it loses power: switched off, or unplugged
where it has no battery. While FN is
held, F8 to F12, Print Screen, Scroll Lock, Pause and FN show white over the
lighting in effect, and every other key stays as it was. That takes one
per-key frame at the press and one at the release, with no zone effect.
Under an animation, though, the press writes Disabled first and the release writes the animation again,
which starts it over. FN wakes a board dimmed or dark from its idle dimming
as any key does. While a locked or blank screen keeps the lights off, FN
lights nothing. Under
.B disabled
the FN keys still show white, over black. The keyboard carries out its FN
functions itself all the same. It has run on a wired G515 TKL, and not yet
on a G515 LIGHTSPEED TKL. The G515's newer relatives get the
same, with their own keys lit: F2 to F4 on the G915 X models, F7 to F12 on
the G316 X models, and on the G512 X models the keys whose caps print a
second function, \(ga and 1 to 3, F3 to F12 and, on the 75, Print Screen, Page Up
and Page Down. The G915 X LIGHTSPEED reports FN only while its custom
assignments are on, and every model with custom assignments, the G515s
included, is taken to do the same. So with them off its FN keys do not light.
When the keyboard says they went off, as FN+F1 turns them off, the daemon takes
FN as let go and paints its keys back, and takes what its G keys hold as let go
too. The G915 TKL
reports nothing while its FN is held, so its keys do not light.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/gearbench/config.json
The configuration, one JSON object: \fBversion\fR, 4; \fBactive_profile\fR, the
M profile in effect; \fBbrightness\fR, each keyboard model's, keyed by the
model's first product id in four hex digits: \fBc33e\fR for the G915,
\fBc355\fR for the G515 LIGHTSPEED TKL, \fBc343\fR for the G915 TKL,
\fBc352\fR for the PRO X TKL LIGHTSPEED, \fBc356\fR for the G915 X LIGHTSPEED
and \fBc357\fR for the G915 X LIGHTSPEED TKL, each on its cable and its
receiver, and on its cable alone \fBc358\fR for the G515 TKL, \fBc33f\fR for
the G815, \fBc359\fR for the G915 X, \fBc361\fR for the G316 X, \fBc36b\fR for
the G316 X 98, \fBc35f\fR for the G512 X 75, \fBc360\fR for the G512 X 98 and
\fBc336\fR for the G213 Prodigy; \fBsaved_colors\fR, the colors of the Saved
colors row, one list that every color picker in the window shows, at most 24;
\fBactive_game_profile\fR, the game
profile loaded by hand, which the next start loads again; \fBmouse_dpi\fR,
each mouse's DPI by product id (see \fBmouse\-dpi\fR); \fBmouse_rate\fR, the
report rate set live on each mouse whose onboard profile is not written, by
product id in the same way (see \fBmouse\-rate\fR); \fBmouse_selected\fR, the
product id of the mouse last chosen with \fBmouse\-select\fR or on the window's
switcher, which the next start selects again, or null; the switches
\fBmouse_sync_color\fR, \fBheadset_sync_color\fR,
\fBlights_off_when_locked\fR, \fBdpi_notices\fR, \fBmouse_host_mode\fR and
\fBtray\fR, and \fBidle_dim_after\fR, \fBidle_off_after\fR and
\fBnever_dim\fR, which \fBmouse\-sync\fR, \fBheadset\-sync\fR,
\fBlock\-lights\fR, \fBdpi\-notices\fR, \fBmouse\-host\fR, \fBtray\fR and
\fBidle\fR set;
\fBkeyboard_link_notices\fR, true by default and set only by hand, which has
the keyboard's receiver send its link notices; \fBbattery_notices\fR, which
\fBbattery\-notices\fR sets, each device's low-battery notices, \fB30/15\fR (below 30% and 15%),
\fB10/5\fR (below 10% and 5%), \fBoff\fR (none, the tray marking a battery
below 15% all the same) or \fB30/15\-urgent\fR (as \fB30/15\fR, the
second notice at critical urgency, but on a device whose node the kernel
gives a power supply under GNOME or KDE Plasma, which warn of it), keyed by a
keyboard's or a mouse's model as \fBbrightness\fR and \fBmouse\fR are,
or by a headset's key in
.IR spatial.json ,
such as \fBa50\fR or \fBg933\fR, each in any case; a device left out takes
\fB30/15\fR, and a key or a value this version does not know is kept, the
value read as \fB30/15\fR and named once in the journal;
\fBnever_dim_with_game_profile\fR, which \fBidle\fR also sets, false by
default, the switch for no idle dimming while a game profile is loaded; \fBsync_keyboard\fR, which
\fBsync\-keyboard\fR sets, the keyboard model that leads (see
\fBsync\-keyboard\fR for what follows it), by its key, or null for
the one connected first since the daemon started of those still connected;
\fBfollow_leader\fR,
which \fBkeyboard\-follow\fR sets, each keyboard model's follow switch by its
key, true or false, a model not named following; and \fBprofiles\fR, the three M
profiles keyed "1" to "3". Each has a \fBname\fR, \fBkeys\fR and \fBshift\fR
(the G-key bindings, and the G-Shift layer's), \fBmouse\fR (the mouse bindings,
keyed by mouse model, the model's first product id such as 4099 for the G502 X
PLUS, each by button name; a model or a button left out takes its defaults),
\fBmouse_shift\fR (the mouse bindings of the G-Shift layer, keyed as
\fBmouse\fR is; a button left out, or bound to null, has none of its own
there) and \fBlighting\fR, keyed by keyboard model as \fBbrightness\fR is,
each with \fBmode\fR, \fBcolor\fR (the Static color), \fBsecond_color\fR
(pulsar\-point's second), \fBbackground\fR,
\fBkeys\fR (the painted keys), \fBeffect\fR, \fBparams\fR, \fBspeeds\fR (each
animation's that takes one) and \fBdirection\fR (wave's).
A version 1 file, which kept one brightness for the whole file and one
lighting in each M profile, still loads, both as the G915's; a version 1 or 2 file's one set of
G4 to G9 bindings loads as the G502 X PLUS's. A file of version 1, 2 or 3 is
saved as version 4 at its first save, after a copy of it goes to
.IR backup/config.v1.json ,
.I backup/config.v2.json
or
.IR backup/config.v3.json .
Gearbench 1.2.1 and every release before it do not read version 4:
each starts on the defaults and writes nothing over the file.
A
.I config.json
that is not valid JSON or not a JSON object, has a version other than 1 to
4, or holds a field of the wrong type or a value the daemon refuses, such as
a brightness above 100, leaves the daemon on the defaults at its start, and
nothing is saved over the file. So does one larger than 1 MiB, one that names
a field twice in one object, one that is neither a regular file nor a link to
one, such as a named pipe, or a link to a file that is not there. Found at a
.BR reload ,
such a file changes nothing. A save that would make
.I config.json
larger than 1 MiB is refused, and the file stays as it was; a save writes
through a link to the file it names.
.TP
.I ~/.config/gearbench/profiles/
Game profiles, one JSON file each, named after the profile: its name in
lowercase, with its letters and digits kept and each run of anything else
between them made one hyphen, as in
.I arc\-raiders.json
for Arc: Raiders. A name with no letter or digit gets
.I profile\-
and a short hash instead, and one that comes out longer than 200 bytes is cut
short and given a hash at its end.
Each file is one JSON object: \fBversion\fR, 4; \fBname\fR; \fBprocesses\fR,
the programs that load it; \fBlighting\fR, keyed by keyboard model as
.IR config.json 's
is, each with \fBmode\fR, \fBeffect\fR, \fBspeeds\fR, \fBdirection\fR,
\fBbase\fR (the Static color), \fBsecond_color\fR (pulsar\-point's second),
\fBbackground\fR and \fBkeys\fR (the painted keys); \fBbindings\fR and
\fBshift\fR (the G-key bindings, and the G-Shift layer's); \fBmouse\fR and
\fBmouse_shift\fR (the mouse bindings, and the G-Shift layer's, by mouse model, as
.IR config.json 's);
\fBmouse_tuning\fR, its own DPI stages and report rate per mouse
model; and \fBmouse_color\fR, the color shown, while it is loaded, by a mouse and a
headset's lights that follow the keyboard, or null for the keyboard's.
A version 1 file, which kept one lighting at its top, without
\fBsecond_color\fR, still loads, as the G915's; a version 1 or 2 file's one
set of mouse bindings loads as the G502 X PLUS's.
Gearbench 1.2.1 and every release before it do not read version 4: each
skips the file and writes nothing over it.
Two profiles whose names give the same file name, such as C and C++, or
Half\-Life and Half Life, count as one name. Of their two files, the one with
that file name is read, else the first by file name, and the other is skipped,
with
.BI "\%skipping game profile " file ": the same name as " other ", which is read instead"
in the journal, until the profile in it is given a name of its own or one of
the two files goes. Renaming a file changes which is read by the same rule:
the skipped one comes back under that file name, or, while neither file has
it, under one that comes first.
.TP
.I ~/.config/gearbench/macros/
Macros, one JSON file each, named after the macro as a game profile's file
is. Of two files whose macros' names give the same file name, one is read by
the rule for game profiles, and the other is skipped, with
.BI "\%skipping macro " file ": the same name as " other ", which is read instead"
in the journal.
A game profile's or macro's file is read only if it is a regular file, or a
link to one, of at most 1 MiB, that names each field once in each object, and
is skipped otherwise. It is skipped too
when the game profile's or macro's name runs over 1,000 characters, or when
that name or a program the game profile names holds a control character or a
lone surrogate, half of a pair written as a JSON escape such as
.BR \eudce9 .
The name is measured and checked with the white space at its two ends cut
off, a tab or a newline included, so a name that ends in a newline is read
without it; a program's name is checked as written.
A save or rename that would make one of these files larger than 1 MiB is
refused, and the file stays as it was.
A save writes through a link to the file it names, and a rename of a file
that is a link moves the link to the new file name and writes through it.
.TP
.I ~/.config/gearbench/backup/
Backups of the mouse's onboard profile directory and profile sectors, one file
per sector per write:
.IR .bin ,
or
.I .bad\-read
for a sector whose CRC did not verify, which a restore never picks, nor a
.I .bin.unchanged
that a write or a restore, done or failed, set aside because it left
profile 1 as it was.
.B mouse\-restore
writes back the newest
.I .bin
of sector 0x0001 only if it is a regular file, or a link to one, of the
sector's size that passes its CRC;
.B RECOVERY
below lists each refusal.
Beside them,
.IR config.v1.json ,
.IR config.v2.json ,
.IR config.v3.json ,
and, under its own file name in
.IR profiles.v1/ ,
.I profiles.v2/
or
.IR profiles.v3/ ,
each game profile's file: a copy of each file as an older Gearbench
wrote it, in version 1, 2 or 3, made once, just before its
first save in version 4, and never written over; and
.IR g432\-eq.v1.json ,
the same for
.I g432\-eq.json
before its first save in its version 2. Another model's is named by its key,
.I g733\-eq.v1.json
for the G733. A copy that cannot be made refuses the
save, with the copy's path and the cause, and the file stays as it was. To go back to such a
Gearbench, copy each one over its file but an
.I \-eq.v1.json
copy: leave the presets file it was made from, such as
.IR g432\-eq.json ,
as it is, since every release that reads one reads its version 2.
.TP
.I ~/.config/gearbench/spatial.json
Gearbench Spatial's head and room, and the headset picked, which it and the
three outputs of
.B headset\-outputs
play to while more than one is plugged in, saved by
.B headset\-spatial
and the window's Headset tab, as in
\fB{"head": "kemar", "room": "immersive", "headset": "g432"}\fR,
with no headset key for none picked.
A file that is missing, cannot be read, is not valid JSON, names a field
twice in one object or is not a JSON object reads as ku100, competitive and
none picked; a missing or unknown
head, room or headset, such as \fB"KEMAR"\fR, reads as its own default, with
no warning. Whether it is on is the unit's enable link, kept nowhere else.
.TP
.I ~/.config/gearbench/g432\-eq.json
Logitech G432 Equalizer's presets, saved by
.B g432\-eq
and the window's Headset tab: \fBversion\fR, 2; \fBactive\fR, the number of
the preset it plays, from 1; and \fBpresets\fR, 3 to 16 objects in order,
the shipped three, then those saved as new, each with a \fBname\fR and its
gains, as in
\fB{"name": "Mine", "bands": [0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 4, 0, 0, 0, 0], "bass": 3, "treble": 0}\fR.
A preset's name is 1 to 49 bytes in UTF\-8, not spaces alone, with no
control character, as
an A50 preset's is.
A file that is missing reads as the three defaults, Media, Gaming and
Everyday, with Everyday active; a file keeps the names it holds, such as
Rock and Alt and FPS Spatial, Gearbench 1.1.0's names for the first two.
A shipped preset that is not an
object reads as its default, a name it refuses as the default's; a gain
that is not a whole number from \-12 to +12 reads as 0 on its own, a list of
bands of another length as every band at 0, and an active number as the
preset it numbered in the file, or as 3 when that one was left out or is
none.
A file that cannot be read, is not valid JSON, names a field twice in one
object, is not a JSON object, is
larger than 1 MiB, has a \fBversion\fR other than 2, or holds \fBpresets\fR
with no \fBversion\fR plays the three
defaults and is never written over: each change is refused with the file's
path and the cause, and the report says so on a
.B warning:
line on standard error, until the file is fixed or moved away. So is one
that is, or sits in a folder that is, a link to something not there, such
as a dotfiles folder not mounted. One that
holds a preset saved as new that is not an object or has a name it
refuses, or more than 16 presets, plays those it read, and one whose
presets are neither a list nor null the three defaults; each change to either
is refused in the same way, naming what was left out, until it is fixed.
A version 1 file,
the gains alone with no \fBversion\fR, reads
as preset 1, named Custom and active, unless every gain is 0, and is saved
as version 2 at its first save, after a copy of it goes to
.IR backup/g432\-eq.v1.json .
Whether it is on is the transient unit running, kept nowhere else.
Each other model of the G432's family with no equalizer of its own keeps its
presets the same way in a file named by its key,
.I g733\-eq.json
for the G733.
.TP
.I ~/.config/gearbench/a50\-eq.json
The A50's preset library, saved by
.BR headset\-eq " " \-\-save\-as ,
.BR headset\-library ,
.BR headset\-import " " \-\-library
and the window's Headset tab, never by the daemon: \fBversion\fR, 1, and
\fBpresets\fR, up to 16 objects in order, each a \fBname\fR, its five
\fBgains\fR and its five \fBbands\fR, as
.B headset\-eq
sends them, as in
\fB{"name": "Night", "gains": [2, \-5, 3, 4, 2], "bands": [{"band": 1, "freq": 80, "width": null}, ...]}\fR.
A \fBpresets\fR that is neither a list nor null, a preset that is not an object, has a
value the base station would refuse, lacks one of the five bands or gives one of them twice, and those past the
16th are left out:
the rest are listed and can be written to a slot, but every change of the
library is refused, saying what was left out, until the file is fixed. A file
that is missing reads as an empty library; one that
cannot be read, is not valid JSON, names a field twice in one object, is not
a JSON object, is larger than 1 MiB, or has no \fBversion\fR or one other
than 1 is refused, and nothing is saved over it until it is fixed or
moved away. So is one that is, or sits in a folder that is, a link to
something not there, such as a dotfiles folder not mounted.
.TP
.I ~/.config/gearbench/a50x\-eq.json
The preset library of the A50 X; each other Centurion headset's is a file of
its own, \fBa50gen5\-eq.json\fR, \fBpro_x_2\-eq.json\fR, \fBg522\-eq.json\fR
and \fBg325\-eq.json\fR, in the same layout. Written by
.B audio\-presets
and the window's Headset tab, never by the daemon: \fBversion\fR, 1, and
\fBpresets\fR, up to 16 objects in order, each a \fBname\fR, its
\fBinstance\fR,
.B headphones
or
.BR microphone ,
one the model has, and its \fBbands\fR, ten, or the PRO X 2's five, as in
\fB{"name": "Bass", "instance": "headphones", "bands": [{"freq": 20, "q": 22, "gain": 6.0}, ...]}\fR.
A file that is missing reads as the model's three starting presets (see
.BR audio\-presets ).
A preset whose name is not 1 to 49 bytes in UTF\-8, not spaces alone, with
no control character, as an A50 preset's is, or that has a value the headset would
refuse is left out: the rest are listed and can be picked, but every change
of the library is refused, saying what was left out, until the file is
fixed. A file that does not read is left as it is, as for
.IR a50\-eq.json .
.TP
.I ~/.config/gearbench/g933\-eq.json
The preset library of the equalizer in the G933; the G935's and the G633's
are \fBg935\-eq.json\fR and \fBg633\-eq.json\fR, in the same layout.
Written by
.B g432\-headset\-presets
and the window's Headset tab, never by the daemon: \fBversion\fR, 1, and
\fBpresets\fR, up to 16 objects in order, each a \fBname\fR and its
\fBbands\fR, 1 to 15, as in
\fB{"name": "Bass", "bands": [{"freq": 32, "gain": 6}, ...]}\fR.
A file that is missing reads as the model's three starting presets (see
.BR g432\-headset\-presets ).
A preset that does not read is left out: the rest are listed and can be
picked, but every change of the library is refused, saying what was left
out, until the file is fixed. A file that does not read is left as it is,
as for
.IR a50\-eq.json .
.TP
.I ~/.config/gearbench/g933\-eq\-picked.json
The preset picked on the G933, \fBg935\-eq\-picked.json\fR and
\fBg633\-eq\-picked.json\fR on the others: \fBversion\fR, 1, its
\fBname\fR and its \fBbands\fR, as a library's preset. Written by the
daemon at a pick, sent again at each attach and each time the headset comes
back, and removed by the window's Flat and by
.BR "g432\-headset\-eq \-\-flat" .
One that is, or sits in a folder that is, a link to something not there,
such as a dotfiles folder not mounted, is not read as none picked: an
attach sends no gains and says so in the journal. Behind a folder's link
.B \-\-flat
answers that the pick could not be removed; a link to the file itself it
removes.
.TP
.I $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/gearbench.sock
The daemon's control socket. It falls back to
.I /run/user/<uid>/gearbench.sock
when
.B XDG_RUNTIME_DIR
is unset, empty or a relative path. A request is read to end of file, so a client shuts its
write side once it has sent one. A request over 1 MiB, one that sends nothing
for 0.5 s before its end, or one not sent whole within 2 s of the daemon
accepting the connection is refused with a
.B bad request
reply and the connection closed.
.TP
.I $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/gearbench.lock
The daemon's single-instance lock, beside the socket, held while it runs and
left in place when it stops. While a daemon holds it, another refuses to
start.
.TP
.I /usr/lib/systemd/user/gearbench.service
The user unit that runs the daemon. Before each start it runs
.BR "install \-d \-m 0700" ,
which creates
.I ~/.config/gearbench/
when it is missing and sets its mode to 0700 when it is there. Manage
it with
.BR "systemctl \-\-user" ,
never with
.BR "sudo systemctl" ,
which does not see a user unit. Installed from a git checkout by
.B install.sh
it lands in
.I ~/.config/systemd/user/
instead.
.TP
.I /usr/lib/systemd/user/gearbench\-spatial.service
.TQ
.I ~/.config/systemd/user/gearbench\-spatial.service
The user unit that runs Gearbench Spatial from each login once switched on.
Its process follows PipeWire with
.B pw\-dump \-\-monitor
and, while a headset it plays to is there, runs the chain as a PipeWire
client of its own on
.IR gearbench\-spatial.conf ,
in the runtime directory.
It starts the chain again when the headset or the output the chain plays
to changes, as on the G432 when Logitech G432 Equalizer starts or stops.
The package installs the first and
.B install.sh
writes the second. Neither enables it;
.B headset\-spatial on
and the window's Headset tab do. Its log is
.BR "journalctl \-\-user _SYSTEMD_USER_UNIT=gearbench\-spatial.service" ;
.B \-u gearbench\-spatial
adds systemd's lines about the unit, and any line another program of the
user logs under its name.
.TP
.I $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/gearbench\-g432\-eq.json
The G432's own volume from before Logitech G432 Equalizer held it at 0 dB,
and whether it was muted, written by
.B g432\-eq on
and the window. While it keeps that volume,
.B g432\-eq off
ends the hold, and puts that volume back, muted if the G432 was, only when
the equalizer shows no volume and the G432 is still at 0 dB. The file also
keeps whether the equalizer was the default output when it went off with the
G432 unplugged, or on a profile with no output, for the next
.B on
to make it the default again. Once it keeps neither, it is removed, as by a
.B g432\-eq off
that ends the hold with the G432's output there; otherwise it goes with the
runtime directory, when every session of the account has ended or, with
lingering on, at a restart; the hold goes then too, since WirePlumber never
saves the held level.
In a runtime directory the daemon refuses (see
.BR XDG_RUNTIME_DIR ),
.BR "g432\-eq on" ,
.B g432\-eq off
and
.B headset\-noise on
refuse and use no file there.
Another model's equalizer keeps its record in a file named by its key,
.I gearbench\-g733\-eq.json
for the G733.
.TP
.I $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/gearbench\-g432\-eq.conf
Logitech G432 Equalizer's PipeWire configuration, written by
.B g432\-eq on
and the window at each switch\-on from the active preset's gains, aimed at
the G432's output, and run by
.IR gearbench\-g432\-eq.service .
Another model's is named by its key,
.I gearbench\-g733\-eq.conf
for the G733.
.TP
.I $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/gearbench\-g432\-mic.conf
The G432's noise suppression's PipeWire configuration, written by
.B headset\-noise on
and the window at each switch\-on, aimed at the G432's microphone, and run
by
.IR gearbench\-g432\-mic.service .
Another headset's is named by its key,
.I gearbench\-g733\-mic.conf
for the G733.
.TP
.I $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/gearbench\-g432\-mic.json
The default input saved at the switch\-on, or the headset's own microphone where there was none, as in
\fB{"input": "alsa_input.usb\-..."}\fR, written by
.B headset\-noise on
and the window before they start the unit, and given back by
.B headset\-noise off
while the input is the default. It goes with the runtime directory, as the unit does.
Another headset's is named by its key,
.I gearbench\-g733\-mic.json
for the G733.
.TP
.I ~/.config/gearbench/outputs.json
The balance, saved each time a balance word moves it, and the default output
saved when Outputs was switched on, as in
\fB{"balance": 106, "default": "alsa_output.usb\-..."}\fR, with no default key
for none saved. A file that is missing, does not read or is not a JSON object
reads as the balance 128 and no default; a balance that is not a whole number
from 0 to 255 reads as 128, and a default that is not text, is empty, holds a
quote, a backslash or a control character from U+0000 to U+001F, or is
Gearbench Game's, Chat's or Media's own sink, as none, each
on its own and with no warning. The next save writes the file whole over one
that did not read. While the unit runs its process is the file's only writer;
.B headset\-outputs on
and the window's Headset tab write the default before they start the unit.
.TP
.I /usr/lib/systemd/user/gearbench\-outputs.service
.TQ
.I ~/.config/systemd/user/gearbench\-outputs.service
The user unit that runs Gearbench Game, Chat and Media from each login once
switched on. Its process binds the socket below, follows PipeWire with
.B pw\-dump \-\-monitor
and runs the three as one PipeWire client on the configuration below, which
it never restarts for a new output: it moves each with
.BR pw\-metadata .
The package installs the first and
.B install.sh
writes the second. Neither enables it nor restarts it;
.B headset\-outputs on
and the window's Headset tab enable it and start it, or only enable it while
it runs, so a running unit takes a changed file at its next start. Its log is
.BR "journalctl \-\-user _SYSTEMD_USER_UNIT=gearbench\-outputs.service" ;
.B \-u gearbench\-outputs
adds systemd's lines about the unit, and any line another program of the
user logs under its name.
.TP
.I $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/gearbench\-outputs.sock
The Outputs unit's datagram socket, mode 0600, for the balance's words:
.BR game ,
.B chat
and
.B set
.IR N ,
N from 0 to 255, at most 16 bytes. Anything else is refused, and logged at
most once a minute.
In a runtime directory the daemon refuses (see
.BR XDG_RUNTIME_DIR ),
the unit's process binds no socket and the unit fails.
.TP
.I $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/gearbench\-outputs.conf
The Outputs unit's PipeWire configuration, written at each start of the unit,
aimed at the outputs the three play on then.
.TP
.I $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/gearbench\-spatial.conf
Gearbench Spatial's PipeWire configuration, written each time the chain is
started, for the headset and the output it plays to, from the head and room in
.I spatial.json
and the template and audio files in
.I /usr/share/gearbench/spatial/
(from a git checkout, its
.IR spatial/ ).
It goes with the runtime directory: when every session of the account has
ended, or, with lingering on, at a restart.
.TP
.I /usr/share/gearbench/pack/
The profiles and macros pack, read by
.B import\-pack
and the window, never written (from a git checkout, its
.IR pack/ ).
.TP
.I \%/usr/share/alsa\-card\-profile/mixer/profile\-sets/\:gearbench\-astro\-a50.conf
.TQ
.I \%~/.config/alsa\-card\-profile/mixer/profile\-sets/\:gearbench\-astro\-a50.conf
.TQ
.I \%/usr/share/wireplumber/wireplumber.conf.d/\:60\-gearbench\-astro\-a50.conf
.TQ
.I \%~/.config/wireplumber/wireplumber.conf.d/\:60\-gearbench\-astro\-a50.conf
.TQ
.I \%/usr/share/alsa\-card\-profile/mixer/profile\-sets/\:gearbench\-astro\-a50x.conf
.TQ
.I \%~/.config/alsa\-card\-profile/mixer/profile\-sets/\:gearbench\-astro\-a50x.conf
.TQ
.I \%/usr/share/wireplumber/wireplumber.conf.d/\:60\-gearbench\-astro\-a50x.conf
.TQ
.I \%~/.config/wireplumber/wireplumber.conf.d/\:60\-gearbench\-astro\-a50x.conf
The profile sets of the A50 Gen 4 and the A50 X, which give each base station's Game
and Chat an output each and the A50 X's Stream Out an input, and each one's
WirePlumber rule, which has its base station use its set. The package
installs them in
.I /usr/share
and does not restart WirePlumber;
.B install.sh
puts them in
.I ~/.config
and restarts a running WirePlumber when one of these files, or of the G432's
rules below, changed, which stops all audio for a moment. WirePlumber reads
them when it starts with the user manager: at a login once every session of
the account has ended or, with lingering on, at a restart, or on
.BR "systemctl \-\-user restart wireplumber" .
.TP
.I \%/usr/share/wireplumber/wireplumber.conf.d/\:60\-gearbench\-logitech\-g432.conf
.TQ
.I \%~/.config/wireplumber/wireplumber.conf.d/\:60\-gearbench\-logitech\-g432.conf
.TQ
.I \%/usr/share/wireplumber/wireplumber.conf.d/\:60\-gearbench\-hidpp\-headsets.conf
.TQ
.I \%~/.config/wireplumber/wireplumber.conf.d/\:60\-gearbench\-hidpp\-headsets.conf
The WirePlumber rules that give the G432's adapter, and the rest of the
G432's family, PipeWire's own
.I analog\-only.conf
profile set: one analog output, whose volume on the G432 is the adapter's
own, and the microphone, with no digital output beside them. That the rest
of the family's cards have the G432's outputs is assumed. They are installed
and read as the files above.
.TP
.I \%/usr/share/dbus\-1/services/\:io.github.gearbench.Gearbench.service
What D-Bus starts the window from, as
.BR "\%gearbench\-ctl gui \%\-\-gapplication\-service" ,
when the app grid or the tray icon asks for
.B io.github.gearbench.Gearbench
and no window runs: the desktop entry is
.BR DBusActivatable ,
and the daemon, a sandboxed service, never starts the window itself.
Where GTK 4 and libadwaita for Python are installed,
.B install.sh
writes it to
.I \%~/.local/share/dbus\-1/services/
instead, with the launcher's path.
.TP
.I /usr/share/gnome\-shell/extensions/gearbench\-focus@gearbench.github.io/
The Gearbench focus extension for GNOME Shell, which sends the daemon
the focused window's
process id over D\-Bus, for game profiles. The package installs it; after the
next login, the window's Turn on, or
.B \%gnome\-extensions \%enable \%gearbench\-focus@gearbench.github.io\c
, turns it on.
.B install.sh
puts it in
.I ~/.local/share/gnome\-shell/extensions/
instead and enables it itself; GNOME loads it at the next login.
.B install.sh
also removes the copy an install from before 1.1.0 left in that folder under
the extension's old name, and takes that name out of GNOME Shell's lists of
enabled and disabled extensions.
.TP
.I /usr/lib/udev/rules.d/70\-g915.rules
.TQ
.I /usr/lib/udev/rules.d/70\-g515.rules
.TQ
.I /usr/lib/udev/rules.d/70\-g502x.rules
.TQ
.I /usr/lib/udev/rules.d/70\-g305.rules
.TQ
.I /usr/lib/udev/rules.d/70\-g502hero.rules
.TQ
.I /usr/lib/udev/rules.d/70\-g502x\-lightspeed.rules
.TQ
.I /usr/lib/udev/rules.d/70\-pro\-x\-superlight.rules
.TQ
.I /usr/lib/udev/rules.d/70\-g502x\-wired.rules
.TQ
.I /usr/lib/udev/rules.d/70\-g502x\-falcon.rules
.TQ
.I /usr/lib/udev/rules.d/70\-g502\-lightspeed.rules
.TQ
.I /usr/lib/udev/rules.d/70\-pro\-wireless.rules
.TQ
.I /usr/lib/udev/rules.d/70\-g903\-hero.rules
.TQ
.I /usr/lib/udev/rules.d/70\-pro\-x\-superlight\-2.rules
.TQ
.I /usr/lib/udev/rules.d/70\-pro\-x2\-superstrike.rules
.TQ
.I /usr/lib/udev/rules.d/70\-g203\-lightsync.rules
.TQ
.I /usr/lib/udev/rules.d/70\-astro\-a50.rules
.TQ
.I /usr/lib/udev/rules.d/70\-g432.rules
.TQ
.I /usr/lib/udev/rules.d/70\-hidpp\-headsets.rules
.TQ
.I /usr/lib/udev/rules.d/70\-astro\-a50x.rules
.TQ
.I /usr/lib/udev/rules.d/70\-astro\-a50\-gen5.rules
.TQ
.I /usr/lib/udev/rules.d/70\-lightspeed\-headsets.rules
.TQ
.I /usr/lib/udev/rules.d/70\-g915\-tkl.rules
.TQ
.I /usr/lib/udev/rules.d/70\-g815.rules
.TQ
.I /usr/lib/udev/rules.d/70\-pro\-x\-tkl.rules
.TQ
.I /usr/lib/udev/rules.d/70\-g213.rules
.TQ
.I /usr/lib/udev/rules.d/70\-g915\-x.rules
.TQ
.I /usr/lib/udev/rules.d/70\-g316\-x.rules
.TQ
.I /usr/lib/udev/rules.d/70\-g512\-x.rules
Without the rules the daemon cannot open the devices.
The udev rules tag with
.B uaccess
the vendor HID++ interface of each keyboard and each mouse plugged in by its
cable but the G815, G213 Prodigy, G502 HERO, G502 X and G203 LIGHTSYNC, and
of the G915's and G915 TKL's receivers.
They tag each mouse's own hidraw node through its receiver and the G502
HERO's, G502 X's and G203 LIGHTSYNC's interface 01, which carry beside HID++
the keystrokes the mouse's onboard profile binds to its buttons (through the
receiver, going by the kernel's
hid\-logitech\-dj.c).
Of the headsets they tag:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
the HID interface of the A50 Gen 4's base station, which carries its
settings and no keystrokes;
.IP \(bu 2
interface 03 of each headset of the G432's family on its adapter, cable or
receiver, which on the G432's adapter carries its sidetone, volume wheel and
mute and no keystrokes;
.IP \(bu 2
interface 08 of the A50 X's and the A50 Gen 5's base stations, their control
interface;
.IP \(bu 2
interface 03 of the PRO X 2 LIGHTSPEED's, G522 LIGHTSPEED's and G325
LIGHTSPEED's receivers and of the G522 on its cable, their control interface
beside the headset's volume and mute keys.
.RE
.IP
The G502 X PLUS's event node through its receiver and its pointer's node on
its cable, interface 00, are
.B root:gearbench
0640 instead: a member of the system group
.B gearbench
reads them and cannot write events to them. Going by the kernel's source, the
one through the receiver also carries the keystrokes of a button the onboard
profile binds to a key. The evdev ioctls ignore the open mode, though, so a
member's program that has a node open can still grab it or remap its
buttons. Going by the kernel's source, that lasts until a replug. The package
creates the group and adds no one: run
.BR "sudo \%usermod \-aG \%gearbench \%$USER" ;
.B install.sh
creates it and adds the account that runs it. The daemon has the groups its
user manager started with, so an account added since reads the nodes once it
has logged out of every session and back in, or, with lingering on, once the
machine restarts. Until then, with host mode off or not taken,
.B status
says so under its problems.
.IP
These grant with
.B uaccess
a keyboard's node that carries what is typed on the keyboard beside HID++:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
.IR 70\-g915\-tkl.rules :
the G915 TKL's node of its own behind its receiver, on a kernel that gives it
one;
.IP \(bu 2
.IR 70\-g515.rules :
the G515 LIGHTSPEED TKL's node behind its receiver;
.IP \(bu 2
.IR 70\-pro\-x\-tkl.rules :
the PRO X TKL LIGHTSPEED's node behind its receiver;
.IP \(bu 2
.IR 70\-g915\-x.rules :
the G915 X LIGHTSPEED's and G915 X LIGHTSPEED TKL's nodes, each behind
its receiver;
.IP \(bu 2
.IR 70\-g815.rules :
the G815's interface 01;
.IP \(bu 2
.IR 70\-g213.rules :
the G213 Prodigy's interface 01.
.RE
.IP
For the G815's node this goes by a published report descriptor, the G213
Prodigy's is taken to be the same, and for those behind a receiver it goes by
the kernel's
hid\-logitech\-dj.c.
The vendor HID++ interface of the G515 TKL and the G515 LIGHTSPEED TKL on
their cable, and of the G915 X, G316 X and G512 X models, carries each press
and release of the modifier keys too, from the time their FN reports are
turned on until the keyboard loses power: switched off, or unplugged where
it has no battery. A wired G515 TKL did so on 2026-10-21 and a G915 X
LIGHTSPEED on 2026-10-23, which kept them on through a replug of its cable;
the others are taken to. The daemon turns
them on at each setup (HID++ feature 0x8051 function 3), and any program
with the node can turn them on itself. The daemon reads FN alone.
Through the same node a program can also write the keyboard's custom
assignments (HID++ feature 0x8101) so that keys report there (feature 0x1B05)
in place of typing, read them and type them back through
.IR /dev/uinput .
F1 to F4 did so on a wired G515 TKL on 2026-10-22, and G1 to G9 on a
G915 X LIGHTSPEED on its cable on 2026-10-23; other keys are taken to.
The daemon writes custom assignments to the G915 X LIGHTSPEED alone, for its
G keys.
With its custom assignments off the G915 X LIGHTSPEED sent no report of FN
held on 2026-10-23, nor of Ctrl, Shift, Alt or Super held on 2026-10-30,
and the others are taken to do the same.
.IP
.B uaccess
gives a node to the account whose session has the seat. While another account
has the seat, or a text console where this account is not logged in, the
daemon cannot open a node it does not already hold, and
.B status
says so under its problems until this account has the seat again.
On a node that carries what is typed, a program one account opened while it
had the seat reads what a later account types: the ACL
.B uaccess
gives is checked only at open, so a program that already holds a node keeps it.
On a machine several people use, limit the node to a group's accounts: in a
copy of the node's rule file in
.IR /etc/udev/rules.d/ ,
put the group grant that the comment in
.I 70\-gearbench\-uinput.rules
gives in place of the tag. The group's programs then read the node from any
session, whoever has the seat, so put only your own account in the group.
.I GUIDE.md
(see
.BR "SEE ALSO" ),
in its section The udev rules, gives the steps.
.IP
.B install.sh
puts the same files, and the one below, in
.I /usr/local/lib/udev/rules.d/
instead.
.TP
.I /usr/lib/udev/rules.d/70\-gearbench\-uinput.rules
Tags
.I /dev/uinput
with
.BR uaccess ,
the same line the \%steam\-devices package's
.I \%60\-steam\-input.rules
carries. With it any program the user runs can create a virtual input
device, a keyboard included. The grant also crosses accounts: a program that
opened the node keeps its keyboard after a user switch, or after a logout
that leaves its processes running, and can type into a later account's
session, a sudo prompt included. On a machine several people use, the rule's
own comment gives a group grant to use instead. A member's programs can then
open the node from any session, whoever has the seat, so put only your own
account in the group. With the \%steam\-devices package installed, its rule's
tag still grants the node to the account with the seat; the comment says what
to do about it.
.TP
.I /dev/uinput
Where the virtual keyboard and mouse that the bindings and macros type
through are made. The user needs write access to it, which systemd's own rules
do not give and the rule above does, as an ACL. Without it the bindings type
nothing, a
G915 TKL or PRO X TKL LIGHTSPEED is left on its onboard profile, and
.B status
says so on its
.B uinput
line;
the daemon tries again every 30 seconds and at the next bind.
With the rule installed, the line says
.B none ready
where uinput is a module not loaded yet: the rule applies at the next boot,
or at once after
.B sudo \%modprobe \%uinput
and
.BR "sudo \%udevadm \%trigger \%\-\-subsystem\-match=misc \%\-\-sysname\-match=uinput \%\-\-action=add" .
.TP
.I /usr/lib/udev/hwdb.d/71\-gearbench\-mouse\-dpi.hwdb
Gives each product id of each mouse model Gearbench drives a
.B MOUSE_DPI
of
.BR 1000@1000 .
libinput scales the motion of a mouse whose hwdb DPI is above 1000 down to what
it would be at 1000, as systemd's
.I /usr/lib/udev/hwdb.d/70\-mouse.hwdb
has it for the G502 HERO, at 2400, so without this file the pointer moves
slower than the DPI Gearbench sets. The name sorts after
.IR 70\-mouse.hwdb ,
so its
.B MOUSE_DPI
takes the place of that file's for the same mouse. This package rebuilds the
hwdb udev reads before its udev reload, at each install, upgrade and removal:
.I /etc/udev/hwdb.bin
where it is there, which udev reads first, and
.I /usr/lib/udev/hwdb.bin
otherwise. A mouse the compositor holds takes the entry, or loses it, at its
next plug-in or login.
.B install.sh
puts the file in
.I /etc/udev/hwdb.d/
instead, as
.B systemd\-hwdb
reads no folder under
.IR /usr/local ,
and rebuilds the hwdb before its udev reload; a mouse plugged in then takes the
entry at its next plug-in or login, when the compositor opens it again.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B HOME
Where
.I ~
is: the configuration's base without an absolute
.BR XDG_CONFIG_HOME ;
.I ~/.local/state/wireplumber
without
.BR XDG_STATE_HOME ;
.IR ~/.local/share/gnome\-shell/extensions/ ,
where the window also looks for the focus extension, without
.BR XDG_DATA_HOME ;
and
.I ~/.Xauthority
without
.BR XAUTHORITY .
.TP
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
The base for the configuration directory, and for the autostart directory
whose programs
.B apps
leaves out, a launcher aside. Defaults to
.I ~/.config
when unset or not an absolute path,
giving
.I ~/.config/gearbench/
and
.IR ~/.config/autostart/ .
Where the service's sandbox applies, the one folder under the home folder
the daemon may write is
.I gearbench
under the systemd user manager's own
.BR XDG_CONFIG_HOME .
A value set only in
.I ~/.config/environment.d
or by the desktop session reaches the daemon and not the manager: the daemon
logs an error at its start, and where the sandbox applies it saves nothing.
Set it for the manager itself, with
.B \%ManagerEnvironment=
in
.IR \%~/.config/systemd/user.conf ,
or not at all.
A command run as root saves nothing below a directory or link another account
could change, such as the user's own that
.B su
without its dash and
.B sudo \-E
leave set: run it as the account whose settings they are.
.TP
.B XDG_STATE_HOME
Where the Outputs unit's process looks for WirePlumber's stream\-properties,
which it reads at each start: in its
.I wireplumber
folder, or in
.I ~/.local/state/wireplumber
when it is unset or empty.
.TP
.B XDG_RUNTIME_DIR
The directory holding the control socket, the daemon's lock, the Outputs
unit's socket and PipeWire configuration, Gearbench Spatial's PipeWire
configuration, and the
equalizer files of the G432 and its family and each headset's noise
suppression files. For the
control socket and the lock it defaults to
.IR /run/user/<uid> ,
which a relative path also gets, as the XDG Base Directory specification says;
.B g432\-eq on
and
.B headset\-noise on
refuse to switch, and the Outputs unit fails at its start, while it is unset,
empty or relative.
For
.B probe
and
.B report
under sudo, run0 or pkexec, see
.BR SUDO_UID .
It must be the user's own: the daemon refuses to start in a directory another
account owns or other accounts may write in, through its group or as any
account, such as
.IR /tmp .
It also refuses one below, or reached through, a directory or link that an
account other than the user or root owns, or a directory other accounts may
write in without the sticky bit, since another account could
swap it for its own.
Inside its systemd unit's user namespace
.RB ( PrivateUsers=yes )
the daemon cannot tell root's files from another account's, so the unit makes
the same check before each start, outside the namespace, and a refusal there
fails the start with the daemon's words.
The Gearbench Spatial and Outputs units keep the same rule for the directory of
their PipeWire configuration, and a refusal fails the unit at its start.
A command sends no request for the daemon to a socket another account's
process listens on. One run as root under sudo, run0 or pkexec from another
account sends its requests only to a socket that account listens on, and to none
of root's.
One run as root with no such account sends none to a socket of root's in a
directory another account could change, such as the user's own that
.B su
without its dash leaves set.
The daemon makes the directory, when
it is not there yet, with mode 0700, and each missing parent of it with mode
0700 too; run by its unit, the check before each start makes them, so that
another account cannot make them first below a directory others may write in,
such as
.IR /tmp .
.TP
.B SUDO_UID
.TQ
.B PKEXEC_UID
.B SUDO_UID
is set by sudo and run0, and
.B PKEXEC_UID
by pkexec, to the id of the user who ran them.
.B probe
or
.B report
run as root with one of them set to the id of a user other than root and
.B XDG_RUNTIME_DIR
unset, empty, relative or
.I /run/user/0
uses
.I /run/user/<id>
as that directory, so it looks for the daemon of the user who ran it, not for
root's.
.B report
also runs
.B pw\-dump
as that user.
Run as root with neither set to such an id, they cannot ask the user's daemon
(see
.B probe
and
.BR report ).
.TP
.B GEARBENCH_SETTINGS_GRANT
Set by the service's unit to
.IR %E/gearbench ,
the one folder under the home folder its sandbox lets the daemon write.
When the daemon's settings folder is another one, it logs an error at its
start; see
.BR XDG_CONFIG_HOME .
.TP
.B GEARBENCH_ASSUME_SHUTDOWN
Set to
.B 1
to make the daemon treat its own stop as a system shutdown: it turns the
keyboard's lights off and keeps its claim and host mode instead of handing them
back, and the lights of a mouse and a headset that follow it go black. It is for
testing that path without a
reboot. A G915 TKL or PRO X TKL LIGHTSPEED is put back in onboard mode after
the dark all the same.
Otherwise the daemon asks
.B systemctl is\-system\-running
and anything but
.B stopping
counts as not shutting down.
.TP
.B GEARBENCH_ASSUME_BATTERY
Set to a number from 0 to 100, like
.B 29
(any other value, 29% included, is ignored),
and every reading the keyboard's low-battery notices go by is that percentage,
off external power, whether the keyboard is on its cable, through its
receiver or not attached at all. With more than one keyboard, only the one that leads
gets it; the others' notices, and the mice's and the headset's, still go by
their own readings. It is for seeing the notice: two readings a minute
apart that round to below a level of the keyboard's preset, 30 by default,
post it; under
.B off
none does.
The notices themselves, on the receiver and below the levels of the
keyboard's preset (see
.BR battery\-notices ),
need python3\-gi and a notification server; the switch for Gearbench in GNOME
Settings, Notifications, silences them.
.TP
.B XDG_CURRENT_DESKTOP
The desktop the daemon runs under, as the session puts it in the user
manager's environment: where it lists
.B GNOME
or
.BR KDE ,
that desktop warns of a device's battery itself where the kernel gives the
device a power supply, so the daemon posts no urgent notice for that device
and
.B battery\-notices
names the desktop.
Any other desktop, or none, counts as not warning.
.TP
.B DISPLAY
.TQ
.B XAUTHORITY
The X server the daemon reads window focus from, as any X11 program finds it:
a local display such as
.BR :0 ,
reached through its abstract socket and then
.IR /tmp/.X11\-unix/X0 ,
with the MIT\-MAGIC\-COOKIE\-1 from
.B XAUTHORITY
or
.IR ~/.Xauthority ,
read only if it is a regular file of at most 1 MiB.
DISPLAY is read once, at the daemon's start. Without a local display, game
profiles follow window focus through the Gearbench focus extension, or
else the running programs (see
.BR "Game profiles" ).
The daemon's connection keeps Xwayland running where GNOME starts it on
demand.
.TP
.B DBUS_SESSION_BUS_ADDRESS
.TQ
.B DBUS_SYSTEM_BUS_ADDRESS
Where the daemon finds the session and system buses, as any D-Bus client does.
Without the first it uses
.I $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/bus
when XDG_RUNTIME_DIR is an absolute path and that file exists; the
.I /run/user/<uid>
default does not apply here. With neither, the daemon has no session bus, and
does without the screen blank, GNOME's idle monitor, the focus the Gearbench
focus extension reports, desktop notices and the tray icon.
Without the second it uses
.IR /run/dbus/system_bus_socket .
.TP
.B JOURNAL_STREAM
Set by systemd when a service's standard output or standard error is the
journal. When it names
the daemon's standard error,
.B gearbench\-ctl daemon
starts each log line with its syslog priority, as in
.B <4>
for a warning, and leaves out the time, which the journal keeps.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The command succeeded.
.TP
.B 1
The daemon answered with an error, the command refused its arguments before
sending anything, a local command such as
.BR probe ,
.B report
or
.B gui
could not do its job, or
.B headset\-revert
could not put everything back. The error follows
.B error:
on standard error. More may follow on the next line in parentheses: a HID++
error or the A50 base station's error name,
the request that got no answer, what was wrong with an answer, or the system's
error. With
.BR \-\-json ,
the daemon's reply is printed on standard output instead.
.TP
.B 2
The daemon could not be reached, did not answer in time, closed the connection
without answering, or its reply could not be read. Or the command line did not
parse:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
an unknown command or option;
.IP \(bu 2
a word no argument takes;
.IP \(bu 2
a missing argument that has no alternative in the synopsis;
.IP \(bu 2
a preset of
.B battery\-notices
without its device, or a device without its preset;
.IP \(bu 2
two options that exclude each other, given together
.RB ( \-\-slot
and
.B \-\-library
on
.BR headset\-import ,
.B \-\-mute
and
.B \-\-unmute
on
.BR audio\-mix ,
.B \-\-gains
and
.B \-\-flat
on
.BR g432\-headset\-eq ,
any two of
.BR \-\-keyboard ,
.B \-\-mouse
and
.B \-\-headset
on
.BR battery\-notices );
.IP \(bu 2
a value of the wrong type or outside its choices.
.RE
.IP
Where no daemon listens, the error says to start it with
.B "systemctl \-\-user start gearbench"
or
.BR "gearbench\-ctl daemon" ,
or, for a command run as root, to run it as yourself.
.B gearbench\-ctl
waits 30 seconds for the commands under
.BR "Mouse onboard memory" ,
10 for
.BR mouse\-dpi ,
.BR mouse\-host ,
.BR headset\-eq ,
.BR headset\-import ,
.BR headset\-revert ,
.B "headset\-library \-\-write"
and
.BR "headset \-\-read" ,
and 3 for the rest. A timeout can mean the daemon is busy with a slow write.
A mouse's onboard profile takes about a second when the mouse answers, and a
headset EQ change or revert is up to tens of requests to the base station
one after another. Either takes longer when a device stops answering part
way.
.TP
.B 130
Interrupted. A running
.B daemon
takes an interrupt as a stop, as it does SIGTERM and, unless it was started
under
.BR nohup (1),
a hangup: it shuts down and exits 0.
.TP
.B 141
The program reading the output closed it before reading all of it, as
.BR head (1)
does once it has its lines; the rest of the output is dropped. A shell
gives the same status for a program that SIGPIPE ended.
.SH RECOVERY
The last paragraph of
.B DESCRIPTION
lists the writes a device keeps through a power-off. Of those, this section
covers a mouse's onboard profile 1, written by the commands under
.BR "Mouse onboard memory" ,
the flash of the A50 Gen 4's base station, written by
.BR headset\-save ,
the A50 X's settings and the G432 adapter's sidetone level.
Gearbench never writes the keyboard's onboard profiles, and it writes no
firmware.
.SS The mouse's backups
Every onboard write first backs up the profile directory, sector 0x0000, and
every profile sector to
.IR ~/.config/gearbench/backup/ ,
a file for each, named
.IB model \-sector\- sector \- time .bin\fR,
where
.I model
is
.BR g502x ,
.B g305
or
.BR g502hero ,
.I sector
is four hex digits,
.B 0001
for profile 1, and
.I time
is the second that file was saved, just before the write, in UTC, as in
.BR 20260929T181500Z .
A sector that fails its CRC is saved with
.I .bad\-read
in place of
.IR .bin .
When a file has that name, or that name with
.I .unchanged
added, as after a second backup of the sector in the same second,
.BR \-1 ,
.B \-2
and so on go before the suffix.
Each file is on disk before the flash write, so a
.I .bin
is whole or absent: one that cannot be put on disk is removed, and the write
is refused. Nothing else deletes them; delete by hand if the space
matters, keeping the oldest, which hold the mouse as it was before
Gearbench's first write.
Gearbench never writes the directory, whose backups record which profiles
were enabled.
.PP
.B mouse\-restore
writes back the newest
.I .bin
of sector 0x0001 for the model of the mouse it writes, by modification time, and
its
.B restored from:
line names the file. Of files with the same modification time, as a copy of
the directory can leave them, it takes the one with the later
.I time
in its name, then the one with the higher number before the suffix.
It backs up the sector it replaces first, so after a
restore over a good profile 1, a second run puts that back. The newest is
the model's, whichever unit wrote it: with two G502 X PLUS it can be the
other one's, so after a write on one, move its backups out of the directory
before a restore on the other. To restore an older backup, set the newer
ones aside: move them out of the directory, or add
.I .unchanged
to their names, and no restore picks them.
.B ls \-t
lists the backups newest first by modification time. Of files with the same
modification time its order is not the one
.B mouse\-restore
uses, so after a copy that left those times equal, set aside the one with
the latest
.I time
and highest number, not the first one listed. Where the modification times
differ:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
cd ~/.config/gearbench/backup
# on the G305: g305 for g502x and G305 for "G502 X PLUS"
# on the G502 HERO: g502hero for g502x and "G502 HERO" for "G502 X PLUS"
ls \-t g502x\-sector\-0001\-*.bin
newest=$(ls \-t g502x\-sector\-0001\-*.bin | head \-1)
mv "$newest" "$newest.unchanged"
gearbench\-ctl mouse\-restore \-\-mouse "G502 X PLUS"
.EE
.RE
.SS A write that failed
A write that fails part way can still have changed profile 1, so the daemon
reads it again. When that read finds what was written, as after a commit
whose answer the mouse did not send, the write counts as done: the command
answers as for any write that went through, and the journal says
.BR "the \%write of sector 0x0001 failed (" ... "), but it reads back as written" .
When the read-back after the write's commit differs from what was
written and the read after it differs too, or the read after a failure
fails, the error says what it found:
.TP
.B it still holds a valid profile
Not the one written. Nothing needs restoring; run the command again.
.TP
.B it now fails its CRC
Run
.B mouse\-restore
before switching the mouse off: what the mouse does at power-on with such a
sector is not known. See
.B Profile 1 fails its CRC
below.
.TP
.B could not be read back
The read after the write failed too, so what profile 1 holds is not known,
and
.B gearbench\-ctl mouse
says
.B not known
on its
.B "onboard profile 1:"
line, and the window's Onboard profile 1 group says
.B Profile 1 not known
on its Stages and rate row. Leave the mouse switched on. Once it answers,
.B gearbench\-ctl mouse
reads profile 1 again. With
.B CRC ok
on that line, the stages and buttons shown are what profile 1 holds: run the
write again if they are not the ones you wanted. With
.BR "CRC fails" ,
see below.
When that read found the mouse's connection gone (unplugged, or the USB link
reset), the daemon drops the mouse instead, and
.B gearbench\-ctl mouse
lists it again, with profile 1 as read then, once it is attached again.
When the read-back after the commit differs and the read after it fails with
the mouse still attached, the error gives both: first the words above for what that read-back found, then
these, which are the ones to act on.
.PP
After any other failed write,
.B gearbench\-ctl mouse
shows what profile 1 holds now, with
.B CRC ok
or
.B CRC fails
on its
.B "onboard profile 1:"
line.
A write, failed or done, that leaves a profile 1 with a good CRC as it was,
as a second
.B mouse\-enable\-buttons
does, renames its backup of profile 1, adding
.IR .unchanged ,
and the journal says
.BR "the \%write left sector 0x0001 as it was" ,
so
.B mouse\-restore
still undoes the last write that changed profile 1.
After a write whose read failed
.RB ( "could not be read back" ),
that happens once profile 1 is read again and found as it was: by
.BR "gearbench\-ctl mouse" ,
the next write, or
.BR mouse\-restore ,
which reads it before choosing a backup.
When the daemon restarts, or drops the mouse, before that read, the
write's backup stays the newest
.IR .bin :
if
.B gearbench\-ctl mouse
then shows profile 1 as it was before that write, set that backup aside by
hand, as
.B The mouse's backups
above says, before running
.BR mouse\-restore .
.SS Profile 1 fails its CRC
The four commands that edit profile 1,
.BR mouse\-rate ,
.BR mouse\-dpi\-stages ,
.B mouse\-enable\-buttons
and
.BR mouse\-fallback\-buttons ,
refuse to write over a sector that fails its CRC:
.BR "onboard profile 1 fails its CRC" .
The window's Onboard profile 1 group says
.B Profile 1 fails its CRC
on its Stages and rate row.
.B mouse\-restore
writes the newest good backup over it; run it before switching the mouse
off. Its backup of the bad sector is kept as
.IR .bad\-read ,
which no restore picks, so a second run writes the same backup again.
.PP
On a model whose onboard profile Gearbench does not write yet, such as the
G502 X LIGHTSPEED,
.B mouse\-restore
is refused too. Its profile 1 may fail its CRC by being blank, on a mouse
no other program has written to; nothing needs doing then, as the Stages
and rate row says.
.SS A restore that failed
These refusals write nothing:
.TP
.BI "the " model " has no backup of profile 1 to restore in " folder
The model has no
.I .bin
of profile 1, or each is a link to a file moved or deleted; see
.B No good backup
below.
.TP
.IB folder " could not be listed (" reason )
.TQ
.IB folder ": a link to " target ", which is not there"
.TQ
.IB folder ": " link " is a link to " target ", which is not there"
The backup folder,
.IR ~/.config/gearbench/backup/ ,
cannot be read or is not a folder, or it or a folder above it is a link to a
folder that is not there, such as a dotfiles folder not mounted. No backup
in it was read: fix the folder or mount it, and run
.B mouse\-restore
again.
.TP
.IB path ": not a regular file"
.TQ
.IB path ": larger than " size
.TQ
.IB path " is " n " bytes, the sector is " size
.TQ
.IB path " fails its CRC; not writing it"
.TQ
.BI "could not read " path ": " reason
Set that file aside, as
.B The mouse's backups
above says, and run
.B mouse\-restore
again: it takes the next newest. A link to a good backup restores.
.PP
A
.I .bin
that is a link to a file moved or deleted is skipped with
.BI "\%skipping backup " path
in the journal, and the newest of the rest restores.
.PP
A restore that backs up a good profile 1, then fails and leaves it as it
was, renames that backup, adding
.IR .unchanged ,
and the journal says
.BR "the \%restore left sector 0x0001 as it was" .
Run
.B mouse\-restore
again: it writes the backup the first run meant. Nothing is deleted. If the
journal warns
.BI "could not set " path " aside"
instead, set that file aside by hand before the second run.
.PP
A restore whose profile 1 reads back as the backup counts as done, even when
the mouse did not answer the commit.
In host mode, one that answers
.BI "the " model " went away after the profile 1 write"
has written the backup too.
A second run then does what it
does after any restore: when profile 1 passed its CRC before the restore, it
puts back profile 1 as it was; over one that fails it, it writes the same backup again.
A restore that fails after changing profile 1 to something else leaves the
backup of profile 1 it just made in place. So does one that could not read it
back, until profile 1 is read again: found as it was, a backup that is a
.I .bin
is renamed then, adding
.IR .unchanged ,
and the journal says
.BR "the \%restore left sector 0x0001 as it was" .
Once the mouse answers:
.IP 1. 4
Run
.BR "gearbench\-ctl mouse" .
With
.B CRC ok
and the stages and buttons of the backup you meant, the restore is done.
.IP 2. 4
Otherwise check which
.I .bin
is newest, since that is the one
.B mouse\-restore
writes. When profile 1 passed its CRC before the restore and step 1 renamed
nothing, the failed run's backup is now the newest
.IR .bin ;
over one that fails it, that backup is a
.IR .bad\-read ,
and the newest
.I .bin
is still the backup the failed run meant.
When the newest is the backup the failed run made, set it aside, or a second
run puts back the state from before the first.
.IP 3. 4
Run
.B mouse\-restore
again.
.SS No good backup
With none of the model's backups of profile 1 left, or none a restore takes,
the edits still work while profile 1's CRC is good. The mouse keeps a
read-only factory copy of profile 1 in ROM, whose stages, default and shift
go back with
.B "mouse\-dpi\-stages \-\-factory"
while no game profile is loaded,
and on the G502 X PLUS and the G502 HERO
.B mouse\-fallback\-buttons
gives G4 to G8 their factory entries and G9 nothing, where from the factory
it cycles the onboard profiles.
.PP
With profile 1 failing its CRC as well,
.B gearbench\-ctl
can write nothing to it, and no command writes the ROM copy or the directory
back. Two scripts below write a sector back whole with the daemon stopped,
through the installed package;
from a checkout, run them in the checkout with
.B python3 \-
in place of
.BR "python3 \-I \-" .
Each backs up the sector it replaces first, and
.B write_sector
reads the sector back and raises if it differs.
Leave the mouse switched on until one has run.
.PP
The newest good backup of a sector, by hand: the directory, or profile 1 when
.B mouse\-restore
cannot run. For profile 1 it takes the file
.B mouse\-restore
would, reads it the same way and makes the same size and CRC checks, so where
a restore would take no backup, it stops before writing anything.
When it stops on a file it names, set that file aside, as
.B The mouse's backups
above says, and run it again: it takes the next newest.
Set
.B MODEL
to the name
.B gearbench\-ctl mouse
prints, and
.B SECTOR
to
.B PROFILE_SECTOR
(profile 1) or
.B DIRECTORY_SECTOR
(sector 0x0000, the list of profiles). Put a good directory back before
switching the mouse off: what a directory whose CRC fails does at power-on
is not known, and one that disables profile 1 puts a G305, whose only
writable profile it is, on its ROM copy. A run that backs up a good sector,
then fails to write and leaves it as it was, renames that backup, adding
.IR \%.unchanged ,
as
.B mouse\-restore
does, so a second run takes the same file. A write that raised can still have
taken, as when the answer to the commit is lost. When the sector then reads
back as the image written, the script says so and exits with status 0: the
write took, as
.B mouse\-restore
counts it. When it reads back as neither that image nor its
.I .bin
backup, or cannot be read back, nothing is renamed. Over a good sector, that
backup is now the newest
.IR .bin :
set it aside before a second run, or that run writes the state from before
this one back. Over a sector whose CRC failed, the backup is a
.IR .bad\-read ,
which no run takes, and the newest
.I .bin
is still the backup this run meant, so a second run takes it again.
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
systemctl \-\-user stop gearbench.service
python3 \-I \- <<'PY'
from gearbench.hidpp import Hidpp
from gearbench.mouse import BACKUP_NAMES, CABLED_IDS, MODELS, PROFILE_SECTOR, find_attached_mice
from gearbench.onboard import DIRECTORY_SECTOR, OnboardProfiles, sector_crc_ok
from gearbench.settings_files import ConfigError, read_capped
from gearbench.wording import escaped

MODEL = "G502 X PLUS"       # or "G305" or "G502 HERO"
SECTOR = PROFILE_SECTOR     # or DIRECTORY_SECTOR
# On its cable the G502 X PLUS stops answering on its receiver, whose node stays.
found = sorted((m for m in find_attached_mice() if MODELS.get(m[1]) == MODEL),
               key=lambda m: m[1] not in CABLED_IDS)
if not found:
    raise SystemExit(f"no {MODEL} attached; stop here")
node, product, index = found[0]
dev = Hidpp(node, device_index=index)
onboard = OnboardProfiles(dev)
path = OnboardProfiles.newest_backup(BACKUP_NAMES[product], SECTOR)
if path is None:
    raise SystemExit("no backup of this sector for this model; stop here")
# Anyone who can put a file in the folder chooses its name, and it can be a pipe or
# any size: read as mouse\-restore reads it.
shown = escaped(path)
try:
    image = read_capped(path, onboard.info.sector_size)
except ConfigError as exc:
    raise SystemExit(f"{escaped(exc)}; stop here") from None
if len(image) != onboard.info.sector_size:
    raise SystemExit(f"{shown} is the wrong size; stop here")
if not sector_crc_ok(image):
    raise SystemExit(f"{shown} fails its CRC; stop here")
# Every directory seen lists profile 1 first, and it must be enabled.
if SECTOR == DIRECTORY_SECTOR and image[0:3] != bytes((0, 1, 1)):
    raise SystemExit(f"{shown} does not enable profile 1; stop here")
made = onboard.backup(BACKUP_NAMES[product], SECTOR, onboard.read_sector(SECTOR))
try:
    onboard.write_sector(SECTOR, image)
except Exception:
    now = onboard.read_sector(SECTOR)
    if now != image:
        if made.suffix == ".bin" and now == made.read_bytes():
            OnboardProfiles.set_aside(made)
        raise
    print(f"wrote {escaped(path.name)} to sector 0x{SECTOR:04x}; the write failed, but the "
          "sector reads back as written")
else:
    print(f"wrote {escaped(path.name)} to sector 0x{SECTOR:04x}; read back byte for byte")
dev.close()
PY
systemctl \-\-user start gearbench.service
.EE
.RE
.PP
The factory copy in ROM, the last resort. Sector 0x0101 is the read-only copy
of profile 1. Its last two bytes are ff ff where the CRC goes, so
.B write_sector
refuses it as read;
.B OnboardProfiles.reseal_rom
computes the CRC and changes nothing else.
.B write_sector
refuses sectors from 0x0100 up, so the resealed image goes into profile 1:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
systemctl \-\-user stop gearbench.service
python3 \-I \- <<'PY'
from gearbench.hidpp import Hidpp
from gearbench.mouse import BACKUP_NAMES, CABLED_IDS, MODELS, PROFILE_SECTOR, find_attached_mice
from gearbench.onboard import OnboardProfiles

MODEL = "G502 X PLUS"       # or "G305" or "G502 HERO"
# On its cable the G502 X PLUS stops answering on its receiver, whose node stays.
found = sorted((m for m in find_attached_mice() if MODELS.get(m[1]) == MODEL),
               key=lambda m: m[1] not in CABLED_IDS)
if not found:
    raise SystemExit(f"no {MODEL} attached; stop here")
node, product, index = found[0]
dev = Hidpp(node, device_index=index)
onboard = OnboardProfiles(dev)
# Twice, and they must agree: the ROM copy has no CRC to catch a chunk delivered late.
rom = onboard.read_sector(0x0101)
if rom != onboard.read_sector(0x0101):
    raise SystemExit("two reads of the ROM disagree; stop here")
sealed = OnboardProfiles.reseal_rom(rom)
onboard.backup(BACKUP_NAMES[product], PROFILE_SECTOR, onboard.read_sector(PROFILE_SECTOR))
onboard.write_sector(PROFILE_SECTOR, sealed)
print("restored profile 1 from the ROM copy; read back byte for byte")
dev.close()
PY
systemctl \-\-user start gearbench.service
.EE
.RE
.SS The keyboard
A daemon killed before it could stop can leave the G, M and MR keys diverted
and doing nothing, the lighting held where it left it, and the keyboard in
host mode. A clean stop, with
.B systemctl \-\-user stop \%gearbench.service
or SIGTERM, an interrupt or a hangup to
.BR "gearbench\-ctl daemon" ,
turns the M and MR LEDs off, gives the keys back, releases the lighting claim
and puts the keyboard in onboard mode; a daemon whose loop fails does the
same on its way out. At a shutdown or reboot it instead turns off the
keyboard's lights and those of a mouse and a headset that follow it, and keeps
the claim and host mode, so a keyboard dark after a power-off is as intended. A G915
TKL or PRO X TKL LIGHTSPEED is put back in onboard mode after the dark.
A keyboard the daemon has
found not answering, such as one switched off behind its receiver, is sent
nothing at either stop. A
.B kill \-9
or an out-of-memory kill skips the stop. Run as the unit, the daemon is
started again 2 seconds later and sets the keyboard up again, up to five
starts a minute. Past that limit, or for a daemon run with
.BR "gearbench\-ctl daemon" ,
start the daemon, wait until
.B status
says G keys diverted, then stop it cleanly: a stop that comes before the
daemon has begun setting the keyboard up hands nothing back. A unit past its
limit refuses a start until the minute is over.
.B systemctl \-\-user \%reset\-failed \%gearbench.service
zeroes the unit's start and restart counters and clears a failed state, so it
can start at once. On a unit within its limit the restart that follows runs
the same either way, so the commands below start with it.
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
systemctl \-\-user reset\-failed gearbench.service
systemctl \-\-user restart gearbench.service
gearbench\-ctl status    # repeat until the claim line ends in "G keys diverted"
systemctl \-\-user stop gearbench.service
.EE
.RE
.PP
On a G915 TKL or PRO X TKL LIGHTSPEED the claim line ends in
.B "F1 to F12 diverted, typed by Gearbench while unbound"
instead. A daemon killed before it could stop can leave either in host
mode, where its F1 to F12 type nothing until the daemon is running again or
the keyboard is switched off and on.
On a keyboard whose keys the daemon does not divert, the G213 Prodigy or one
with custom assignments such as a G515 TKL or a G915 X LIGHTSPEED, the claim
line says
.B "not claimed"
until the daemon has set the keyboard up, and then begins with
.BR claimed :
repeat until it does.
A G915 X LIGHTSPEED's G1 to G9 are all set to report to the daemon while it
has a virtual keyboard and the session is active. A daemon killed then,
before its stop, leaves them sending nothing, F13 to F21 included, until the
daemon starts again or the keyboard loses power: its cable out and the
keyboard switched off.
.PP
Where the daemon cannot reach the keyboard, power cycle it: unplug the cable
of one with no battery and plug it back in, or switch one with a battery off
and on with its cable out. After a power cycle it has lost the diversion, the
claim and host mode.
.SS The headset
Gearbench writes the flash of the A50 Gen 4's base station only at
.B headset\-save
or the Save on the window's Headset tab, and never its firmware or its id.
What can go wrong is in its memory: a change not wanted, or a base station
that stops answering.
.B \%headset\-revert
puts back every setting and equalizer preset that differs from the flash, except
.BR auto\-shut\-off ,
which preset is active and a saved value Gearbench cannot write back exactly (the note names it). Unplugging the base station's USB cable
and plugging it back in starts it from its flash, dropping everything not
saved. That is also what
.B status
says to do for a base station that is not answering or could not be set up.
The A50 X keeps each setting written to it through a power cycle, so an
unplug undoes none of them;
.B "audio\-set put\-back"
writes back the value the daemon first read of each setting since it started.
The daemon's wait for a base station that did not answer when found starts
at 5 seconds and doubles up to a minute. Unplugging it for more than about a
second ends that wait, and one plugged back in is looked at within about 5
seconds. An A50 Gen 4's base station that stops answering after it was set
up is asked again every 30 seconds.
.PP
The G432's adapter kept its sidetone level through an unplug when tested on
2026-10-17.
.B "g432\-sidetone put\-back"
writes back the level the daemon first read since it started, or 0 where no
read found one before the first write.
.SS Firmware
.B gearbench\-ctl
updates no device's firmware. It uses no HID++ firmware update feature, and
the base station's bootloader and the headset's updater are among the
commands it refuses to send.
.SH EXAMPLES
See what the daemon and each device are doing; anything wrong is listed
under problems, a line each:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
gearbench\-ctl status
.EE
.RE
.PP
Report a mouse Gearbench does not drive yet, with the daemon stopped for the
devices it holds. No rule Gearbench installs grants that mouse's node: where
the report prints a
.B setfacl
command for it, run that command, then the report again. On a node that also
carries key reports, any program of yours can read what is typed on it until
you unplug the device, which ends the grant. Start the daemon again once the
report is done:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
systemctl \-\-user stop gearbench
gearbench\-ctl report
# run the sudo setfacl command it printed, then:
gearbench\-ctl report
systemctl \-\-user start gearbench
.EE
.RE
.PP
Make a keyboard already on the static effect a dim orange:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
gearbench\-ctl brightness 25
gearbench\-ctl color \(aq#ff2200\(aq
.EE
.RE
.PP
Bind G1 to a macro, and to F15 on the G-Shift layer:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
gearbench\-ctl bind G1 \(aqmacro:Reload and heal\(aq
gearbench\-ctl bind G1 f15 \-\-g\-shift
.EE
.RE
.PP
Hold Shift for as long as G2 is held, for a crouch:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
gearbench\-ctl bind G2 shift
.EE
.RE
.PP
With
.B mouse\-host
off, rewrite the G502 X PLUS's extra buttons so the daemon reads them, then
bind the paddle to F15:
.PP
