.EX
gearbench\-ctl idle \-\-dim\-after 120 \-\-off\-after 600
.EE
.RE
.PP
Load a Command Center preset into the A50 Gen 4's slot 2, switch to that slot,
and save every setting and equalizer preset to the base station's flash:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
gearbench\-ctl headset\-import "A50 MOD KIT.astroeq" \-\-slot 2
gearbench\-ctl headset\-set preset 2
gearbench\-ctl headset\-save
.EE
.RE
.PP
Play a Steam game in 7.1 on the headset through Gearbench Spatial: switch it
on before the game starts, give the game the launch option below in Steam,
and set the game's own audio output to surround or System Default. A game
started while it is off plays in stereo until the game is started again:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
gearbench\-ctl headset\-spatial on
# the game's launch option in Steam:
PULSE_SINK=gearbench\-spatial %command%
.EE
.RE
.SH NOTES
The Game Mode button is the keyboard's own Game Mode, and
.B gearbench\-ctl
does not touch it. Game Mode turns off the Super key: if the Super key does
nothing, Game Mode is on; press the Game Mode button again.
.PP
With the daemon stopped a keyboard runs its own lighting and, where it has them,
its onboard profiles and the power saving that turns it off a few minutes after
the last key. A G915's
G1 to G5 then type F1 to F5 on M1, 1 to 5 on M2 and 6 to 0 on M3, and a G915
TKL's or PRO X TKL LIGHTSPEED's F1 to F12 type F1 to F12. A daemon killed
before it could stop can leave the G keys diverted and typing nothing, and
the keyboard in host mode;
.B RECOVERY
says how to hand them back.
.PP
Pair nothing else to a mouse's receiver. A keyboard paired to one could
have its keystrokes arrive on that mouse's nodes, which the udev rules grant:
the G502 X PLUS's hidraw and event nodes, and the hidraw node of the G305,
the G502 X LIGHTSPEED, the PRO X SUPERLIGHT, the G502 X PLUS MILLENNIUM
FALCON, a PRO X SUPERLIGHT 2 model or the PRO X2 SUPERSTRIKE. In host mode, when a key arrives on the G502 X PLUS's event node, the daemon warns
in the journal once each time the mouse attaches, and
.BR status ,
the tray and the window's Mouse tab show the warning until the mouse
detaches.
.PP
Pair nothing else to a keyboard's receiver either. Going by the
kernel's hid\-logitech\-dj.c, a keyboard or mouse paired there after it
could have the keystrokes it sends arrive on the node of the one paired
first, which the udev
rules grant for the G515 LIGHTSPEED TKL, the PRO X TKL LIGHTSPEED, the
G915 X LIGHTSPEED, the G915 X LIGHTSPEED TKL and, on a kernel that gives it
one, the G915 TKL.
.PP
Six of the daemon's commands have no subcommand:
.B paint
and
.BR rename ,
which the window's Keyboard tab sends;
.B save_color
and
.BR forget_color ,
which each color picker's Saved colors row sends;
.BR macro_delete ,
which the Macros tab sends; and
.BR quit .
All six are reachable over the control socket from a script: a request is one
JSON object that names its command in
.BR command .
.B quit
takes nothing more;
.BR rename ,
.BR save_color ,
.B forget_color
and
.B macro_delete
take
.BR value ;
.B paint
takes
.BR base ,
a color as #rrggbb, and
.BR zones ,
an object that gives each key's HID++ 0x8081 zone id, as decimal text, its
color.
.BR profile\-programs ,
.B profile\-mouse\-color
and
.B rename\-profile
send
.BR profile_processes ,
.B profile_mouse_color
and
.BR rename_profile ,
as the window's Game profiles dialog does.
.B import\-ghub
sends
.BR macro_save ,
as the Macros tab does, and
.B import_profile
and
.BR import_m_profile ,
as the window's Import from G HUB does.
.B import\-pack
sends
.BR macro_save ,
.B import_profile_lighting
and
.BR profile_mouse_color ,
as the window's Import pack does. The window's Mouse and Headset tabs send
.B mouse
and
.B headset
with
.B ask
false at times such as while another tab shows or the window is not in
front; the daemon then answers
from what it last read and sends the device nothing, though a
.B mouse
request about a mouse not answering, or with none attached and no
.BR device ,
still brings the daemon's next attempt forward to within 30 seconds (see
.BR mouse ).
.B report
sends
.B mouse
with
.B ask
false and
.BR device ,
a mouse's product id in hex, for each attached mouse beside the selected
one: the daemon answers for the attached mouse with that id, else for the
first attached mouse of the model the id names, and leaves the selection as
it is; it refuses, in words, an id that names no attached mouse's model.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR hwdb (7),
.BR inotify (7),
.BR journalctl (1),
.BR pw\-cli (1),
.BR pw\-dump (1),
.BR pw\-metadata (1),
.BR systemctl (1),
.BR systemd\-run (1),
.BR systemd.service (5),
.BR udev (7),
.BR udevadm (8)
.PP
The package installs the project's own documentation in
.IR /usr/share/doc/gearbench/ .
.I README.md
gives the devices Gearbench drives, the two ways to install it and how to
report a device it does not drive yet.
.I GUIDE.md
gives the install in full and how to use it; its section The models and
their ids gives each model's ids, what it was built from and what is not
tested on it yet.
.I SECURITY.md
says what counts as a security problem and how to report one.
In the source tree the README and
.I SECURITY.md
are at the top and the guide in
.IR docs/ .
The project is at
.UR https://github.com/gearbench/gearbench
.UE .
.SH BUGS
Report them at
.UR https://github.com/gearbench/gearbench/issues
.UE ,
with the text of the Debugging Information page, which writes the home folder
as ~ (in the window's main menu, About Gearbench, then Troubleshooting); or
with the output of
.B gearbench\-ctl status
and
.BR "journalctl \-\-user \-u gearbench \-\-no\-hostname" ,
after you replace the home folder's full path in it with ~.
For a device Gearbench does not drive yet, or one that misbehaves, run
.B report
and paste its block into the Device report form (see
.BR report ).
Report a security problem privately instead, on the repository's Security
tab: Report a vulnerability.
